window.CURRICULUM = {"built":"2026-10-02T13:07:54.885Z","courses":[{"id":"math","title":"Math","bank":"math","guide":{"title":"Suggested order","audience":"If you are scoring around 1200 to 1300","lead":"Start with 1.1. The approach and strategy in it apply to every question on the test, so the rest of Math opens once you have watched it. After that you can work straight through and nothing here is wasted, or take it in the order below, which pays off fastest.","steps":[{"lessons":["m1.1"],"text":"Read this first if you have not. It is short, and it covers how the section is built and what the calculator can do for you."},{"lessons":["m2.2","m3.1"],"text":"Start the calculator lessons with these two. 7 lessons in the course are built around the calculator, but these are the two that make all the others fast. At this level most of what costs you points is time and execution rather than not knowing the topic."},{"lessons":["m3.2","m3.3","m3.4","m3.5","m3.6","m3.7","m3.8","m3.9","m3.10"],"text":"Advanced Math next, in this order. It is about a third of the test and it holds the most hard questions in the course. If you are short on time, 3.3, 3.4, 3.5 and 3.7 are the heaviest of them."},{"lessons":["m2.1","m2.3","m2.4","m2.5","m2.6","m2.7","m2.8","m2.9"],"text":"Then the rest of Algebra, which is the other third of the test."},{"lessons":["m5.1","m5.2","m5.3","m5.4","m5.5","m5.6","m5.7"],"text":"Problem Solving and Data Analysis can run alongside the two above. A lot of it will already feel familiar at this level."},{"lessons":["m4.1","m4.2","m4.3","m4.4","m4.5","m4.6","m4.7","m4.8","m4.9"],"text":"Geometry after that. Some of these are the hardest lessons in the course, but geometry is only about 15 percent of the test, so it comes after the two big domains."},{"lessons":["m2.10"],"text":"Leave this one for last. Every question in it is hard on purpose, and it is far more useful once the rest is solid."}]},"domains":[{"name":"Introduction","lessons":[{"id":"m1.1","num":"1.1","title":"SAT Math Section: Logistics, and General Approach","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What does the Math Section look like?","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Two modules, each 22 questions at 35 minutes (~1.5 minutes per question)"},{"sub":false,"html":"The second module is adaptive, meaning: you will either be given an “easy” or “hard” second module based on your performance in the first. A more difficult second module means that you did well in the first module."},{"sub":false,"html":"For both modules, the questions start easy and get more difficult as you progress."}]},{"title":"Key Math Topics Covered","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Algebra</strong> ~ 35% of math questions"},{"sub":true,"html":"Linear equations and inequalities"},{"sub":true,"html":"Interpreting and defining linear models"},{"sub":true,"html":"Interpreting graphs of linear functions"},{"sub":true,"html":"Systems of equations and inequalities"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Advanced Math</strong> ~ 35% of math questions"},{"sub":true,"html":"Interpreting, creating, and analyzing quadratic, exponential, rational and radical functions"},{"sub":true,"html":"Solving systems of nonlinear functions"},{"sub":true,"html":"Determining the number of solutions to non-linear equations and systems"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Geometry &amp; Trigonometry</strong> ~ 15% of math questions"},{"sub":true,"html":"Properties of angles, triangles, circles, and polygons"},{"sub":true,"html":"Calculations involving area, perimeter, surface area, and volume"},{"sub":true,"html":"Basic right-triangle trigonometry: sine, cosine, tangent"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Problem Solving &amp; Data Analysis</strong> ~ 15% of math questions"},{"sub":true,"html":"Interpreting, creating, and analyzing linear and non-linear scatterplots (linear, quadratic, exponential)"},{"sub":true,"html":"Ratios, proportions, percentages, and complex word problems"},{"sub":true,"html":"Probability, statistics, and data interpretation (graphs, charts, and tables)"}]},{"title":"How we Use Desmos","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Use Desmos whenever you can intelligently.</strong> It removes arithmetic mistakes, does the algebra for you, and solves the problems that decide a 1500+ score."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Predict before you solve.</strong> Your foundational understanding determines how useful Desmos will be. Can’t predict the approach? Skip it and come back."},{"sub":false,"html":"The only question that matters: <strong>what is the most efficient path to the correct answer?</strong>"}]},{"title":"Quick Calculator Shortcuts & Elements","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<code>Shift + 8</code> – Multiplication"},{"sub":false,"html":"<code>/</code> – Fractions"},{"sub":false,"html":"<code>Shift + 6</code> – exponent"},{"sub":false,"html":"<code>table</code> – T-Chart Table"},{"sub":false,"html":"<code>Cmd + A</code> + grouping command (parentheses, division, etc.) – Group expressions"},{"sub":false,"html":"<code>sqrt</code> – Square root"},{"sub":false,"html":"<code>nthroot</code> – nth degree root"},{"sub":false,"html":"<code>pi</code> – π"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Edit List</strong> – Duplicate, reorder, table, delete-all"}]},{"title":"Two rules before we start","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>If we see a function: GRAPH IT.</strong> More often than not, it’s naturally going to lend itself to the answer."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Think within the confines of what you know.</strong> The SAT isn’t testing anything beyond what you already know. See a quadratic but don’t know what to do? Apply what you already know about them: vertex, solutions, quadratic formula, etc. You’ll naturally happen on the answer by doing what you’ve trained to do on the questions."}]},{"title":"Why I have Designed this Curriculum","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"The Desmos calculator is available throughout the entire Math section. But properly using it is only effective when we actually understand underlying concepts – something that many of these other tutors and videos don’t emphasize all that much."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>What we don’t want happening:</strong> wasting time figuring out how to use Desmos during the test. A combination of a solid foundation in the topics paired with comfort in navigating the calculator will earn higher scores."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Expectations:</strong> competence in basic pre-algebra/algebra topics."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Advanced Students:</strong> skip around the curriculum to dedicated practical calculator strategies."}]}],"qids":[],"title":"Why I have Designed this Curriculum"}]}]},{"name":"Algebra","lessons":[{"id":"m2.1","num":"2.1","title":"Linear Equations","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"Definition","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A line tells us the relationship between two variables: <strong>x</strong>, the input/independent variable, and <strong>y</strong>, the output/dependent variable. These definitions will be more important when we talk about word problems."},{"sub":false,"html":"We can tell when we’re dealing with a line because the x-variable won’t be squared, cubed, or under a root (square roots) – just plain x and y. We say it is a <strong>first-degree polynomial function</strong>, meaning the highest exponent on the x-variable is always one."}]},{"title":"What do we need to create a line?","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"To create one you need <strong>two points</strong>, or <strong>one point and the slope</strong>."},{"sub":false,"html":"Any linear problems on the SAT will ask for a version of or necessitate one of the following:"},{"sub":false,"html":"– the y- or x-intercept"},{"sub":false,"html":"– the slope"},{"sub":false,"html":"– a point or coordinate (e.g., (1,-2) or f(3)=4)"},{"sub":false,"html":"– the equation of the line"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means, for each problem, we are actively looking for or solving for a slope or points, or finding an equation. In either case, we want to use three specific formulas to identify and create linear equations, and find relevant information."}]},{"title":"Slope-Intercept Form","lead":"The most common way to write a linear function is:","formula":"f(x) = mx + b","listhead":"The components of this formula are defined as:","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>f(x)</strong> (or y): The dependent variable or the output value of the function."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>x</strong>: The independent variable or the input value."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>m</strong>: The slope of the line, which indicates its steepness and direction — the change in y over the change in x: <strong>m = Δy/Δx = (y<sub>2</sub> − y<sub>1</sub>) / (x<sub>2</sub> − x<sub>1</sub>)</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>b</strong>: The y-intercept, which represents the point where the line crosses the vertical y-axis as <strong>(0, b)</strong> – the output value when x = 0."}]}],"qids":["aad7e1b9","1fe778dc","3174f07d","8a1544f1","ca452900","9ed4c1a2"],"title":"Slope-Intercept Form","checkQids":["ed856e9c","a91a2b75","7ff68d45","10c448d6","343aaff6","10bdb26d","d11910d6","8eb0e19e","b2845d88","e0f59119"]},{"notes":[{"title":"Standard Form","lead":"You can write the standard form of a linear equation as:","formula":"Ax + By = C","listhead":"The components of this formula are defined as:","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>A, B, and C</strong> are usually integers in class — on the SAT they can be anything, and the slope rule still works."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Slope (m) = −A / B</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"These will appear more often in <strong>linear word problems</strong> than they do as we see here."}]}],"qids":["db0107df","51568fb9","00b9bd37","cb58833c"],"title":"Standard Form","checkQids":["94b48cbf","9f70fd47","534a4fca","1137b9eb","75c15b42","3cdbf026","0b46bad5","2e0290c3","e671805d"]},{"notes":[{"title":"Point-Slope Form","lead":"You can write the point-slope form of a linear equation as:","formula":"f(x) = m(x − x<sub>1</sub>) + y<sub>1</sub>","listhead":"The components of this formula are defined as:","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>f(x)</strong> (or y): The dependent variable or the output value of the function."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>x</strong>: The independent input variable."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>m</strong>: The slope of the line, which indicates its steepness and direction — the change in y over the change in x: <strong>m = Δy/Δx = (y<sub>2</sub> − y<sub>1</sub>) / (x<sub>2</sub> − x<sub>1</sub>)</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>(x<sub>1</sub>, y<sub>1</sub>)</strong>: The coordinates of your known point."},{"sub":false,"html":"Use this when the point you know is <strong>not</strong> the y-intercept. It's easier to think about this form as a modified version of <strong>slope-intercept form</strong>."}]}],"qids":["946ab892","9f3cb472"],"title":"Point-Slope Form","checkQids":["9aaf7786","1a182bed"]}]},{"id":"m2.2","num":"2.2","title":"Tables","calc":true,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“For the linear function f, the table shows three values of x and their corresponding values of f(x). Which equation defines f(x)?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“y = 70x + 8. Which table gives three values of x and their corresponding values of y for the given equation?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"Any question that gives us two points"}]},{"title":"What is a table actually giving us?","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A table is just a <strong>list of points</strong>, and two points make a line (2.1). So every linear table is a line waiting to be written."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"a <strong>missing value</strong> in the table"},{"sub":true,"html":"the <strong>equation</strong> the table follows"},{"sub":true,"html":"the <strong>slope</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"the value at an <strong>x that is not in the table</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"<strong>which table</strong> matches a given equation"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: take two clean rows, find the slope, and the question solves itself. In Desmos, type the rows into a table and regress. <strong>“Which table matches this equation” runs the other way</strong> – plug each row in and check."}]},{"title":"Table → equation","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Select the <strong>“Add Item”</strong> button and select <strong>“table”,</strong> or simply type table"},{"sub":false,"html":"Plug in the points"},{"sub":false,"html":"A small line of best fit icon will pop up that says <strong>“Add Regression”</strong>. Select that."},{"sub":false,"html":"The equation will be written out for you below! We can use the graph and parts of the equation to answer the question."}]}],"qids":["a9039591","9551cd91","c651cc56","f7e39fe9","78391fcc","e25f0807"],"title":"Table → equation","checkQids":["597c0de3","12983c1e","bbb0359a","606cdce7","23c5d458","96136818","7e1bff94","49800634","50f4cb9c","9bbce683"]},{"notes":[{"title":"Equation → table","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Type the equation into Desmos exactly as given"},{"sub":false,"html":"Find equivalent functions using <strong>“create table”</strong> – Desmos fills in points on the line"},{"sub":false,"html":"Match it against the answer choices – or plug the first x-value from each choice into the equation and check. We can use this for more complex simplification questions later."}]}],"qids":["1efd8202","a130fcdc","4acd05cd","cea27ab2"],"title":"Equation → table","checkQids":["e7343559","83f2c3bf","a5834ea4","935b8735","d0e614a6"]},{"notes":[{"title":"When given two points","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Treat it like a table problem: type the points into the table, select <strong>“Add Regression”</strong>, and use the equation to find your answer."}]}],"qids":["d7c8ba0b","fb43b85f"],"title":"When given two points","checkQids":["f81a0503","a775af14","7e3f8363","6c5c2a81","c22b5f25","4b0c156b","265f2a53","c387583f","7d7ce88a","fdee0fbf"]}]},{"id":"m2.3","num":"2.3","title":"Word Problems: Building the Equation","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“Sean rents a tent at a cost of $11 per day plus a onetime insurance fee of $10. Which equation represents the total cost c, in dollars, to rent the tent with insurance for d days?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“A teacher is creating an assignment worth 70 points. The assignment will consist of questions worth 1 point and questions worth 3 points. Which equation represents this situation, where x represents the number of 1-point questions and y represents the number of 3-point questions?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"\"The relationship between two variables, x and y, is linear. For every increase in the value of x by 1, the value of y increases by 8. When the value of x is 2, the value of y is 18. Which equation represents this relationship?\""}]},{"title":"Two shapes, and the words that tell you which","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Every linear word problem is <strong>one of two shapes</strong>, and deciding which one is the whole setup. Ask one question: <strong>is there a starting amount?</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"<strong>yes – a starting amount plus a rate</strong> → slope-intercept, y = mx + b. The giveaway is a fee, a down payment, an initial value, “at the beginning” – alongside a “per” or an “each”."},{"sub":true,"html":"<strong>no – two different things adding to one total</strong> → standard form, Ax + By = C. The giveaway is two quantities being counted and a single total: 1-point and 3-point questions, 5-pound and 10-pound boxes, hours consulting and hours drawing."},{"sub":false,"html":"Once the shape is settled, they will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"the <strong>equation</strong> that models the situation"},{"sub":true,"html":"the <strong>output</strong> at a given input"},{"sub":true,"html":"the <strong>input</strong> that reaches a target output"},{"sub":true,"html":"what a <strong>number in the equation means</strong> (2.4)"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: <strong>name the shape before writing anything.</strong> Everything after that is 2.1 – the same two forms, now with a story in front of them."}]},{"title":"**Slope-Intercept Form**: given the slope and y-intercept","lead":"Almost every one of these is the same shape:","formula":"total (y) = (rate • x) + starting amount","listhead":"Find those three things in the paragraph and the equation writes itself:","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Slope:</strong> average, constant, rate, each, per, increase"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>y-intercept:</strong> down payment, initial, “at the beginning”, starting amount"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Point:</strong> “at ___ units, it’s ___ units”. These cases we end up using <strong>point-slope form</strong>, which we can recall is just a slightly modified version of slope-intercept form."}]},{"title":"How do we tell parts of the equation?","lead":"Recall that we say x is the <strong>independent variable,</strong> and that y is the <strong>dependent variable</strong>. The context of the problems will tell us what our x and y variables are.","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Example:</strong> Sean rents a tent at a cost of $11 per day plus a onetime insurance fee of $10. Which equation represents the total cost c, in dollars, to rent the tent with insurance for d days?"},{"sub":true,"html":"What are the units/measurements presented in the problem?"},{"sub":true,"html":"Which one <strong>depends on the other?</strong> The <strong>total cost</strong> (y) depends on the <strong>number of days</strong> (x)."},{"sub":true,"html":"<strong>We can use the slope rate</strong> <strong>y/x too</strong>. The top unit (y) matches the total output unit (total dollars), and the bottom unit (x) follows rate keywords like “per” or “each”."}]}],"qids":["b8cbe394","1ecaa9c0","dae126d7"],"title":"How do we tell parts of the equation?","checkQids":["fe287f7e","1d18794b","520c8177","cee5b352","84664a7c","c039b198","53dc9842","0d6ab461","1c29bfd1","8c515062"]},{"notes":[{"title":"**Standard form**: two things adding to one total","lead":"Not every word problem has a starting amount. When <strong>two different things add up to one total</strong> – 1-point and 3-point questions, 5-pound and 10-pound boxes, hours consulting and hours drawing – the equation is in standard form (2.1):","formula":"(value of one)(how many/x) + (value of one)(how many/y) = total","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Each coefficient is a “per” – the price of one, the points for one, the weight of one. That’s also the answer when they ask what a coefficient means (2.4)."},{"sub":false,"html":"There are always two variables a part of the same category – adults and children, large and small boxes. This is your x and y"},{"sub":false,"html":"Given one of the two amounts? Plug it in and solve for the other. In Desmos: graph the equation, type x = or y = (that amount) on its own line, and read the intersection."},{"sub":false,"html":"Two unknowns and a second fact about them (“twice as many”)? That’s a system – coming up in 2.5."}]}],"qids":["029c2dc2","c5479c1a"],"title":"**Standard form**: two things adding to one total","checkQids":["174885f8","c8e0f511","d1042cf8","b450ab03","ebf8d2b7","dfa45424","610e3d08","39571c77","7038b587","9c7741c6"]},{"notes":[{"title":"When they give you a point instead of the start","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“Three hours after the hike began, the hiker’s elevation was 6,150 feet” – that’s a <strong>point</strong>, not the y-intercept. Use point-slope from 2.1."},{"sub":false,"html":"Two values at two different times? Find the slope first using the table method (“Add Regression”) we’ve discussed."},{"sub":false,"html":"A <strong>graph</strong> gives points too. Read straight across and straight down to a gridline – never estimate from the middle of a square – and two points off the graph give the rate the same way two from a story do."}]}],"qids":["53487897","835eda62","590d662d","16889ef3"],"title":"When they give you a point instead of the start","checkQids":["f09097b1","5be959b2","4fe4fd7c","1e925454","8ad64841","41fdc0b8","2937ef4f","3c26c317","2b15d65f"]},{"notes":[{"title":"“The first ___, then ___ each”","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"HARD","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“$950 for the first 2 hours and an additional $50 per hour for each hour after the first 2 hours” – the $50 only applies to the hours <strong>after</strong> the first 2."},{"sub":false,"html":"So it’s 50(t − 2), not 50t. The starting amount already paid for those first hours."}]}],"qids":["76f29fa5","bbf9e5ce"],"title":"“The first ___, then ___ each”","checkQids":["67d63e19","a7e2859a","be9cb6a2","8c5e6702","114ae8a9","1a2a0f59","36ab4122","d561ceea","6dcf5526"]}]},{"id":"m2.4","num":"2.4","title":"Word Problems: Interpretation","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“d = 16t. The given equation represents the distance d, in inches, where t represents the number of seconds since an object started moving. Which of the following is the best interpretation of 16 in this context?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"There’s nothing to solve. They’re asking <strong>what a number means</strong> – and the answer is always one of the parts of the line."}]},{"title":"What each part means","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Slope</strong> – The rate, or how much y changes for every one of x. Always a “per” or “each”: per hour, per day, per mile."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>y-intercept</strong> – the value when x = 0. The start, the flat fee, the down payment."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>x-intercept</strong> – when y hits 0. The tank is empty, the cargo is gone, the balance runs out. Only really relevant for scenarios where something is being used or spent or sold."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Point</strong> – units of y at units of x"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Desmos can’t help here.</strong> It gives you numbers – it has no idea what they mean."}]}],"qids":["6863c7ce","768b2425","f0773a55","2493c767","daad7c32","e103300e","e86a06fe"],"title":"What each part means","checkQids":["776cfa7c","bc638f2d","8643d906","b9839f9e","5cf1bbc9","b249b059","038d87d7","2e1a7f66","cc7ffe02","2d54c272"]},{"notes":[{"title":"What (20 − x) means","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"HARD","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A minus sign in front of the x means the amount is going <strong>down</strong> – something is being used, drained, spent, or sold."},{"sub":false,"html":"Inside parentheses, <strong>(20 − h) is what’s left</strong> of 20 after h of them are gone. The 20 is the full amount we started with, and h is how much has been taken away."},{"sub":false,"html":"A number in front multiplies what’s left: in 5(20 − h), each of the remaining hours is worth 5 more percent of charge."},{"sub":false,"html":"The x-intercept is when the inside hits zero – when it’s all gone. In 5(20 − h) that’s h = 20 hours, the battery dead."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>(d − 2)</strong> shifts the start instead: nothing counts until d = 2 – the same “after the first 2” idea from 2.3."}]}],"qids":["cf4ffc38","113b938e","620fe971","952240c2"],"title":"What (20 − x) means","checkQids":["1768c6ae","af2ba762","8b9960b2","28996d16","3e9eaffc","56dc8045","1bc11c4e","9d9fe1e6"]}]},{"id":"m2.5","num":"2.5","title":"Systems of Equations","calc":true,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“y = 3x and 2x + y = 12. The solution to the given system of equations is (x, y). What is the value of 5x?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"Two equations, two unknowns. It’s the same idea we’ll use again for single-variable equations in 2.6 – <strong>we’re looking for where two graphs cross.</strong>"}]},{"title":"What can they ask for once we have the crossing?","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Two equations and two unknowns give us <strong>one point</strong> – and the test almost never wants the point:"},{"sub":true,"html":"<strong>x</strong> or <strong>y</strong> on its own"},{"sub":true,"html":"a <strong>combination</strong>, like x + y or 5x"},{"sub":true,"html":"the <strong>number of solutions</strong> (2.7)"},{"sub":true,"html":"a <strong>constant</strong> that makes the system behave a certain way (2.10)"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means graphing is only step one. <strong>Read the last line again before answering</strong> – both coordinates are usually sitting in the wrong answers."},{"sub":false,"html":"A word problem is the same list in disguise. <strong>The two things being counted ARE x and y</strong> – name them before writing anything, and “how many did the dealer sell” turns back into “what is x.”"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>What the test actually does with systems:</strong> 43% of the HARD ones are the no-solution or infinitely-many kind, where the real job is finding a constant, and about a third want a combination rather than x or y on its own. We will get to this in 2.10."}]},{"title":"Process","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Type each function in on its own line, exactly as given. We can tell it’s a function if there are <strong>two variables within a single equation.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"Find where the two graphs cross: <strong>that intersection point is your solution.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"Read the question again – they usually want x, y, or something like x + y or 5x. Both coordinates are sitting in the answer choices"},{"sub":false,"html":"Going forward, when we see two equations and it asks us for the solution, I want us <strong>always graphing.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"If the variables aren’t x and y (like r and s), change them to x and y first – Desmos only graphs x and y."}]}],"qids":["3409707e","7efe5495"],"title":"Process","checkQids":["cd33b015","38a43902","4ec95eab","0d1dca87","ffb371f5","79be5180","e3bbde69","55447be2","2875ba81","b544a348"]},{"notes":[{"title":"Reading the solution off a graph","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Already graphed for us? The solution is the point where the two lines cross – read it straight off the grid."},{"sub":false,"html":"Asked which system is shown? Find each line’s slope and y-intercept from the graph (2.1) and match them to the choices."}]}],"qids":["1006cad7","3f5a3602"],"title":"Reading the solution off a graph","checkQids":["b0fc3166","e6545fa8","e1259a5a","4b06557b","f6b055dc","ccc3ad6b","5f46fc76","3c8801a0","2704399f","6e6a3241"]},{"notes":[{"title":"When they ask for a combination","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“What is the value of x + y” or “8x + 7y”: graph as always, then type the combination in with the x and y we found."},{"sub":false,"html":"Look at the two equations first, though. <strong>Adding or subtracting them</strong> often cancels a variable and hands us the combination directly: x + 2y = 6 and x − 2y = 4 add up to 2x = 10."}]}],"qids":["092ad67d","edc1b7b7"],"title":"When they ask for a combination","checkQids":["608eeb6e","8a87c2c8","ed92fb68","570b5a5d","a94ed4e0","bd45df49","52cb8ea4","3a84f885","73b3b7d8","ea7dec1b"]},{"notes":[{"title":"Systems in words","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"HARD","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Two unknowns – normally <strong>categorical</strong> (e.g., adults vs. children, small boxes vs. large boxes) – in the story means two equations. Give each unknown a letter unless otherwise given, then write <strong>one equation per sentence</strong>."},{"sub":false,"html":"The equations will be in <strong>standard form</strong>: one will sum the actual unknowns, and the other will sum a total amount of money made/the \"total\" unit."},{"sub":false,"html":"Once they’re written, it’s the same process – graph both, find the crossing."},{"sub":false,"html":"Mixtures and percents: the first equation adds up the amounts, the second adds up only the ingredient – <strong>percent × amount</strong>: 0.20a + 0.60b = 0.40(30)."}]}],"qids":["b86123af","71189542","7866a908"],"title":"Systems in words","checkQids":["dba8d38a","44d65912","74c03c21","0e926898","bf4a8b6a","c02f7332","0c541d87","b5021138","792cd940","1e11190a"]}]},{"id":"m2.6","num":"2.6","title":"Solving single-variable equations","calc":true,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“If 2x + 3 = 9, what is the value of 6x − 1?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“What is the solution to the equation √(x + 7) − √x = 1?”"}]},{"title":"One variable, and only four questions","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Everything here reduces to one idea: <strong>where does one side equal the other?</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>value of x</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>how many solutions</strong> there are"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>constant</strong> that makes it have none or infinitely many"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>rearranged form</strong> – x in terms of the other letters (3.1)"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means each side becomes its own “y =” and the crossing is the answer. <strong>What the two sides look like does not matter</strong> – roots, fractions, absolute values, all the same move."}]},{"title":"Process","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Identify an equation with only one variable – no unknown constants (like k or a), no second variable"},{"sub":false,"html":"If it’s a non-x variable (e.g., t, p, r) – change it to x"},{"sub":false,"html":"Take each side of the equation and set it to “y = ”. We’re essentially creating a system of equations!"},{"sub":false,"html":"Intersections point to the solution: the x-coordinate(s) are your solution — then give the question what it actually asked for (6x − 1, not x)"}]}],"qids":["fa80893a","097e10f5","018a2704","7a5a74a6","4771a64a"],"title":"Process","checkQids":["0adbe034","02d29b27","23758540","eac739b2","620abf36","45bba652","ce6b52d8","f60aa0dc","b12e1ab9","771bd0ca"]},{"notes":[{"title":"It works on any equation, not just lines","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Roots, fractions, absolute values, squares – it doesn’t matter what the two sides look like. Each side still becomes its own “y =”, and the crossing is still the solution."},{"sub":false,"html":"The only difference: these can cross <strong>more than once</strong>, so read the question – it may want the positive one, the greatest one, or the sum."},{"sub":false,"html":"Then check the answer in the original equation. A root can’t come out negative, and a fraction can’t have zero on the bottom."}]}],"qids":["09e5e4d3","5ae4803e","10d44e3d"],"title":"It works on any equation, not just lines","checkQids":["c303ad23","d9f0b9aa","da602115","fc3dfa26","6c9bcb46","104bff62","802549ac","5ae186b4","75772200","3d7d7534"]},{"notes":[{"title":"In context","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A story with only <strong>one</strong> unknown is one equation, not a system. Build it the 2.3 way – starting amount + (rate × x) – and set it equal to the total they give."},{"sub":false,"html":"Then it’s the same Process: each side as its own “y =”, and read the intersection."}]}],"qids":["46f68129","8339793c"],"title":"In context","checkQids":["12255364","c3989ef8","078a92d7","69d89f9c","2cdbcdc2","56e373b3","289845c1","79cf8505","feb78194","775a2319"]}]},{"id":"m2.7","num":"2.7","title":"Parallel, Perpendicular & Solution Types","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“y = 2x + 10 · y = 2x − 1. At how many points do the graphs of the given equations intersect in the xy-plane?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“Line k is defined by y = 7x + 1/8. Line j is perpendicular to line k in the xy-plane. What is the slope of line j?”"}]},{"title":"Two numbers, and the order you check them","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Everything in this lesson is decided by the <strong>slope</strong> and the <strong>y-intercept</strong> – and the slope goes first."},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>different slopes</strong> → they cross once, <strong>one solution</strong>. The intercepts never come into it."},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>opposite reciprocals</strong> – flip it over and change the sign, so 7 becomes −1/7 → <strong>perpendicular</strong>. Also slope only."},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>same slope</strong> → now, and only now, look at the y-intercept:"},{"sub":true,"html":"–   different intercept → <strong>parallel</strong>, never meet, <strong>no solution</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"–   same intercept → <strong>the same line twice</strong>, <strong>infinitely many solutions</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: compare the slopes first and stop there if they differ. <strong>The y-intercept is the tiebreak</strong> – and it is the only thing separating “no solution” from “infinitely many”, which is exactly the pair the hardest questions turn on (2.10)."}]},{"title":"Parallel & Perpendicular","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Parallel</strong> – same slope. They never meet."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Perpendicular</strong> – the slopes multiply to −1: flip it over <strong>and</strong> change the sign. 7 becomes −1/7."},{"sub":false,"html":"Given in standard form? Find the slope first with <strong>−A/B</strong> from 2.1."}]}],"qids":["2d0e13a6","deee9063","63be6c9a","92aa3a94","c39dbbdf","184ce5aa","6d8ad460"],"title":"Parallel & Perpendicular","checkQids":["b8fa27db","24854644","0451d754","5b8a8475","002dba45","4722abd2","a0c0a137","b9835972","32f255d5","00723d16"]},{"notes":[{"title":"What do we think when we see “solutions”?","lead":"For lines, a solution is an intersection. Two lines can only do three things:","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Cross once</strong> – different slopes → <strong>one solution</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Never cross</strong> – same slope, different y-intercept (parallel) → <strong>no solution</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Same line twice</strong> – same slope, same y-intercept → <strong>infinitely many solutions</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"One equation works the same way. 3x + 21 = 3x + k is two lines, y = 3x + 21 and y = 3x + k – same slope, so it all comes down to the y-intercept."}]}],"qids":["5c94e6fa","dcc4886a","4b76c7f1","0dd6227f","e77a76ce","6a87902f","79784c23"],"title":"What do we think when we see “solutions”?","checkQids":["e53870b6","9326eeb5","15daa8d6","12ee1edc","63c31372","d909cd31","e142f83c","797a81fb","5e9b6079","e2e3942f"]}]},{"id":"m2.8","num":"2.8","title":"Linear Inequalities I: Solving Them","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“Each episode of a certain podcast must be at least 45 minutes long and at most 55 minutes long. The host has recorded 28 minutes of the next episode. Which of the following could be the number of additional minutes the host records?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“Julissa needs at least 100 hours of flight time to get her private pilot certification. If Julissa already has 86 hours of flight time, what is the minimum number of additional hours she needs?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“y ≤ x + 7 · y ≥ −2x − 1. Which point (x, y) is a solution to the given system of inequalities in the xy-plane?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"An inequality is a line with a <strong>range</strong> of answers instead of one. Everything from 2.1 to 2.3 still applies – we build it the same way, we graph it the same way. The only new thing is the comparison at the end."}]},{"title":"An inequality is a line plus a direction","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Build it exactly as you would an equation, then make <strong>one extra decision</strong>: which way the sign points."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>inequality</strong> that models the story"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>largest or smallest value</strong> that works"},{"sub":true,"html":"– whether a given value <strong>satisfies</strong> it"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>range</strong> of values"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means the sentence decides the sign and the question decides the rounding: <strong>down for a maximum, up for a minimum.</strong> Build first, solve second."}]},{"title":"The language","lead":"Four symbols, and the words that give them away:","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>&lt;</strong> less than, fewer than, below, under – the endpoint does <strong>not</strong> count"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>&gt;</strong> greater than, more than, above, over – the endpoint does <strong>not</strong> count"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>≤</strong> at most, no more than, maximum, up to, within – the endpoint <strong>does</strong> count"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>≥</strong> at least, no less than, no fewer than, minimum – the endpoint <strong>does</strong> count"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>between</strong> → two comparisons at once: 150 ≤ s ≤ 170. <strong>inclusive</strong> tells us the endpoints count (≤ and ≥); “strictly between” or “more than … but less than …” means they don’t (&lt; and &gt;)."},{"sub":false,"html":"Careful with the word <strong>less</strong>. “x is less <strong>than</strong> 17” is a comparison – x &lt; 17. “17 less <strong>than</strong> x” is subtraction – x − 17. Questions put both in one sentence: “x is at most 17 less than 5 times y” means <strong>x ≤ 5y − 17.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"Which side is which? Put the thing being described on the left and read the sentence out loud: “the speed is at least 25” → s ≥ 25."}]}],"qids":["842cec4d","915463e0","75990d2f","c17d9ba9"],"title":"The language","checkQids":["9e5863bd","86f7483f","2869fe95","74d0906a","8f35aea5","563407e5","3216388f","0ef4a7b6","8f0c82e2","5065164d"]},{"notes":[{"title":"Building an inequality from a story","lead":"Same shape as 2.3 – find the starting amount and the rate – but instead of setting it equal to a total, we compare it to a limit:","formula":"starting amount + (rate × x)  ≤  the limit","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“Already has 86 hours, needs at least 100” → 86 + x ≥ 100. What they have goes with what they still need."},{"sub":false,"html":"Two different things and one budget? That’s the standard form from 2.3, with ≤ instead of =: 11x + 14y ≤ 115. Each coefficient is still the cost of one."},{"sub":false,"html":"“Which inequality represents this situation?” – nothing to graph yet. Build it and match, the same way we do in 2.3."}]}],"qids":["4a090a46","c50ede6d","68c5c81a"],"title":"Building an inequality from a story","checkQids":["4047cfa7","00ec9102","ee439cff","208626df","e8fd6d99","34aca984","777c0d1f","dd875c97","963da34c","55ea82f3"]},{"notes":[{"title":"Solving an inequality","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Solve it exactly like an equation (2.6): each side as its own “y =”, and look at where one graph sits above the other. Desmos also takes the inequality typed in one line and shades the x-values that work."},{"sub":false,"html":"The one rule that’s different: <strong>multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative flips the symbol.</strong> −2x &lt; 10 becomes x &gt; −5. Adding and subtracting never flip it."},{"sub":false,"html":"The answer is a range, not a number. The question then asks for one end of it – the most, the fewest, the greatest possible value."},{"sub":false,"html":"Check by plugging a number from the range back into the story."}]}],"qids":["b75f7812","f02b4509","b8e73b5b"],"title":"Solving an inequality","checkQids":["68f2cbaf","72a5fd28","b78cd5df","6fb9804c","55402ee7","eaefa863","aa58c912","27f36beb","e8f9e117","03503d49"]}]},{"id":"m2.9","num":"2.9","title":"Linear Inequalities II: Graphing Them","calc":true,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"Finding Solutions","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Graph the inequality as written"},{"sub":false,"html":"The shaded region is the solution set"},{"sub":true,"html":"<strong>Dashed line</strong> – the line itself is NOT included (&lt; or &gt;)"},{"sub":true,"html":"<strong>Solid line</strong> – the line IS part of the solution (≤ or ≥)"},{"sub":false,"html":"Testing a point? Type it in and see whether it lands in the shading"},{"sub":false,"html":"By hand, the shading is just the y-side: y ≥ means everything above the line, y ≤ means everything below. Not sure? Test (0, 0) – if it makes the inequality true, shade the side it’s on."}]}],"qids":["744ee7d7","e723bd67","0b221d05"],"title":"Finding Solutions","checkQids":["c9355dec","59a49431","89541f9b","c41e5688","d02193fb","bf5f80c6","6cb9bf45","d9733ed9","a049f400","187f74bc"]},{"notes":[{"title":"The answer is a region, not a point","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Once it is graphed, the shaded area <strong>is</strong> the answer set."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– which <strong>point</strong> lies in the solution region"},{"sub":true,"html":"– which <strong>system</strong> the graph shows"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>maximum or minimum</strong> inside the region"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: graph it as written and read the shading. A point has to work in <strong>every</strong> inequality, so a single failure rules it out – which is the fastest way to eliminate."}]},{"title":"Systems of inequalities","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Two inequalities, two shaded regions. The solutions are where they <strong>overlap</strong>."},{"sub":false,"html":"Same idea as systems of equations – except now the answer is a whole region instead of one point."},{"sub":false,"html":"Checking a point? It has to work in <strong>both</strong> – one failure rules it out."}]}],"qids":["968e9e51","a4ce75f7","541bef2f"],"title":"Systems of inequalities","checkQids":["cfe67646","948087f2","f2bbd43d","ecca0603","830120b0"]},{"notes":[{"title":"Maximums and minimums in words","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"The answer is usually a whole number – packages, trips, people."},{"sub":false,"html":"Solve, then round the right way: <strong>down for a maximum, up for a minimum.</strong> Desmos will hand you 15.8 and stop – the rounding is yours."},{"sub":false,"html":"Rounding a minimum down is the classic miss: 4.2 packages means we buy 5, not 4."}]}],"qids":["f224df07","45cfb9de","ee2f611f"],"title":"Maximums and minimums in words","checkQids":["2fba2b36","15b7e479","05b8dcf6","d0404b03","b19204a7","d914abec","90bd9ef8","64c85440","6c71f3ec","e7c0f085"]}]},{"id":"m2.10","num":"2.10","title":"The Hardest Algebra Questions","calc":true,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“(3/2)y − (1/4)x = 2/3 − (3/2)y · (1/2)x + 3/2 = py + 9/2. In the given system of equations, p is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of p?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“ax + by = 72 · 6x + 2by = 56. In the given system of equations, a and b are constants. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of a?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“7x + 6y = 5 · 28x + 24y = 20. For each real number r, which of the following points lies on the graph of each equation in the given system?”"}]},{"title":"Every question here is a rule you already know, wearing a disguise","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Nothing new is tested in this lesson. What changes is that the <strong>form is hidden</strong>."},{"sub":false,"html":"The disguises, and what each one really is:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the two equations are written differently → <strong>rearrange before you compare</strong> (2.7)"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a constant sits where a number should be → <strong>match the slopes, or the intercepts</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– two constants at once → find the <strong>relationship</strong> between them, not their values"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the function is given as f(3) = 17 → that is a <strong>point</strong> (2.1)"},{"sub":true,"html":"– “for all values of k” → the k has to <strong>cancel</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: before solving anything, put everything into the same form. <strong>That step is the question.</strong>"}]},{"title":"Rearrange before you compare","lead":"The rule from 2.7 has not changed. What changes at the top end is that they hide it.","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"The two equations are almost never handed to us in the same form. Variables on both sides, fractions on both sides, the constant buried in the middle – <strong>get both into y = mx + b first</strong>, then compare."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>No solution</strong> – same slope, different y-intercept. <strong>Infinitely many</strong> – same slope, same y-intercept. Matching the slopes is what gives us the constant."},{"sub":false,"html":"The constant sits in the slope? Set the two slopes equal and solve for it. The constant sits in the y-intercept instead? The slopes already match, so the answer comes from the intercepts."},{"sub":false,"html":"In Desmos: put a <strong>slider</strong> on the constant and drag it until the lines lie on top of each other (infinitely many) or run parallel (no solution). Slower than by hand, but it never lies."},{"sub":false,"html":"“Which equation could be the second equation” is the same question backwards – find the slope of the one they gave us, then find the choice with that same slope and a different y-intercept."}]}],"qids":["ff501705","59813abf","571174f3","4becad44","b5f62071"],"title":"Rearrange before you compare","checkQids":["f75bd744","45a534d0","14360f84","59352689","e1248a5c","4f2419a6","41fe726e","567ac7ab"]},{"notes":[{"title":"Two constants at once","lead":"With two unknown constants, one equation is not enough. What we are after is the relationship between them.","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Line the two equations up and ask what the first has to be multiplied by to become the second. That multiplier is the whole question."},{"sub":false,"html":"They usually ask for a <strong>combination</strong> – a/b, g/k, or the value of one constant once the other is known. Answer what they asked for, not the thing we solved for first."},{"sub":false,"html":"A system with ordinary numbers in it can still be done the fast way – type both equations into Desmos and click the intersection. The constants are the only reason we are doing this by hand."}]}],"qids":["1b1deebe","f718c9cf","fb5e7f59"],"title":"Two constants at once","checkQids":["1982016e","1eb097ba","e5a71a84","46c43c4f","16546c6f","174cf4c5"]},{"notes":[{"title":"“For each real number r”","lead":"A shape worth recognizing on sight, because it looks like a system and is not.","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“7x + 6y = 5 · 28x + 24y = 20” – the second equation is the first one multiplied by 4. It is <strong>the same line twice</strong>, so every point on that line is a solution."},{"sub":false,"html":"Check it in one step: divide the larger equation through and see whether it collapses onto the smaller one."},{"sub":false,"html":"The choices are written in terms of r. Substitute one into the original equation and see whether it holds – or type the line into Desmos and test each choice."}]}],"qids":["979c6ebc","75012ee7"],"title":"“For each real number r”","checkQids":["f03465dc"]},{"notes":[{"title":"Inequalities at the top end","lead":"The same rules as 2.8 and 2.9, in harder wrapping.","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“At most” and “at least” still set the direction. The trap is the order: “x is at most 2 less than 3 times y” is <strong>x ≤ 3y − 2</strong>, not 2 − 3y."},{"sub":false,"html":"The answer is usually the largest or smallest whole number that works. Solve for the boundary first, then round the right way – <strong>down for a maximum, up for a minimum.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"Budgets, base salary plus commission, consecutive integers: build the inequality first and solve it second. Desmos will not build it for us."}]}],"qids":["1a621af4","ee7b1de1","fbd5483f"],"title":"Inequalities at the top end"},{"notes":[{"title":"Working backwards through the function","lead":"A linear function still only has a slope and a starting value. These hide one of them inside the notation.","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“f(3) = 17 and f(11) = 5” is <strong>two points</strong>. Slope first, then the equation, then answer what they actually asked – or put the two points in a Desmos table and regress, whichever is faster."},{"sub":false,"html":"“f(x + 4) − f(x) = 10” says the output climbs 10 every time x climbs 4. That is the slope: <strong>10/4</strong>."},{"sub":false,"html":"The input is not always plain x. In f(cx) = x − 8, the input is <strong>cx</strong> – so f(2) means cx = 2. Substitute it; do not pattern-match on the 2."},{"sub":false,"html":"“for all values of k” means the k has to cancel. Group the terms carrying k and find the x that makes that group zero – the same idea as “for each real number r” above."}]}],"qids":["e03b6e4c","0b0fa68b","a54f1ba0","99b3ba51"],"title":"Working backwards through the function","checkQids":["a5882327","14f23a5b","6989c80a","0bd33265","4b817dac","e3cf671f","91004b16","d861d8ab","2b7888b5","ff85f441"]}]}]},{"name":"Advanced Math","lessons":[{"id":"m3.1","num":"3.1","title":"Introduction to Regressions: Solving for X and Expressing One Variable in Terms of Another","calc":true,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What is Regression?","lead":"Recall when we put two or more points into the table to create a linear equation, an “Add Regression” option appears. What’s happening is that Desmos is writing the equation “y1 ~ mx1 + b” and fitting the points to the equation to find the constants m and b. y and x can only be expressed as y1 and x1 The equals sign “=” becomes a tilde “~” This is called <strong>Regression,</strong> and it’s different from normal equations because it allows us to approximate <strong>direct values in an equation</strong> (faster way to solve for x!) instead of graphing equations.","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[]},{"title":"What regression is actually for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Desmos will find any constant in any equation if we give it points. That turns three different-looking questions into one."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>constant</strong> inside an equation, given points or a table"},{"sub":true,"html":"– one variable <strong>in terms of</strong> the others"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>model</strong> that fits a set of data (5.5)"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>value</strong> the model predicts"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: if there is a letter we do not know and numbers that must satisfy the equation, it is a regression. Type it with ~ and x<sub>1</sub>, and read the constant off."}]},{"title":"Solving for the Unknown","lead":"Instead of graphing each side of the equation and finding the intersection, we can now <strong>type the equation as a regression using tilde “~” and x1 or y1 if needed.</strong> Two things though: We cannot reliably use this for functions that yield more than one solution since regression only gives us one solution and one solution only. Don’t completely dismiss the previous method. Generally speaking, it’s best to use this strategy when we have relatively long but simpler equations.","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[]}],"qids":["f11ffa93","0380bbdc","bf749912","d0a2fed5","bef4b1c6"],"title":"Solving for the Unknown"},{"notes":[{"title":"Expressing One Variable in Terms of Another: What we’re looking For","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“6r = 7s + t. The given equation relates the variables r, s, and t. Which equation correctly expresses s in terms of r and t?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“The speed of sound in dry air, v, can be modeled by the formula v = 331.3 + 0.606T. Which of the following correctly expresses T in terms of v?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"We can tell it’s one of these because the answer choices have <strong>no numbers to find</strong> – just letters rearranged."}]},{"title":"Another Way to Solve Instead of by Hand","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Find the letter you’re solving for – that’s the only one that ends up alone on its own side in the answers."},{"sub":false,"html":"Rewrite the given equation, <strong>but instead of = we use ~</strong>, and we set values to the other variables in the equation (e.g., p = 3, c = 4) on separate expression lines. You’ll see that Desmos will automatically solve for the letter you’re solving for."},{"sub":false,"html":"Finally, rewrite the answers and see which value matches with the value Desmos found earlier."}]}],"qids":["ad03127d","95ed0b69","b03adde3","c8e9a011","17d0e87d","133f3e41"],"title":"Another Way to Solve Instead of by Hand"},{"notes":[{"title":"When there’s a story","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Same move – the letters just mean something now. <strong>The story doesn’t change the algebra</strong>, so don’t let it slow you down."},{"sub":false,"html":"Sometimes they ask for a whole chunk – like 4j + 9 – instead of one letter. Treat the chunk as if it were one letter."},{"sub":false,"html":"Normally these are just easiest to solve by hand since it’s two-three algebra steps."}]}],"qids":["98f735f2","630897df","652054da","f2f3fa00","5910bfff"],"title":"When there’s a story"}]},{"id":"m3.2","num":"3.2","title":"Equivalent Expressions","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What We’re Looking For","lead":"“Which expression is equivalent to _____?” “The expression above is equivalent to ax^2 + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of b?”","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[]},{"title":"They are asking whether two things are the same","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Every question in this lesson is one idea: <strong>are these two expressions equal?</strong> The work is only ever rewriting."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– which expression is <strong>equivalent</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>constant</strong> inside an equivalent form (a, b, c, k)"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>factor</strong> of the expression"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the expression in a <strong>named form</strong> – expanded, factored, or over one denominator"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: pick a number for every letter, type the original and each choice, and keep what matches. <strong>If two choices both survive, test a second number</strong> – equivalent means equal for EVERY x, and two expressions can agree at one value by accident."}]},{"title":"When Constants are NOT Involved, Solve by Hand or Graph Answers","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Sometimes questions are simple enough to solve by hand."},{"sub":false,"html":"If we’re not confident in our skills, though, we can also just graph the given equation and match it to the given answers. Your answer will overlap with the original equation."}]}],"qids":["fd4b2aa0","974d33dc","5d93c782","0b3d25c5","22fd3e1f"],"title":"When Constants are NOT Involved, Solve by Hand or Graph Answers"},{"notes":[{"title":"When Constants ARE Involved, Use Regression","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Write the full equation using ~ instead of =, and x<sub>1</sub> instead of x."},{"sub":false,"html":"Desmos should immediately give you an answer."},{"sub":false,"html":"Depending on the degree, you may need multiple values of x<sub>1</sub> — create a list, x<sub>1</sub> = [1...10], and the solution updates itself."},{"sub":false,"html":"To check equivalence instead: type y = in front of the original and each option; the identical curve disappears underneath."}]}],"qids":["b4a6ed81","26e83bbc","a391ed22","137cc6fd","00165291","38c90632","ea6d05bb"],"title":"When Constants ARE Involved, Use Regression"},{"notes":[{"title":"Exponents and Roots","lead":"Same base multiplied → <strong>add</strong> the exponents. Divided → <strong>subtract</strong> them. A power of a power → <strong>multiply</strong> them.","formula":"xᵃ · xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ     xᵃ / xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ     (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A root is a fraction exponent: the nth root of xᵐ is x^(m/n). A square root is the power 1/2, a cube root is 1/3."},{"sub":false,"html":"A negative exponent flips it to the other side of the fraction: x⁻² = 1/x²."},{"sub":false,"html":"Desmos way – the same move as 3.1: pick a number for every letter (like x = 2 and y = 3) on their own lines, then type the original and each answer choice. The choice with the same decimal is the equivalent one."}]}],"qids":["290cdc2c","1be909aa"],"title":"Exponents and Roots"},{"notes":[{"title":"Radicals: Make Every Root a Fraction Exponent","lead":"A root and a power are the same thing written two ways:","formula":"ⁿ√(xᵐ) = x^(m/n)","listhead":"Convert first, and the three rules above do the rest:","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Rewrite every root as a fraction exponent before anything else. √(x³) · ∛(x⁵) becomes x^(3/2) · x^(5/3) – now it is just <strong>add the exponents</strong>, and 3/2 + 5/3 = 19/6."},{"sub":false,"html":"The negative-exponent rule above is what makes these collapse: an x⁻² sitting in a <strong>denominator</strong> comes back <strong>up</strong> as x², so a fraction of roots usually becomes a single product."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Same base, both sides.</strong> 8^x = √(32) looks impossible until we notice 8 = 2³ and 32 = 2⁵. Over base 2 it reads 2^(3x) = 2^(5/2), so 3x = 5/2. <strong>Rewriting both sides over one base is the whole move</strong> – look for the powers of 2, 3 and 5 hiding in 4, 8, 9, 16, 25, 27, 32 and 81."},{"sub":false,"html":"Already sharing a base? Then there is nothing to rewrite – convert the root to its fraction exponent and set the two exponents equal."},{"sub":false,"html":"Desmos checks it in one line: pick x = 2, type the original and our answer on their own lines, and compare the decimals."}]}],"qids":["a520ba07","40c09d66","2c88af4d","287149b8","2289b199"],"title":"Radicals: Make Every Root a Fraction Exponent"},{"notes":[{"title":"Algebraic Fractions","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Common denominator first.</strong> For 4/(4x − 5) − 1/(x + 1), multiply each fraction by whatever the other one’s denominator is, then combine the tops and leave the bottom factored."},{"sub":false,"html":"A fraction divided by a fraction? <strong>Flip the bottom one and multiply.</strong> Dividing never needs a common denominator."},{"sub":false,"html":"Top heavier than the bottom? Factor the top and look for the bottom hiding inside it. If it does not divide cleanly – like x² − 2x − 5 over x − 3 – that is the signal to do the long division and keep the remainder."},{"sub":false,"html":"The “where x &gt; 9” tacked on the end is only there to keep the denominator off zero. It never changes the answer."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>The Desmos check is the same as always</strong>: pick a number for x, type the original and each choice on its own line, and keep the one that matches. Faster than the algebra, and it cannot be fooled."}]}],"qids":["abc61109","6a77859a","d8789a4c","89fc23af","433184f1"],"title":"Algebraic Fractions"},{"notes":[{"title":"Factoring","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A factor is a piece that multiplies to make the expression. We’ll use factors to find solutions in 3.3."},{"sub":false,"html":"Pull out what every term shares first: 2x² + 38x + 10 = 2(x² + 19x + 5)."},{"sub":false,"html":"Difference of squares: a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b). Look for two perfect squares with a minus sign between them."},{"sub":false,"html":"Desmos way: a factor (x − r) means the expression equals 0 when x = r. Graph the expression and check where it crosses the x-axis – or multiply each answer choice out and see which graph lands on top of the original."}]}],"qids":["4a5af623","1e8d7183","16de54c7","c3b116d7"],"title":"Factoring"}]},{"id":"m3.3","num":"3.3","title":"Quadratics I: Standard & Factored Form, and Solutions","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"Definition","lead":"A <strong>Quadratic</strong> (or parabola) is a second-degree polynomial, i.e., an equation containing at least one term that is squared. There are multiple aspects of quadratics that we’ll look into, including:","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Solutions, solution types, and the quadratic formula (3.3)"},{"sub":false,"html":"The vertex, or maximum/minimum (3.4)"},{"sub":false,"html":"Forms of quadratics (3.3 and 3.4)"},{"sub":false,"html":"Word problems (3.9)"}]},{"title":"What can they ask about a parabola?","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A parabola has a small, fixed set of features, and every question is about one of them:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>solutions</strong> – also called roots, zeros, or x-intercepts"},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>how many</strong> solutions there are"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>y-intercept</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>vertex</strong>, and the maximum or minimum (3.4)"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>equation</strong>, in one of three forms (3.4)"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means the question is almost never “solve this.” It is <strong>which feature do they want, and which form shows it?</strong> Pick the form that displays it and most of the work disappears."}]},{"title":"Standard Form","lead":"<strong>The standard form of a quadratic is given by the following equation:</strong>","formula":"f(x) = ax² + bx + c","listhead":"The components of this formula are defined as:","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>a</strong>: Where a is a non zero number. It tells us which way the parabola opens: a &gt; 0 opens up (a U, so it has a minimum), a &lt; 0 opens down (so it has a maximum)."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>b</strong>: doesn’t give us a point on its own – we’ll use it together with a later on (the vertex, the sum of the solutions)."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>c</strong>: the y-intercept – the output when x = 0, same as b for a line in 2.1."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>In Desmos:</strong> type the equation in and click on the parabola. Desmos marks the important points in grey: <strong>the y-intercept, the x-intercepts, and the vertex.</strong> Almost every quadratic question is really asking about one of those points – the rest of 3.3 and 3.4 is about knowing which one, and what to do when a grey point isn’t enough."}]}],"qids":["1863e3be","26f5269a","bd4d0e0c"],"title":"Standard Form"},{"notes":[{"title":"What “Solutions” Mean for a Quadratic","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Before, we looked at solutions in the context of <strong>when there is more than one function (a system):</strong> this is where we explicitly look for where the two functions cross. This applies to nonlinear functions as well, but there is a difference when we talk SOLELY about a singular quadratic function."},{"sub":false,"html":"For quadratics (ax² + bx + c): this often means <strong>ZEROS — also called intercepts, x-intercepts, where y = 0, roots, solutions, and factors</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"To find the solutions, just look at the x-intercepts:"},{"sub":true,"html":"A U-shape meets the x-axis three ways: cuts through (two), just touches/bounces off the x-axis (one), or no intersections (none)."},{"sub":false,"html":"Not set equal to 0, like (x − 1)² = −4? Two options: move everything to one side first, or graph each side as its own “y =” and count where they cross – exactly like 2.6."},{"sub":false,"html":"An unknown constant in the equation? Desmos can’t draw one parabola, so put a slider on the constant and move it until the graph does what the question says – cuts through, just touches, or misses the x-axis entirely."}]}],"qids":["332cd67b","3934dd0a","5d30293f","88a0c425","71014fb1"],"title":"What “Solutions” Mean for a Quadratic"},{"notes":[{"title":"Solutions from Factored Form","lead":"","formula":"f(x) = a(x − r)(x − s)","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"This is the “factors” from the list above. <strong>Each factor gives a solution:</strong> if (x − r) is a factor, then x = r is a solution and (r, 0) is an x-intercept. Those are all the same fact."},{"sub":false,"html":"Why it works: a product can only equal 0 if one of its pieces is 0. So set each factor equal to 0 and solve."},{"sub":false,"html":"Careful with the number in front of x: 5x + 4 = 0 gives x = −4/5, not −4."},{"sub":false,"html":"In Desmos we don’t need to factor anything – type the equation exactly as given and the x-intercepts are the solutions. Factoring by hand is only worth it when it’s quick."}]}],"qids":["2926cc6d","13e57f0a","062f86db"],"title":"Solutions from Factored Form"},{"notes":[{"title":"The Quadratic Formula","lead":"","formula":"x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / (2a)","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"It gives the exact solutions of any quadratic in standard form. The ± is why there are usually two."},{"sub":false,"html":"Desmos gives us the x-intercepts as <strong>decimals</strong>. When the answer choices have square roots in them, we have two options:"},{"sub":true,"html":"Use the formula to get the exact answer."},{"sub":true,"html":"Or click the x-intercept, then type each answer choice into Desmos and see which one gives the same decimal."},{"sub":false,"html":"Asked for a missing piece, like a and b in (a + √b)/6? Work the formula out and match it piece by piece – Desmos gives a decimal, not the a and the b."},{"sub":false,"html":"When the answer choices are already in formula shape, we’re just reading off a, b and c: the −b, the b² − 4ac under the root, the 2a underneath."},{"sub":false,"html":"Type the <strong>whole denominator in brackets</strong>: <code>(-b + sqrt(b^2-4ac))/(2a)</code>. Without them Desmos reads <code>/2a</code> as “divide by 2, then multiply by a” and the answer comes out wrong."}]}],"qids":["928498f3","a050f15e","5da5c665"],"title":"The Quadratic Formula"},{"notes":[{"title":"Sum and Product of Solutions","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Desmos: graph it, click both x-intercepts, and add or multiply the two x-values."},{"sub":false,"html":"Not set equal to 0? Same as before – graph both sides and use the x-values of the intersections."},{"sub":false,"html":"By hand, straight off standard form: the solutions <strong>add to −b/a</strong> and <strong>multiply to c/a</strong>. This is the only way when the equation has letters in it."}]}],"qids":["4b6f0a3f","f90aecc2","722de804","2c05d312"],"title":"Sum and Product of Solutions"}]},{"id":"m3.4","num":"3.4","title":"Quadratics II: The Vertex, and Which Form to Use","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“f(x) = (x − 10)(x + 13). The function f is defined by the given equation. For what value of x does f(x) reach its minimum?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“f(x) = −6x² + 60x − 126. Which of the following equivalent forms of the equation displays the maximum value of the function as a constant or coefficient?”"}]},{"title":"Three forms, and which one shows what","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A parabola has one shape but three ways to write it, and the question tells us which one to use by what it asks for."},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>standard</strong> ax² + bx + c → shows the <strong>y-intercept</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>vertex</strong> a(x − h)² + k → shows the <strong>vertex</strong>, and the max or min"},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>factored</strong> a(x − r)(x − s) → shows the <strong>x-intercepts</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means the question “which equivalent form displays…” is not asking us to rewrite anything. <strong>Find the value first – click it in Desmos – then pick the choice with that number sitting in it.</strong>"}]},{"title":"The Vertex","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"The vertex is the turning point of the parabola – the lowest point if it opens up (a &gt; 0), the highest point if it opens down (a &lt; 0)."},{"sub":false,"html":"It answers two different questions. The <strong>y-value</strong> is the minimum or maximum. The <strong>x-value</strong> is where (or when) it happens. Read the question to see which one they want."},{"sub":false,"html":"Desmos: graph it and click the vertex – it’s one of the grey points from 3.3. This works no matter what form the equation is in."}]}],"qids":["782a8a53","341ba5db","39714777","358f18bc"],"title":"The Vertex"},{"notes":[{"title":"Vertex Form","lead":"","formula":"f(x) = a(x − h)² + k","listhead":"The components of this formula are defined as:","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>(h, k)</strong>: the vertex – we can read it straight off without graphing. Careful with the sign: (x − 3) means h = 3, and (x + 3) means h = −3."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>k</strong>: the minimum (if a &gt; 0) or the maximum (if a &lt; 0)."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>a</strong>: same job as in standard form – which way it opens."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Which form to use</strong> depends on what they are asking for:"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Standard form</strong> f(x) = ax² + bx + c → shows the y-intercept (c)."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Vertex form</strong> f(x) = a(x − h)² + k → shows the vertex, and the minimum or maximum (k)."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Factored form</strong> f(x) = a(x − r)(x − s) → shows the x-intercepts (r and s)."},{"sub":false,"html":"“Displays ___ as a constant or coefficient” means the number is sitting right there in the equation. Find the value first (click it in Desmos), then pick the choice where that number shows up."}]}],"qids":["a7711fe8","bba18ecb","16aa9758","04bbce67"],"title":"Vertex Form"},{"notes":[{"title":"Building a Quadratic from Points","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Given points or a table from a quadratic? Put them into a Desmos table and type y<sub>1</sub> ~ ax<sub>1</sub>² + bx<sub>1</sub> + c. Desmos finds a, b and c for us – the same regression move from 3.1, now with a squared term."},{"sub":false,"html":"Know one of the constants already (like a = 2)? Type that number in instead of the letter."},{"sub":false,"html":"The table is for something like f(x) + 4 instead of f(x)? Regress it anyway, then take the 4 back off at the end."},{"sub":false,"html":"Then use the equation to answer what they asked – evaluate it, or click the graph."}]}],"qids":["a1e65979","09d21d79"],"title":"Building a Quadratic from Points"}]},{"id":"m3.5","num":"3.5","title":"Other Nonlinear Equations and Systems","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“|x + 45| = 48. What is the positive solution to the given equation?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“y = 76 · y = x² − 5. The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the point (x, y). What is a possible value of x?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"Same process as 2.6: set each side to “y =” and find where the graphs cross. If we see a function: GRAPH IT."}]},{"title":"Everything here is still just a crossing","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Absolute values, roots, fractions, polynomials – different shapes, one method."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>solution</strong>, or the positive one, or the greatest"},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>how many</strong> solutions there are"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>constant</strong> that controls that number"},{"sub":true,"html":"– which values are <strong>not allowed</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: graph both sides and read the crossings. The only extra care is <strong>checking what the shape forbids</strong> – a root cannot be negative, a denominator cannot be zero."}]},{"title":"Absolute Value","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Absolute value is distance from zero – it can never be negative."},{"sub":false,"html":"So |something| = 9 means something = 9 or −9: usually two solutions."},{"sub":false,"html":"|something| = a negative number – no solution."},{"sub":false,"html":"The 2.6 route works here unchanged: <strong>each side becomes its own “y =”</strong> and the crossings are the solutions. Absolute value usually gives <strong>two</strong>, so read which one they want."}]}],"qids":["c6a26e14","9a1f1941","3a9d60b2"],"title":"Absolute Value"},{"notes":[{"title":"Square Roots and Fractions","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Graph both sides – but not every answer you get by hand actually works. Plug it back in."},{"sub":false,"html":"A square root can never equal a negative number."},{"sub":false,"html":"A fraction is zero when the top is zero and undefined when the bottom is zero — find that value and reject it."}]}],"qids":["b80d10d7","f65288e8","66bce0c1","d0a53ef5"],"title":"Square Roots and Fractions"},{"notes":[{"title":"Polynomials: Zeros and Factors","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A zero at x = 4 means (x − 4) is a factor, and the other way around. Same idea as factored form in 3.3."},{"sub":false,"html":"Given a table or points? Look for where y = 0."}]}],"qids":["6bd40794","db6eeb76","9f2ecade","0121a235"],"title":"Polynomials: Zeros and Factors"},{"notes":[{"title":"Systems with a Parabola","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Type both equations in; the intersections are the solutions."},{"sub":false,"html":"A line and a parabola can cross twice, once, or never – same idea as solution types in 2.7."},{"sub":false,"html":"Exactly one solution with a constant? The line just touches the parabola – use a slider."}]}],"qids":["84e5e36c","6e02cd78","2c5c22d0","f5aa5040"],"title":"Systems with a Parabola"}]},{"id":"m3.6","num":"3.6","title":"Exponentials I: Reading the Equation","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“For the function q, the value of q(x) decreases by 45% for every increase in the value of x by 1. If q(0) = 14, which equation defines q?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“The function f(x) = 240,000(1.22)^x gives a company’s predicted annual revenue, in dollars, x years after the company started selling jewelry online. What is the best interpretation of the statement ‘f(5) is approximately equal to 648,650’ in this context?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“f(x) = 2(3^x). For the function f defined above, what is the value of f(2)?”"}]},{"title":"What do we need to create an exponential?","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"To create one you need a <strong>starting value</strong> and a <strong>multiplier</strong> – y = a · bˣ, and nothing else."},{"sub":false,"html":"Every exponential question hands us, or asks for, one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>a</strong>, the value at the start, when x = 0"},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>b</strong>, what it gets multiplied by each step"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>step</strong> itself – how long one multiply takes (3.7)"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>value</strong> of the function at some x"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>equation</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means we read all of them the same way: <strong>name the a and the b before anything else.</strong> Most of the wrong answers are the other letter."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>What the test actually does with exponentials:</strong> they are about an eighth of all nonlinear-function questions, and the ask is nearly always one of the two letters rather than a calculation. The wrong answers are built from the letter you were not asked about, which is why naming both first is the whole technique."}]},{"title":"Definition","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"An exponential function has the <strong>variable in the exponent</strong>: f(x) = 3(2)^x. That’s how we tell it apart from a line (plain x) or a quadratic (x²)."},{"sub":false,"html":"A line <strong>adds</strong> the same amount every step. An exponential <strong>multiplies by the same factor</strong> every step."},{"sub":false,"html":"Table test: equal differences → linear. Equal ratios (divide each y-value by the one before it) → exponential."},{"sub":false,"html":"In Desmos: an exponential curve is almost flat on one side and shoots up (growth) or drops toward the x-axis (decay) on the other. Click it and Desmos marks the <strong>y-intercept</strong> – there’s no vertex, and no x-intercepts unless the graph has been shifted down."}]}],"qids":["02c67921","d675744f"],"title":"Definition"},{"notes":[{"title":"The Parts of an Exponential","lead":"","formula":"f(x) = a(b)^x","listhead":"The components of this formula are defined as:","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>a</strong>: the starting amount – the value when x = 0. Anything to the power of 0 is 1, so f(0) = a. That’s why a is also the <strong>y-intercept</strong>."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>b</strong>: the growth or decay factor – what we multiply by every time x goes up by 1. b &gt; 1 grows; b between 0 and 1 decays."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>x</strong>: how many times we’ve multiplied – the number of years, hours, bounces."},{"sub":false,"html":"“Best interpretation” questions are the same as 2.4 – Desmos can’t tell us what a number means. Match the number to its job: a is the start, b is the multiplier, and f(5) = 648,650 means “at x = 5, the value is 648,650.”"}]}],"qids":["2fec8bf4","d84a514a","70ebd3d0"],"title":"The Parts of an Exponential"},{"notes":[{"title":"Finding b from a Percent Change","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Increases by r% → b = 1 + r/100. Decreases by r% → b = 1 − r/100. So 4% growth is 1.04, and 20% decay is 0.80."},{"sub":false,"html":"Doubles → b = 2. Halves → b = 1/2."},{"sub":false,"html":"Read the wording carefully: “increased <strong>by</strong> 190%” means b = 1 + 1.90 = 2.9. “Is 85% <strong>of</strong> the previous one” means b = 0.85."},{"sub":false,"html":"The starting amount goes in front, the factor goes in the parentheses: 14(0.55)^x, not 0.55(14)^x."}]}],"qids":["1853bb35","53c07bf9","5c00c2c1","1073d70c"],"title":"Finding b from a Percent Change"},{"notes":[{"title":"Evaluating","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Once we have the function, “what is f(2)” or “how much did it decrease from m = 0 to m = 1” is just plugging in. Type f(2), or T(0) − T(1), straight into Desmos."}]}],"qids":["75915e3c","b90c3993"],"title":"Evaluating"}]},{"id":"m3.7","num":"3.7","title":"Exponentials II: Writing the Equation","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“Sara opened a savings account at a bank. The table shows the exponential relationship between the time t, in years, since Sara opened the account and the total amount d, in dollars, in the account. Which equation could represent this relationship?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“p(t) = 90,000(1.06)^t. The given function p models the population of Lowell t years after a census. Which of the following functions best models the population of Lowell m months after the census?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“For the exponential function f, the value of f(1) is k, where k is a constant. Which of the following equivalent forms of the function f shows the value of k as the coefficient or the base?”"}]},{"title":"Writing one means finding two numbers","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"To write an exponential we need the <strong>starting value</strong> and the <strong>multiplier</strong>. Everything in this lesson is one of those two, or the step they act over."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>equation</strong> that models the story"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the multiplier <strong>b</strong>, from a percent change"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the same model over a <strong>different time period</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– an <strong>equivalent form</strong> that displays one of them"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: write a and b before anything else, then ask what one step is. <strong>If the period changes, only the exponent changes</strong> – the a never moves."}]},{"title":"Finding a and b from Points","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Given a table or two points? Put them in a Desmos table and type y<sub>1</sub> ~ ab^(x<sub>1</sub>). Desmos finds a and b – the same regression move from 3.1 and 3.4."},{"sub":false,"html":"Already know one of them (like g(x) = 19 · a^x)? Type that number in instead of the letter. Or set up the equation, 19a³ = 2,375, and solve it the 2.6 way."}]}],"qids":["dd8ac009","f1c81b3b","43926bd9","45034f94"],"title":"Finding a and b from Points"},{"notes":[{"title":"When the Time Period Changes","lead":"b is what we multiply by for each 1 of the exponent. If the doubling happens every 25 minutes, the exponent has to count 25-minute chunks: (2)^(t/25).","formula":"f(t) = a(b)^(t/n)      n = how long one full multiply takes","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Switching units, like years to months? m months is m/12 years, so (1.06)^t becomes (1.06)^(m/12)."},{"sub":false,"html":"Desmos check: the new function has to give the same value as the old one at the same moment. p(1) (one year) should equal r(12) (twelve months) – plug them in and compare."},{"sub":false,"html":"Asked for the percent over a different stretch of time? Plug in that stretch to get the factor, then subtract 1. 1.04^(1.5) = 1.0606…, so about 6% every 1.5 years."}]}],"qids":["3cf2698e","2992ac30","7bbfbe70","1fe10d97"],"title":"When the Time Period Changes"},{"notes":[{"title":"Equivalent Forms and Shifts","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Exponent rules do the rewriting: b^(x + 2) = b² · b^x and b^(x − 4) = b^x ÷ b⁴. So a shift inside the exponent changes a, not b. We’ll do this for every kind of function in 3.8."},{"sub":false,"html":"“Shows ___ as the coefficient or the base” means that number is sitting right there in the equation. Find the value first – f(0), f(1), whatever they name – by typing it into Desmos, then pick the form where that number shows up (same move as 3.4)."},{"sub":false,"html":"Check any rewrite by graphing the original and the answer choice: equivalent functions sit exactly on top of each other."}]}],"qids":["768b60d2","0e61101e","08d03fe4"],"title":"Equivalent Forms and Shifts"}]},{"id":"m3.8","num":"3.8","title":"Functions: Evaluating and Transforming","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“The function f is defined by f(x) = x³ + 15. What is the value of f(2)?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“The function f is defined by f(x) = −4x + 24, and g(x) = f(x − 5). For what value of x is g(x) = 0?”"}]},{"title":"Function notation is an instruction, not a new topic","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"f(x) means “put this in, get that out.” Every question is one of four directions:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– given x, find <strong>f(x)</strong> – evaluate"},{"sub":true,"html":"– given f(x), find <strong>x</strong> – solve"},{"sub":true,"html":"– given a <strong>graph or table</strong>, read one off the other"},{"sub":true,"html":"– given f(x), describe <strong>g(x) = f(x ± c)</strong> – a transformation"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: decide which direction they want before touching the algebra. <strong>The input is not always plain x</strong> – in f(2x) = x − 6, finding f(6) means setting 2x = 6 first."}]},{"title":"Evaluating","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"If f is defined, type f(desired x-value) directly as we did in section two — Desmos returns the value."},{"sub":false,"html":"f(0) is the y-intercept, same as a line."},{"sub":false,"html":"Two functions? Evaluate each one, then combine them: 7f(2) − g(2)."},{"sub":false,"html":"An expression inside the parentheses, like f(3x) = x − 6? To find f(6), make the inside match: 3x = 6, so x = 2, and f(6) = 2 − 6 = −4."}]}],"qids":["befeb95f","3e28a622","d45572cc","a1696f3e","c4cd5bcc","68f343c9","023c0a8d"],"title":"Evaluating"},{"notes":[{"title":"What if we’re asked for x with a given f(x) or y?","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"We can use <strong>Regression</strong> to directly and quickly solve for the x solution: write the function out on one line, then on the next line type f(x<sub>1</sub>) ~ (the value they gave). Desmos hands back x<sub>1</sub>."},{"sub":false,"html":"It’s the same move as 3.1 – the only difference is that the thing we’re solving for is the input instead of a constant."},{"sub":false,"html":"By hand it’s 2.6: set the two sides equal and find where the graphs cross."},{"sub":false,"html":"Careful when the function can hit that value twice (anything with x² or an absolute value) – regression returns <strong>one</strong> x. Graph it if the question wants the greatest, the least, or all of them (3.10)."}]}],"qids":["2cf7f039","346d5c2e","1f0966db"],"title":"What if we’re asked for x with a given f(x) or y?"},{"notes":[{"title":"Transformations","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"For transformations, graph the shifted function and read it visually. This works for every function type – lines, parabolas, exponentials (3.6 and 3.7)."},{"sub":false,"html":"f(x−2) right 2 · f(x+2) left 2 · f(x)−2 down 2 · f(x)+2 up 2."},{"sub":false,"html":"Inside the parentheses moves the <strong>opposite</strong> way to the sign. Outside moves the way you’d expect."},{"sub":false,"html":"Lines shift the same way: moving a line down 5 subtracts 5 from the y-intercept, and the slope stays the same."}]}],"qids":["e9aed539","b1007ba8","99ea3715","2978bd22","aa95fb33","415ab1d2","cd358b89","841ef26c"],"title":"Transformations"}]},{"id":"m3.9","num":"3.9","title":"Nonlinear Word Problems","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“h(t) = −16t² + 110t + 72. The function above models the height h, in feet, of an object above ground t seconds after being launched straight up in the air. What does the number 72 represent in the function?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"Same idea as 2.4 – they’re asking what a number means. Desmos can’t help with the meaning, only the numbers."}]},{"title":"A story, a shape, and one number they want","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Two steps, always in this order: decide what <strong>shape</strong> the story is, then answer what they asked about it."},{"sub":true,"html":"– a percent per step, or “each year” → <strong>exponential</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a greatest or a least → <strong>quadratic</strong>, and they want the <strong>vertex</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– “when does it hit zero” → the <strong>roots</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– “what was it at the start” → the <strong>y-intercept</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: name the shape, then name what each number in it does, <strong>then</strong> read the question. Most of the wrong answers are a different number from the same model."}]},{"title":"What each part means","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Height, revenue, area — three stories, one parabola."},{"sub":false,"html":"The <strong>vertex</strong> answers “what is the greatest” and “when does it happen” — two different coordinates."},{"sub":false,"html":"The <strong>roots</strong> answer “when does it hit zero”. The <strong>y-intercept</strong> answers “what was it at the start”."}]}],"qids":["0ad5012e","04b985e6","5e63b9cf","7d3a84bf","6676f055","5bf0f84a","cb29c54c","9ff88bb5"],"title":"What each part means"},{"notes":[{"title":"Building the Model","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"Write the story as an equation first, then graph it and answer what they asked. The story tells us which shape to write:","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>A percent per step, or “each year”</strong> → exponential, y = a · bˣ. The a is the starting amount and the b is 1 ± the rate – back to 3.6 and 3.7."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>A greatest or a least in the story</strong> → quadratic. Launched, thrown, dropped, revenue, profit, or an area with a fixed perimeter."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Two things multiplying to a fixed total</strong> → one over the other. A rectangle of area A with length x has width A/x, so its perimeter is 2x + 2A/x."},{"sub":false,"html":"Write down a, b and what the variable means <strong>before</strong> graphing anything. Half of these questions ask what one of those three means, not what the answer is."},{"sub":false,"html":"Once it is written, <strong>name the a and the b before reading the choices.</strong> The wrong answers swap them: ask what the 2,500 in 2,500(0.90)ˣ means and one choice will offer “the percent decrease each year”, which is the 0.90 talking."},{"sub":false,"html":"And the number they want is the <strong>change</strong>, not the b. A b of 0.88 keeps 88% and so loses <strong>12%</strong>; a b of 1.02 is <strong>+2%</strong>, not 102%. A t/7 in the exponent means the step is not one year – divide before interpreting."}]}],"qids":["9da41c80","4618501a","2e209e63","7902bed0","f25a34aa","59d1f4b5","48f83c34"],"title":"Building the Model"}]},{"id":"m3.10","num":"3.10","title":"Advanced Regression Techniques","calc":true,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“The table shows three values of x and their corresponding values of y, where y = f(x) + 4 and f is a quadratic function. What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph of y = f(x) in the xy-plane?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“A quadratic function models the height, in feet, of an object above the ground. The model indicates the object has an initial height of 10 feet and reaches its maximum height of 1,034 feet 8 seconds after being launched. What is the height 10 seconds after being launched?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"These are the questions where the regression from 3.1 does the whole job. We give Desmos <strong>the shape of the function</strong> and <strong>the points it goes through</strong>, and it hands back the constants."}]},{"title":"When the regression needs help","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Same tool as 3.1, used where the straightforward version breaks."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a constant when the table is <strong>not the function itself</strong> – y = f(x) + 4"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a model with <strong>no equation given</strong> – pick it from the data"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a value from <strong>only two points</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: regress what they gave you, then undo the wrapper at the end. <strong>Desmos returns one solution, not every solution</strong> – if the question wants the greatest or all of them, that is a graphing job, not a regression one."}]},{"title":"= vs ~","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Use <strong>=</strong> to graph expressions or assign known values."},{"sub":false,"html":"Use <strong>~</strong> to estimate unknown constants (a, b, x<sub>1</sub>, y<sub>1</sub>…)."}]},{"title":"Rules that trip people up","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Never use lowercase x, y or e in a regression – use <strong>x<sub>1</sub>, y<sub>1</sub>, a, b</strong> instead."},{"sub":false,"html":"Already defined a constant (a = 3)? Regression won’t solve for it."},{"sub":false,"html":"Testing a constant like a or b: simulate many x-values with <code>x_1 = [1...10]</code>."},{"sub":false,"html":"Use { } for constraints, like {p &gt; 1}, when the question says the constant is positive or an integer."}]},{"title":"How Desmos solves it","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"It starts at 0, then checks positive and negative."},{"sub":false,"html":"It tries integers before decimals."},{"sub":false,"html":"It returns <strong>ONE value — not every possible solution.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"So skip regression when the question asks “How many solutions?”, “What is the maximum or minimum solution?”, or “What are all the possible values of x?” Those go back to 3.3 and 3.5."}]},{"title":"With a table","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Type the points into a Desmos table."},{"sub":false,"html":"Type the model equation underneath."},{"sub":false,"html":"Switch it to regression form: rename the variables to x<sub>1</sub> and y<sub>1</sub> (the row 1 headers) and change the = to ~."},{"sub":false,"html":"No equation given? Use the <strong>Add Regression</strong> button and pick the model from the dropdown – or drop in a form we already know: quadratic y = a(x − h)² + k, exponential y = a(b)ˣ, circle (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r²."},{"sub":false,"html":"The table doesn’t have to be the function itself. If it gives y = f(x) + 4, regress it exactly as given and take the 4 back off at the end."},{"sub":false,"html":"Only two points and an exponential? Two points is all a·bˣ needs – the same idea as two points making a line in 2.1."}]}],"qids":["59de8f66","263f9937","84e8cc72","84dd43f8","1178f2df","58b4c6f3","02060533"],"title":"With a table"}]}]},{"name":"Geometry & Trigonometry","lessons":[{"id":"m4.1","num":"4.1","title":"Area and Perimeter","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“A rectangle has a length of 13 and a width of 6. What is the perimeter of the rectangle?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“The area of a triangle is 36 square units. If the height of this triangle is 2 units, what is the length, in units, of the base of this triangle?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"Geometry questions almost always hand us everything we need – in the figure, in the question, or on the reference sheet. The work is knowing <strong>which formula to pull and which number goes where.</strong>"}]},{"title":"Area questions are almost never about the formula","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"The formulas are on the reference sheet. The question is which shape, and what they gave us."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– an <strong>area or perimeter</strong> from given lengths"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>missing length</strong>, given the area or perimeter"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a shape <strong>split into pieces</strong>, or one cut out of another"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a shape <strong>on the coordinate grid</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: <strong>label every length on the figure first.</strong> If a length is missing, it is almost always findable from the shape itself before any formula is needed."}]},{"title":"The Reference Sheet","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Every area and volume formula we need is on the reference sheet – the button at the top of the test. We don’t need to memorize these; we need to know how to use them."},{"sub":false,"html":"It also gives us: the special right triangles, that a circle has 360° (or 2π radians), and that the angles in a triangle add to 180°."},{"sub":false,"html":"The ones we’ll use in this lesson:"},{"sub":true,"html":"Rectangle: A = ℓw"},{"sub":true,"html":"Triangle: A = ½bh"},{"sub":true,"html":"Circle: A = πr²,  C = 2πr"}]},{"title":"Perimeter vs. Area","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Perimeter</strong> – the distance around the outside. Add up every side. The units stay plain (inches)."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Area</strong> – the space inside. Multiply. The units are squared (square inches)."},{"sub":false,"html":"Working backwards is the same formula – the unknown just moves. If A = ℓw and we know A and ℓ, then w = A / ℓ."},{"sub":false,"html":"For a triangle, the height is the straight-up distance to the base – not the slanted side."}]}],"qids":["c88183f7","0d43db90","2a549721","32f99025","a4ed5285","1b687ae3"],"title":"Perimeter vs. Area"},{"notes":[{"title":"When the sides are given with variables","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“The width is 4 units longer than the length” – there’s really only <strong>one</strong> unknown. Name it x, then write every other side in terms of x: length x, width x + 4."},{"sub":false,"html":"Now the formulas take those pieces: perimeter = 2x + 2(x + 4), area = x(x + 4)."},{"sub":false,"html":"Given the area or the perimeter? That’s an equation to solve (2.6). If the x’s multiply into an x², it’s a quadratic (3.3)."},{"sub":false,"html":"Answer choices written as expressions? Multiply them out and compare – or pick a number for x, like x = 2, and see which choice matches (3.2)."}]}],"qids":["a490003a","d4950429","4993b828","2c3aefc9","b0dc920d"],"title":"When the sides are given with variables"},{"notes":[{"title":"Shapes in the xy-plane","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Points given on a graph? Count the side lengths straight off the grid, or plot the points in Desmos and read them."},{"sub":false,"html":"Horizontal side – subtract the x-values. Vertical side – subtract the y-values."}]}],"qids":["cf53cb56","eb70d2d0","30aa51b7"],"title":"Shapes in the xy-plane"}]},{"id":"m4.2","num":"4.2","title":"Volume and Surface Area","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“A right rectangular prism has a length of 11 meters, a width of 8 meters, and a height of 10 meters. What is the volume, in cubic meters, of the prism?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“A cube has a surface area of 54 square meters. What is the volume, in cubic meters, of the cube?”"}]},{"title":"Volume is base times height, with one exception","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>A prism or a cylinder is the area of the base times the height.</strong> There are five formulas on the sheet and this one idea covers four of them – if they hand us the base area, we do not even need the rest of the dimensions."},{"sub":false,"html":"A <strong>cone or a pyramid is one third</strong> of the matching prism or cylinder: same base, same height, a third of the volume."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>The sphere is the exception.</strong> V = (4/3)πr³ has no base and no height in it at all – if the shape is a ball, go straight to the sheet."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>volume</strong>, from the dimensions"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>missing dimension</strong>, worked backwards from the volume"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>surface area</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– what happens when a <strong>dimension changes</strong> (4.3)"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: find the base, get its area, multiply by the height. <strong>Given the diameter, halve it first</strong> – every formula on the sheet uses the radius."}]},{"title":"Volume","lead":"All of these are on the reference sheet:","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Rectangular prism (box): V = ℓwh"},{"sub":false,"html":"Cylinder: V = πr²h"},{"sub":false,"html":"Sphere: V = (4/3)πr³"},{"sub":false,"html":"Cone: V = (1/3)πr²h"},{"sub":false,"html":"Pyramid: V = (1/3)ℓwh"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>What’s actually going on:</strong> every prism and cylinder is the same idea: <strong>V = (area of the base) × (height).</strong> If they give us the base area, we don’t even need the rest."},{"sub":false,"html":"A cone or pyramid is <strong>one third</strong> of the matching cylinder or prism."},{"sub":false,"html":"Given the diameter? <strong>Cut it in half first</strong> – every formula uses the radius."},{"sub":false,"html":"“The volume is nπ” – leave the π off and give n."}]}],"qids":["d683a9cc","50774285","c984f1a5","37dde49f","a2659088","179edb12","ae33e0a1","a07ed090"],"title":"Volume"},{"notes":[{"title":"Surface Area","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Surface area is the total area of every face – imagine unfolding the shape flat and adding up the rectangles."},{"sub":false,"html":"A box has 6 faces in 3 matching pairs: SA = 2(ℓw + ℓh + wh)."},{"sub":false,"html":"A cube is 6 identical squares: SA = 6s². Go from surface area to the side, then from the side to the volume."},{"sub":false,"html":"No lid? One less face."}]}],"qids":["4b7bb316","310c87fe","f7e626b2","ba8ca563"],"title":"Surface Area"}]},{"id":"m4.3","num":"4.3","title":"Scaling","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“Square A has side lengths that are 246 times the side lengths of square B. The area of square A is k times the area of square B. What is the value of k?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“Right rectangular prism P is similar to right rectangular prism Q. The volume of prism P is 972 cubic centimeters and the volume of prism Q is 36 cubic centimeters. One edge of prism P has a length of 12 cm. What is the length of the corresponding edge of prism Q?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"Nothing here needs the shape’s actual dimensions. Scaling questions are about <strong>what happens to the answer when every length is multiplied by the same number.</strong>"}]},{"title":"One number controls everything","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Scaling is a single multiplier k, applied to every length. Area and volume follow it automatically."},{"sub":true,"html":"– lengths × <strong>k</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– area × <strong>k²</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– volume × <strong>k³</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: <strong>find k first, from whichever pair they gave us.</strong> Given two areas, k is the square root of their ratio; given two volumes, the cube root. Then apply it to what they asked about."}]},{"title":"The rule","lead":"","formula":"lengths × k    →    perimeter × k,    area × k²,    volume × k³","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Why:</strong> a length is one measurement, so it scales once. Area multiplies two lengths together, so the k shows up twice. Volume multiplies three, so three times."},{"sub":false,"html":"The same k applies to every length in the figure – sides, heights, radii, diagonals."},{"sub":false,"html":"Surface area is an area, so it scales by k² even though the shape is 3-D."}]}],"qids":["babd7461","e0874bc2","c4e5e23c","03c6994f","1f0b582e"],"title":"The rule"},{"notes":[{"title":"Working backwards from the ratio","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Given the two <strong>areas</strong>, the scale factor is the <strong>square root</strong> of their ratio: 648 / 72 = 9, so k = 3."},{"sub":false,"html":"Given the two <strong>volumes</strong>, it’s the <strong>cube root</strong>: 972 / 36 = 27, so k = 3."},{"sub":false,"html":"Then use that k on the length they ask about."}]}],"qids":["e9c5bfb2","167aff9e","73e1b793","502d9690"],"title":"Working backwards from the ratio"},{"notes":[{"title":"Percents and models are scale factors too","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“Each side increased by 20%” is k = 1.2, the growth factor from 3.6 – so the area is multiplied by 1.2² = 1.44."},{"sub":false,"html":"“The model is 1/10 the size” is k = 1/10, so its area is 1/100 of the real thing."},{"sub":false,"html":"Careful when only <strong>some</strong> lengths change (twice the radius, half the height). Then there’s no single k – put the new numbers into the formula and compare."}]}],"qids":["5b2b8866","93de3f84","4757123b","9fec9d49"],"title":"Percents and models are scale factors too"}]},{"id":"m4.4","num":"4.4","title":"Lines, Angles, and Triangles","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“In the figure, line m is parallel to line n, and line t intersects both lines. What is the value of x?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“In △XYZ, the measure of ∠X is 24° and the measure of ∠Y is 98°. What is the measure of ∠Z?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"Most figures say <strong>“Note: Figure not drawn to scale.”</strong> Trust the numbers and the relationships, not what the picture looks like."}]},{"title":"Angles come from a very short list of rules","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Almost every angle question is one of four facts, and the hard ones just stack two of them."},{"sub":true,"html":"– angles on a <strong>straight line</strong> add to 180°; around a <strong>point</strong>, 360°"},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>vertical</strong> angles are equal"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>transversal</strong> across parallel lines makes every angle one of two values"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>triangle</strong> adds to 180°, and an exterior angle equals the two far ones"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: <strong>write a value on every angle you can, not just the one they asked for.</strong> The answer usually appears two steps away from where you started."}]},{"title":"Angles on a Line","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A straight line is 180°. Two angles that sit next to each other on a line add to 180°."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Vertical angles</strong> – the angles directly across from each other when two lines cross – are equal."}]}],"qids":["d71550d0","a456f28c","087cdcfd"],"title":"Angles on a Line"},{"notes":[{"title":"Parallel Lines","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"When a line crosses two parallel lines, it only makes <strong>two sizes of angle: a small one and a big one.</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"Every small angle is equal to every other small angle."},{"sub":true,"html":"Every big angle is equal to every other big angle."},{"sub":true,"html":"A small angle + a big angle = 180°."},{"sub":false,"html":"So we never need the vocabulary (alternate interior, corresponding…). Just ask: is this angle the small one or the big one?"},{"sub":false,"html":"Angles given as expressions (like 6k + 13)? Set them equal if they’re the same size, or add them to 180 if they’re different. That’s a single-variable equation from 2.6."}]}],"qids":["0d3f51dc","2adbf1b1","686b5212","f67255ea","17912810"],"title":"Parallel Lines"},{"notes":[{"title":"Triangles","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"The three angles of a triangle add to 180° (it’s on the reference sheet)."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Isosceles</strong> – two equal sides means the two angles across from them are equal too."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Exterior angle</strong> – the angle outside the triangle equals the two inside angles across from it, added together."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Polygons</strong> – the angles inside any shape with n sides add to (n − 2) × 180°. This one isn’t on the reference sheet: every polygon can be cut into n − 2 triangles."}]}],"qids":["cbe8ca31","c8d60e48","e570a99c","901e3285","014edcb7","f731d88b","2d2cb85e"],"title":"Triangles"}]},{"id":"m4.5","num":"4.5","title":"Similar and Congruent Triangles","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“Right triangles LMN and PQR are similar, where L and M correspond to P and Q, respectively. Angle M has a measure of 53°. What is the measure of angle Q?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“In triangles ABC and DEF, angles B and E each have measure 27° and angles C and F each have measure 41°. Which additional piece of information is sufficient to determine whether triangle ABC is congruent to triangle DEF?”"}]},{"title":"Similar means the same shape at a different size","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Congruent is the special case where the size matches too."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>missing side</strong>, from the ratio"},{"sub":true,"html":"– an <strong>angle</strong>, which never changes under scaling"},{"sub":true,"html":"– whether two triangles <strong>are</strong> similar"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>ratio</strong> of perimeters or areas (4.3)"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: <strong>match the corresponding vertices in the order the question names them</strong> – that order is what tells us which sides pair up, and mismatching it is the mistake this topic is built to catch."}]},{"title":"Similar Triangles","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Similar</strong> means same shape, different size. Every angle stays the same, and every side is multiplied by the same number – the scale factor."},{"sub":false,"html":"Match the corresponding parts by the order of the letters. In ABC ~ XYZ, A goes with X, B with Y, C with Z."},{"sub":false,"html":"Set up one proportion with the matching sides in the same spots: small / big = small / big. Then solve (2.6)."},{"sub":false,"html":"Where they hide similar triangles:"},{"sub":true,"html":"A line drawn parallel to one side of a triangle cuts off a smaller triangle that’s similar to the big one."},{"sub":true,"html":"Two triangles that meet at a point with parallel sides (a “bowtie”) are similar."},{"sub":true,"html":"Shadows at the same time of day: the object and its shadow make similar triangles."}]}],"qids":["e0d2e21a","3563d76d","055aafe7","933fee1a","51f26ce8","2f7c92ad","81b664bc","5b4757df","1318dfea"],"title":"Similar Triangles"},{"notes":[{"title":"Congruent Triangles, and What’s Enough to Prove It","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Congruent</strong> means same shape AND same size – every side and every angle matches."},{"sub":false,"html":"To prove two triangles are <strong>similar</strong>: two matching angles is enough (the third has to match, since they all add to 180°)."},{"sub":false,"html":"To prove they’re <strong>congruent</strong>: we need at least one pair of matching sides on top of that – SSS, SAS, ASA or AAS."},{"sub":false,"html":"Angles alone can never prove congruent. They only prove similar."}]}],"qids":["0bb39de4","2085e10e","1dc7e423"],"title":"Congruent Triangles, and What’s Enough to Prove It"}]},{"id":"m4.6","num":"4.6","title":"Right Triangles","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“A right triangle has legs with lengths of 11 centimeters and 9 centimeters. What is the length of this triangle’s hypotenuse, in centimeters?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“An isosceles right triangle has a hypotenuse of length 58 inches. What is the perimeter, in inches, of this triangle?”"}]},{"title":"What do we need to solve a right triangle?","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A right triangle has five parts we can be asked about: <strong>three sides and two acute angles.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"Give us <strong>any two</strong> of them and every other one is findable:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– two sides → <strong>Pythagoras</strong> for the third"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a side and an acute angle → <strong>SOH CAH TOA</strong> for the rest (4.7)"},{"sub":true,"html":"– two angles only → we get the shape but never the size: that is a similarity question (4.5)"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means the first move is always the same: <strong>label what they gave us and count it.</strong> Any two except two angles is enough, and which two decides the tool."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>What the test actually does with right triangles:</strong> across every released question, <strong>70% of the hard ones want a LENGTH.</strong> 27% hand you one trig ratio and want another, and 27% are a 30-60-90 or a 45-45-90 in disguise. So the default expectation is a side, and the trig is usually the route rather than the answer."}]},{"title":"The Pythagorean Theorem","lead":"","formula":"a² + b² = c²","listhead":"The components of this formula are defined as:","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>a</strong> and <strong>b</strong>: the legs – the two sides that make the right angle."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>c</strong>: the hypotenuse – the longest side, across from the right angle."},{"sub":false,"html":"Missing a leg instead of the hypotenuse? Then it’s c² − a² = b². <strong>Subtract, don’t add.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"In Desmos, just type it: <code>sqrt(11^2 + 9^2)</code>. The answer choices will often have a root in them, so compare the decimals – <strong>unless the blank itself has a root in it</strong>, which is the next section."},{"sub":false,"html":"A rectangle’s diagonal cuts it into two right triangles – the diagonal is the hypotenuse."}]}],"qids":["de9148c4","c9f8d1e9","36661021","13d9a1c3","a71617d3","7c25b0dc","498d6795","4fb972f2"],"title":"The Pythagorean Theorem"},{"notes":[{"title":"Special Right Triangles","lead":"Also on the reference sheet:","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>45°-45°-90°</strong>: sides s, s, s√2. An isosceles right triangle is always this one."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>30°-60°-90°</strong>: sides x, x√3, 2x. The shortest side is across from 30°, and the hypotenuse is double it."},{"sub":false,"html":"An equilateral triangle cut in half makes two 30-60-90 triangles – that’s how we find its height."},{"sub":false,"html":"If we see √2 or √3 in the answer choices, it’s almost always one of these."}]}],"qids":["bf8d843e","f389569d","58c26db8","ffe862a3","25da87f8"],"title":"Special Right Triangles"},{"notes":[{"title":"When the Answer Keeps Its Radical","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Some questions hand us the shape of the answer: “the height is k√3 centimeters, what is the value of k?” A decimal cannot answer that – we have to carry the exact form to the end."},{"sub":false,"html":"Work symbolically, then <strong>match the coefficient</strong>. An equilateral triangle of side 10 splits into two 30-60-90s with short leg 5, so its height is 5√3 – if the blank says k√3, then k = <strong>5</strong>. Halve the side; that is all the √3 ever asks."},{"sub":false,"html":"Simplify under the root by pulling out perfect squares: √80 = √(16 · 5) = <strong>4√5</strong>."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>√2 means 45-45-90 and √3 means 30-60-90</strong>, nearly every time. A root printed in the blank tells us which triangle we are in before we have read the rest of the question."},{"sub":false,"html":"A shape drawn inside a circle is the commonest place these radicals show up – the rectangle’s diagonal is a diameter, and the square’s is s√2. That is in <strong>4.8</strong>, and it is a right-triangle question wearing a circle."}]}],"qids":["f811d345","a4bd60a3"],"title":"When the Answer Keeps Its Radical"}]},{"id":"m4.7","num":"4.7","title":"Trigonometry","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“In the right triangle shown, what is the value of sin x°?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“In △ABC, ∠B is a right angle and the length of BC is 136 millimeters. If cos A = 3/5, what is the length, in millimeters, of AB?”"}]},{"title":"Trig is three ratios and one relationship","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"SOH CAH TOA turns an angle into a ratio of two sides. That is the whole tool."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>ratio</strong>, given the sides"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>side</strong>, given a ratio and another side"},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>another ratio</strong> of the same angle, or of the other acute angle"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>same ratio in a similar triangle</strong> – which never changes"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: <strong>label opposite, adjacent and hypotenuse from the angle in the question</strong> before writing anything. They swap the moment the question switches angles."}]},{"title":"SOH CAH TOA","lead":"","formula":"sin = opposite / hypotenuse    cos = adjacent / hypotenuse    tan = opposite / adjacent","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Trig only works in a right triangle. It tells us the ratio between two sides, based on an angle."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Opposite</strong> – the side across from the angle we’re standing at. <strong>Adjacent</strong> – the side touching that angle (that isn’t the hypotenuse). <strong>Hypotenuse</strong> – across from the right angle; it never changes."},{"sub":false,"html":"Opposite and adjacent switch depending on which angle the question asks about. <strong>Label the three sides from that angle before anything else.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"Given a trig value and one side? Write the ratio, plug in what we know, and solve for the side."}]}],"qids":["a6097ec2","64c1f044","5a7e3b46","902dc959","2bddbc1b","a4c0547f","053e5e9f"],"title":"SOH CAH TOA"},{"notes":[{"title":"Sine and Cosine of the Two Acute Angles","lead":"","formula":"sin x° = cos(90° − x°)","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"The two acute angles of a right triangle add to 90°."},{"sub":false,"html":"The side opposite one of them is adjacent to the other – so <strong>the sine of one angle equals the cosine of the other.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"“cos(Q) = sin(R)” means Q + R = 90. That turns it into a single-variable equation."}]}],"qids":["659cb706","33e29881","14e7c1f4","8027db3f"],"title":"Sine and Cosine of the Two Acute Angles"},{"notes":[{"title":"Similar Triangles Have the Same Trig Values","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Scaling a triangle doesn’t change its angles (4.5), so it doesn’t change sin, cos or tan either."},{"sub":false,"html":"Same angle → same value, no matter how big the triangle is."}]}],"qids":["bbaac300","bcb66188","6ab30ce3"],"title":"Similar Triangles Have the Same Trig Values"}]},{"id":"m4.8","num":"4.8","title":"Circles I: Arcs, Sectors, and Radians","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“A circle has a circumference of 31π centimeters. What is the diameter, in centimeters, of the circle?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“An angle has a measure of 9π/20 radians. What is the measure of the angle in degrees?”"}]},{"title":"A circle is a center, a radius, and a fraction of the way around","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Arcs, sectors and angles are all the same idea: <strong>what fraction of the whole circle is this?</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– an <strong>arc length</strong> or <strong>sector area</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>central angle</strong>, in degrees or radians"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>radius or diameter</strong>, worked backwards from one of those"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>tangent</strong>, which is perpendicular to the radius (2.7)"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: find the fraction first – angle over 360° – and apply it to the circumference or the area. <strong>Radians are just that fraction written over 2π.</strong>"}]},{"title":"Circle Basics","lead":"","formula":"A = πr²    C = 2πr","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Radius</strong> – center to the edge. <strong>Diameter</strong> – all the way across, through the center: d = 2r."},{"sub":false,"html":"Answer in the form “kπ” or “bπ”? Leave the π off and give the number in front."},{"sub":false,"html":"Circles lean hard: across the released questions the three things asked for are an <strong>arc, sector or radian measure</strong> (23%), the <strong>equation</strong> (23%), and a <strong>tangent</strong> (6%), and the rest reach into triangles and trig. The reference sheet and the center-radius habit have to be automatic before any of that."}]}],"qids":["186fdbca","6ae1360d","38517165","96467fea"],"title":"Circle Basics"},{"notes":[{"title":"Arcs and Sectors","lead":"An arc is a piece of the circumference; a sector is a slice of the area. <strong>Both are a fraction of the whole circle</strong> – and that fraction is the central angle out of 360°.","formula":"arc length / circumference = central angle / 360° = sector area / circle area","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A central angle and its arc have the same degree measure."},{"sub":false,"html":"Two radii are always equal – so a triangle made from two radii is isosceles (4.4)."}]}],"qids":["23c5fcce","800e71b8","149021da","69b0d79d","ca2235f6"],"title":"Arcs and Sectors"},{"notes":[{"title":"Radians","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Radians are just another unit for measuring angles: <strong>180° = π radians</strong> (the reference sheet says 360° = 2π)."},{"sub":false,"html":"Degrees → radians: multiply by π/180. Radians → degrees: multiply by 180/π."},{"sub":false,"html":"Trig of an angle in radians? Make sure Desmos is in radian mode (the wrench icon), then type it in."}]}],"qids":["2d521ca9","a0cacec1","9f9112ab","10e059a6"],"title":"Radians"},{"notes":[{"title":"Tangent Lines","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A tangent line touches the circle at exactly one point and is <strong>perpendicular to the radius</strong> at that point."},{"sub":false,"html":"So the slope of the tangent line is the negative reciprocal of the radius’s slope – straight from 2.7."}]}],"qids":["3e577e4a","d39df688"],"title":"Tangent Lines"},{"notes":[{"title":"Shapes Inside Circles","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A rectangle or square drawn inside a circle, with every corner on the circle: <strong>its diagonal is a diameter.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"That diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right triangle (4.6). For a square, diagonal = side × √2."},{"sub":false,"html":"An equilateral triangle with its vertices on the circle: drop the height, use 30-60-90, and the <strong>radius is two-thirds of that height</strong>."},{"sub":false,"html":"Stuck? <strong>Draw the radius to wherever the shape touches the circle.</strong> The right triangle we need almost always has a radius as one of its sides."}]}],"qids":["ae041e52","44b2b894","d7a0172a"],"title":"Shapes Inside Circles"}]},{"id":"m4.9","num":"4.9","title":"Circles II: Equations and Graphing","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“What is the center of the circle in the xy-plane defined by the equation (x − 1)² + (y + 7)² = 1?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“x² + 20x + y² + 16y = −20. The equation above defines a circle in the xy-plane. What are the coordinates of the center of the circle?”"}]},{"title":"What do we need to create a circle?","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"To create one you need the <strong>center</strong> and the <strong>radius</strong>. Nothing else."},{"sub":false,"html":"Any circle question will ask for, or make us find, one of the following:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>center</strong>, (h, k)"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>radius</strong> – or the diameter, which is twice it"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>point on the circle</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>equation</strong> of the circle"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>line or angle attached to it</strong> – a tangent’s slope, an inscribed triangle. Those still start from the center and the radius (4.8)."},{"sub":false,"html":"That means every one of these is the same question: they hand us two of these and want another. The equation holds all of them at once, so <strong>get it into standard form first</strong> and read off whichever one they asked for."}]},{"title":"Standard Form of a Circle","lead":"","formula":"(x − h)² + (y − k)² = r²","listhead":"The components of this formula are defined as:","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>(h, k)</strong>: the center. Same sign flip as vertex form in 3.4 – (x + 2) means h = −2."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>r²</strong>: the number on the right is the radius <strong>squared</strong>. Take the square root to get the radius (and double it for the diameter)."},{"sub":false,"html":"Moving the circle changes the center, not the radius. Same center, bigger radius? Only the right side changes."},{"sub":false,"html":"This is a form we can read – no Desmos needed. Graph it only to check."}]}],"qids":["d03e29f1","fc8aa563","856372ca","5011b039","fa2771d5","d12bca0a","b0a72bdc","a5ff1a96","a38c0183"],"title":"Standard Form of a Circle"},{"notes":[{"title":"Desmos Calculator Functions to Finish Faster","lead":"","formula":"distance((x<sub>1</sub>,y<sub>1</sub>),(x<sub>2</sub>,y<sub>2</sub>))        midpoint((x<sub>1</sub>,y<sub>1</sub>),(x<sub>2</sub>,y<sub>2</sub>))","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Type either one straight into Desmos with the two points and it hands back the answer. <strong>Almost every circle question that gives us points is really one of these two.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Radius:</strong> given the center and a point on the circle, r = distance(center, point). The right side of the equation is that distance squared – Desmos will square it for us if we type distance(...)²."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Diameter:</strong> given both endpoints, distance(end, end) is the diameter, and half of it is the radius."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Center:</strong> given both endpoints of a diameter, midpoint(end, end) is the center. That’s the whole question."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>The far end of a diameter:</strong> the center is the midpoint, so go the same distance from the center again in the same direction."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Inside, on, or outside?</strong> Compare distance(center, point) to r – smaller is inside, equal is on the circle, bigger is outside."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>How far left and right the circle reaches:</strong> the x-values run from h − r to h + r, and the y-values from k − r to k + r."}]}],"qids":["82c8325f","408b39ea","196e8e6e","981275d2","ebbf23ae","acd30391"],"title":"Desmos Calculator Functions to Finish Faster"},{"notes":[{"title":"When the equation is expanded","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"No completing the square. <strong>Type the equation in exactly as it’s written</strong> – Desmos graphs the circle from any form, even with a 2 in front of x² and y²."},{"sub":false,"html":"The center is the middle of the circle on screen. Click the leftmost and rightmost points of the circle: their <strong>midpoint</strong> is the center, and half the <strong>distance</strong> between them is the radius."},{"sub":false,"html":"Only the radius asked for? Read the widest span across the circle and halve it."},{"sub":false,"html":"Answer choices in √ form? Take the decimal from the graph and match it against the choices (3.3)."}]}],"qids":["ee540927","9e44284b","6585d841"],"title":"When the equation is expanded"}]}]},{"name":"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis","lessons":[{"id":"m5.1","num":"5.1","title":"Ratios, Rates, and Proportions","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“In a box of pens, the ratio of black pens to red pens is 8 to 1. There are 40 black pens in the box. How many red pens are in the box?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“A cherry pitting machine pits 12 pounds of cherries in 3 minutes. At this rate, how many minutes does it take the machine to pit 96 pounds of cherries?”"}]},{"title":"Set up the proportion and the rest is arithmetic","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Every question here is two matching ratios with one piece missing."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>missing amount</strong> in a proportion"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>rate</strong> – per hour, per dollar, per unit"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>total</strong>, scaled up from a part"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>density</strong> – amount per unit of space"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: <strong>write the units on both sides and make them line up.</strong> Matching units across the equals sign catches almost every setup error – the one it does not catch is writing the ratio upside down, so check that the bigger quantity is where you expect it."}]},{"title":"Ratios and Proportions","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A ratio compares two amounts. “8 to 1”, 8 : 1 and 8/1 all mean the same thing."},{"sub":false,"html":"Set up a proportion with <strong>the same thing in the same spot</strong> on both sides: black / red = black / red."},{"sub":false,"html":"Then cross multiply – or type it straight into Desmos with x as the unknown (like 2.6) and read the solution."}]}],"qids":["312ba47c","6310adbc","181cc4d6","9fa781f8","20b69297"],"title":"Ratios and Proportions"},{"notes":[{"title":"Rates","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A rate is a ratio with units: miles per hour, dollars per pound, posters per minute. <strong>“Per” means divide</strong> – same as slope in 2.3."},{"sub":false,"html":"amount = rate × time. If we know two of them, we can solve for the third."},{"sub":false,"html":"Letters instead of numbers (like p dollars for every w hours)? Same setup – the letters just ride along."}]}],"qids":["fe1ec415","551c52b9","be35c117","21e539a0","4c5ea142"],"title":"Rates"},{"notes":[{"title":"Density","lead":"Density is just a rate: mass per volume, or people per square mile.","formula":"density = mass / volume","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Given the shape instead of the volume? Find the volume first (4.2)."}]}],"qids":["e3d49511","873d2838","1b403590","69f6717f"],"title":"Density"}]},{"id":"m5.2","num":"5.2","title":"Unit Conversion","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“How many yards are equivalent to 612 inches? (1 yard = 36 inches)”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“Which of the following speeds is equivalent to 90 kilometers per hour? (1 kilometer = 1,000 meters)”"}]},{"title":"Conversion is multiplying by one","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Every conversion factor equals 1, so we can multiply by as many as we like without changing the value."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– one unit <strong>into another</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>rate</strong> converted on both top and bottom"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>squared or cubed</strong> unit"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: <strong>stack the factors so the unit you want to lose is on the opposite side of the fraction bar from where it started</strong>, and it cancels. For squared units square the whole factor, for cubic cube it."}]},{"title":"Converting One Unit","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Multiply by a fraction that equals 1, like (1 yard / 36 inches). Put <strong>the unit we want to get rid of on the bottom</strong> so it cancels."},{"sub":false,"html":"More than one step (fluid ounces → cups → quarts)? Chain the fractions together, one after another."},{"sub":false,"html":"Sanity check: going to a bigger unit (inches → yards), the number should get smaller."}]}],"qids":["4347a032","85b33aa8","aeeaec96","568bc2a4","3310c2ab"],"title":"Converting One Unit"},{"notes":[{"title":"Converting Rates","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A rate has a unit on top and a unit on the bottom. Convert each one separately – one fraction for the top, one for the bottom."},{"sub":false,"html":"Per second → per hour: there are 3,600 seconds in an hour, so the number gets bigger."}]}],"qids":["3c8fdc40","c47256ca","8917ce38","674a4084","d28c29e1","e7133228"],"title":"Converting Rates"},{"notes":[{"title":"Square and Cubic Units","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Square units convert <strong>twice</strong>, cubic units <strong>three times</strong>. 1 foot = 12 inches, so 1 square foot = 12 × 12 = 144 square inches."},{"sub":false,"html":"Same reason as scaling area in 4.3: two lengths multiplied."}]}],"qids":["1b96c116","7d093333","c7c6445f"],"title":"Square and Cubic Units"}]},{"id":"m5.3","num":"5.3","title":"Percentages","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“What is 20% of 440?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“The number a is 70% less than the positive number b. The number c is 60% greater than a. The number c is how many times b?”"}]},{"title":"Percent questions are multiplication in disguise","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Every one of them is a number times a factor. The only question is which factor."},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>p% of</strong> a number → multiply by p/100"},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>p% greater than</strong> → multiply by 1 + p/100"},{"sub":true,"html":"– <strong>p% less than</strong> → multiply by 1 − p/100"},{"sub":true,"html":"– given the <strong>result</strong>, divide instead of multiplying"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: <strong>read the preposition before you read the numbers.</strong> “Of” and “greater than” are different factors, and that difference is what the hard questions in this lesson are built on."}]},{"title":"Percent Of","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Percent means “out of 100”: 20% = 20/100 = 0.20."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>“Of” means multiply.</strong> 20% of 440 is 0.20 × 440."},{"sub":false,"html":"part = percent × whole. Missing the whole? “21 is 21% of what number” is 21 = 0.21x – solve it by hand or in Desmos."}]}],"qids":["8784bc84","284303f1","93724cc6","05195d8a"],"title":"Percent Of"},{"notes":[{"title":"Percent Increase and Decrease","lead":"","formula":"percent change = (new − original) / original × 100","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Increase by p% → multiply by (1 + p/100). Decrease by p% → multiply by (1 − p/100). These are the growth and decay factors from exponentials in 3.6."},{"sub":false,"html":"“20% greater than 60” is 1.20 × 60. “20% less than 60” is 0.80 × 60."},{"sub":false,"html":"Percent change = (new − original) / original × 100. <strong>Always divide by the original.</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>“Of” and “greater than” are not the same sentence</strong>, and CB writes both: 110% <strong>of</strong> z is 1.10z, but 110% <strong>greater than</strong> z is 2.10z. Of → multiply by the percent. Greater than → multiply by 1 + the percent."},{"sub":false,"html":"The bigger the percent, the bigger the gap: “400% greater than x” is <strong>5x</strong>, not 4x. Read the preposition before touching a number."}]}],"qids":["41b71b4e","8a714fa1","ad1d6adb","40e7a1a9"],"title":"Percent Increase and Decrease"},{"notes":[{"title":"Percents of Percents","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Back-to-back changes <strong>multiply – they don’t add.</strong> Up 14% and then up 4% is 1.14 × 1.04, not 18%."},{"sub":false,"html":"“What percent of the whole is…”, and there’s no starting number? Start with 100."}]}],"qids":["8cbf1415","e635aede","ad911622"],"title":"Percents of Percents"},{"notes":[{"title":"Chains: Three Numbers, Two Comparisons","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Set the number everything else is measured against to <strong>100</strong>. “a is 40% less than b” – so let b = 100."},{"sub":false,"html":"Walk the chain one link at a time, using the factors above. If c is then 50% greater than a: b = 100 → a = 0.60 × 100 = 60 → c = 1.50 × 60 = <strong>90</strong>."},{"sub":false,"html":"Read the last line of the question and answer exactly that. “c is how many times b” is 90/100 = <strong>0.9</strong>. “c is p% of b” is <strong>90</strong>. Same work, two different answers – and CB writes both."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Why this section matters more than it looks:</strong> this shape is a fifth of all percent questions but <strong>45% of the hard ones.</strong> Seven of those thirteen also turn on the “greater than / of” trap, and between them the two account for <strong>just over half of every hard percent question</strong> CB writes."}]}],"qids":["20845d36","993000da","2e92cc21"],"title":"Chains: Three Numbers, Two Comparisons"},{"notes":[{"title":"Working Backwards From the Result","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Forwards we multiply, so backwards we <strong>divide</strong>. “The result of increasing x by 200% is 48” is 3x = 48, so x = <strong>16</strong> – and note the factor is 3, not 2."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>0.80x is a decrease of 20%, not 80%.</strong> Multiplying keeps the 80% and removes the rest – the percent they are asking for is the part that went, so subtract from 1 first."},{"sub":false,"html":"After a removal, check what the number they handed us actually counts. “25% were removed and 600 remained” makes 600 the <strong>75%</strong>, so the original was 800. Then subtract – they asked how many were <em>removed</em>, which is 200, not 800."},{"sub":false,"html":"Up and then down by the same percent does <strong>not</strong> land back at the start: 1.25 × 0.75 = 0.9375, a 6.25% net loss. That gap is the whole question."}]}],"qids":["96a45430","1baffbcf","2afd3cec","6ec47e3a"],"title":"Working Backwards From the Result"}]},{"id":"m5.4","num":"5.4","title":"One-Variable Data: Mean, Median, and Spread","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“2, 9, 14, 23, 32. What is the mean of the data shown?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“Data set X: 5, 9, 9, 13 · Data set Y: 5, 9, 9, 13, 27. Which statement correctly compares the mean of data set X and the mean of data set Y?”"}]},{"title":"What can they ask about a data set?","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"The SAT asks about five things here, and <strong>never about mode or range</strong>:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>mean</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>median</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>maximum</strong> or <strong>minimum</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>spread</strong>, meaning standard deviation – always compared, never calculated"},{"sub":true,"html":"– what happens to any of those when a value is <strong>added or removed</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means the work is <strong>reading the display, not computing.</strong> Get the list of values out of the table, dot plot, or bar graph first and the question turns into arithmetic."}]},{"title":"Reading Bar Graphs and Tables","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Read the title and the axis labels first – what is each bar counting?"},{"sub":false,"html":"The height of a bar is how many times that value shows up (its frequency). Read straight across to the axis."},{"sub":false,"html":"“How many more” is a subtraction of two bars; “how many in total” is adding the bars they name."}]}],"qids":["57481175","3aa0642c","29fa7970"],"title":"Reading Bar Graphs and Tables"},{"notes":[{"title":"Mean and Median","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Mean</strong> – the average. Add everything up and divide by how many values there are."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Median</strong> – the middle value, <strong>once the data is in order.</strong> Even number of values? Average the two in the middle."},{"sub":false,"html":"In Desmos: type the list in brackets, like A = [2, 9, 14, 23, 32], then mean(A) or median(A) – or stats(A) for everything at once."}]}],"qids":["12dae628","f890dc20","35bec412","a5b069b4"],"title":"Mean and Median"},{"notes":[{"title":"Frequency Tables, Dot Plots, and Box Plots","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A frequency table is a shortcut: a value of 4 with a frequency of 3 means 4, 4, 4."},{"sub":false,"html":"For the median, find the middle <strong>position</strong>. With 57 values, the median is the 29th one – count down the frequencies until we pass it."},{"sub":false,"html":"Box plot: the line inside the box is the median. The ends of the whiskers are the minimum and the maximum."}]}],"qids":["57f45509","a456cfd2","560fab82","ecbdbe84","1142af44"],"title":"Frequency Tables, Dot Plots, and Box Plots"},{"notes":[{"title":"Outliers and Standard Deviation","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"An extreme value (an outlier) pulls the mean toward it but barely moves the median."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Standard deviation</strong> measures spread – how far the values sit from the mean. Bunched up close together = smaller standard deviation. Spread out = larger."},{"sub":false,"html":"Adding the same number to every value shifts the mean and median by that number, but the spread doesn’t change."},{"sub":false,"html":"The SAT won’t ask us to calculate standard deviation – just to compare it."}]}],"qids":["55cfaf22","d73a67d7","e7d48c8a","416b6482","1e8ccffd","391ae4b2"],"title":"Outliers and Standard Deviation"}]},{"id":"m5.5","num":"5.5","title":"Two-Variable Data: Scatterplots and Models","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“The scatterplot shows the relationship between two variables, x and y. A line of best fit is also shown. Which of the following equations best represents this line of best fit?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"“Of the following, which is the best model for the data in the scatterplot?”"}]},{"title":"A scatterplot asks four things","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A line or curve through the points, and a question about it."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>predicted value</strong> from the line"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>slope</strong>, meaning the rate of change"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the <strong>y-intercept</strong>, meaning the starting value"},{"sub":true,"html":"– which <strong>model</strong> fits – linear, exponential, or neither"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: the line of best fit is a linear function like any other (2.1). <strong>Read the question for which part of it they want</strong>, and remember a prediction is read off the LINE, never off a dot."}]},{"title":"Line Graphs","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A line graph shows change over time. The highest point is the greatest value; the lowest point is the least."},{"sub":false,"html":"The biggest change between two years is the <strong>steepest</strong> piece of the graph – up or down."}]}],"qids":["2e511919","a6b2fcce"],"title":"Line Graphs"},{"notes":[{"title":"Reading a Line of Best Fit","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"A line of best fit is just a line – everything from 2.1 applies. The slope is the rate; the y-intercept is the starting value."},{"sub":false,"html":"Estimating a value? Go to that x-value <strong>on the line</strong> (not on a dot) and read straight across."},{"sub":false,"html":"Slope of the line: pick two points where the line crosses clean grid spots and use change in y over change in x."}]}],"qids":["39aa146d","ae32cc3c","8baf2118","f46139df","03a16790"],"title":"Reading a Line of Best Fit"},{"notes":[{"title":"Choosing the Model","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Rising from left to right → positive. Falling → negative."},{"sub":false,"html":"A straight pattern → linear. Curving and leveling off, or shooting up → exponential. A U-shape or a hill → quadratic."},{"sub":false,"html":"The same amount each step → linear. The same percent each step → exponential (3.6)."},{"sub":false,"html":"Given the data in a table? Put it into Desmos and add a regression (3.1)."}]}],"qids":["15ce8207","43744269","632cf8f9","82aaa0a1","58171b5e","9b5b23fc"],"title":"Choosing the Model"},{"notes":[{"title":"Interpreting the Model","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Same as 2.4: the slope is how much y changes for each 1 of x, and the y-intercept is the value when x = 0. Use the units in the question."},{"sub":false,"html":"A line of best fit is a prediction – an actual data point can be above or below it."}]}],"qids":["661dfddd","1e1027a7","d6af3572"],"title":"Interpreting the Model"}]},{"id":"m5.6","num":"5.6","title":"Probability and Conditional Probability","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“A box contains 13 red pens and 37 blue pens. If one of these pens is selected at random, what is the probability of selecting a red pen?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"Two-way tables where they ask about only one group: “If a member is selected at random from those who live east of the river, what is the probability…”"}]},{"title":"Probability is one fraction, and the denominator is the trap","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"The numerator is what they want. The denominator is <strong>the group they are counting from</strong>, which is not always everybody."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a probability from a <strong>two-way table</strong>"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a probability from a <strong>plain count</strong> – 13 red pens and 37 blue"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>conditional</strong> one – “given that…”"},{"sub":true,"html":"– an <strong>expected count</strong> in a bigger group"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>missing value</strong> in the table"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: <strong>find the group they named before anything else.</strong> “Of the students who passed” makes the denominator that row, not the whole table – and that single misread is most of what this topic tests."}]},{"title":"Probability","lead":"","formula":"probability = (number of outcomes we want) / (total number of outcomes)","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Probability is always between 0 and 1 – it can be a fraction, a decimal, or a percent."},{"sub":false,"html":"Read the total carefully. It’s everything in the pool we’re picking from."}]}],"qids":["c943acba","1dcea480","2a08d878","4e527894","46b2e169"],"title":"Probability"},{"notes":[{"title":"Two-Way Tables","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Each cell is a group that fits two descriptions at once (lives east of the river AND is under 40)."},{"sub":false,"html":"The totals row and column are already added for us – use them."}]}],"qids":["2905ded0","b680e76d","47624288"],"title":"Two-Way Tables"},{"notes":[{"title":"Conditional Probability","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“Given that”, “of those who”, “if a ___ is selected from the ___” – these <strong>shrink the total</strong> down to just that group."},{"sub":false,"html":"The denominator is the group they name, not the whole table."}]}],"qids":["0301c5dc","e1ad3d41","912cd125","d4413871","5dc386fb","89f20d9e"],"title":"Conditional Probability"}]},{"id":"m5.7","num":"5.7","title":"Samples, Margin of Error, and Study Design","calc":false,"parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"“In a study, the data from a random sample of a population had a mean of 37, with an associated margin of error of 3. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?”"},{"sub":false,"html":"Almost none of these need a calculator. They’re asking what we are allowed to <strong>conclude</strong> – just like interpretation in 2.4."}]},{"title":"They are asking what we are ALLOWED to say","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Almost no calculation. The question is which conclusion the study design supports."},{"sub":false,"html":"They will ask for one of:"},{"sub":true,"html":"– an <strong>estimate</strong> for the whole population"},{"sub":true,"html":"– a <strong>plausible range</strong> – the estimate either side of the margin of error"},{"sub":true,"html":"– which <strong>conclusion</strong> is appropriate"},{"sub":true,"html":"– the effect of a <strong>bigger sample</strong>"},{"sub":false,"html":"That means: find two words before reading the choices – <strong>random sample</strong> lets us generalize, <strong>random assignment</strong> lets us say caused. Without the first we can say nothing; without the second, only that two things are associated."}]},{"title":"Using a Sample, and the Margin of Error","lead":"","formula":"estimate = (fraction in the sample) × (size of the population)","listhead":"A random sample stands in for the whole population – the same fraction in the sample means the same fraction in the population.","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"Estimate ± margin of error is the range of <strong>plausible</strong> values for the population. A mean of 37 with a margin of error of 3 means 34 to 40 is plausible – not certain, and the true value can still sit outside it."},{"sub":false,"html":"A <strong>bigger sample</strong> gives a <strong>smaller</strong> margin of error."}]}],"qids":["e7d9649f","97631565","1e562f24","6a305cd0","4096a482"],"title":"Using a Sample, and the Margin of Error"},{"notes":[{"title":"What We Can Conclude","lead":"","formula":"","listhead":"Everything here turns on two words – random <strong>sample</strong> and random <strong>assignment</strong>:","rows":[{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Random sample</strong> → we can generalize, but only to the population it was drawn from."},{"sub":false,"html":"<strong>Random assignment</strong> to groups (an experiment) → we can say the treatment <strong>caused</strong> the difference. No random assignment, just observing → <strong>association only</strong>."},{"sub":false,"html":"Volunteers, or whoever was convenient – people at a playground, viewers who choose to answer a poll → the sample is biased, so we cannot generalize at all."}]}],"qids":["9bf4c545","82dfb646","37930b2a"],"title":"What We Can Conclude"}]}]}]},{"id":"english","title":"Reading & Writing","bank":"rw","domains":[{"name":"Introduction","lessons":[{"id":"e1.1","num":"1.1","title":"SAT Reading and Writing: Logistics, and General Approach","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What does the Reading and Writing Section even look like?","html":"<ul><li>Two modules, each 27 questions at 32 minutes (~71 seconds per question).</li><li>The second module is adaptive, meaning: you will either be given an “easy” or “hard” second module based on your performance in the first. A more difficult second module means that you did well in the first module.</li><li>Every question is its own short passage (usually 25 to 150 words) with exactly one question attached. There are no long passages with ten questions like the old SAT.</li><li>Questions of the same type are grouped together, and each group runs roughly from easy to hard. The groups always come in the same order:</li><li class=\"sub\">Craft and Structure (vocabulary, purpose and structure, two-passage questions)</li><li class=\"sub\">Information and Ideas (main idea, evidence, tables, inferences)</li><li class=\"sub\">Standard English Conventions (punctuation and grammar)</li><li class=\"sub\">Expression of Ideas (transitions, then the notes questions at the very end)</li></ul>"},{"title":"Key Topics Covered","html":"<ul><li><b>Standard English Conventions</b> ~ 26% of questions</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Boundaries</b>: punctuation. Where does one clause stop and the next one start, and what mark goes there?</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Form, Structure, and Sense</b>: verbs, pronouns, possessives, and modifiers.</li><li><b>Expression of Ideas</b> ~ 20% of questions</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Transitions</b>: the relationship between two sentences.</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Rhetorical Synthesis</b>: use a student’s notes to accomplish a stated goal.</li><li><b>Information and Ideas</b> ~ 26% of questions</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Central Ideas and Details</b>: what the text says.</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Command of Evidence</b>: what proves (or weakens) a claim, from a quotation, a finding, or a table.</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Inferences</b>: what logically follows from the text.</li><li><b>Craft and Structure</b> ~ 28% of questions</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Words in Context</b>: what a word means here.</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Text Structure and Purpose</b>: why the author wrote it, and what a sentence is doing.</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Cross-Text Connections</b>: how two authors would respond to each other.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Accuracy matters far more than speed","html":"<ul><li>Even a 770-scoring, professional SAT tutor still runs out of time on the hardest question in Reading and Writing. The test is not designed for every question to be answerable.</li><li>If you try to answer every question without the skill to do it properly, you shortcut your reading and fall for traps you’d otherwise catch.</li><li>College Board has spent decades engineering wrong answers that look right to someone who skimmed. The hard questions are where this bites hardest.</li><li>Give yourself permission to skip the questions that take the longest, and spend that time getting everything else 100% right instead.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Skipping the hardest questions makes the whole test easier","html":"<ul><li>Removing the longest-taking questions removes the entire reason the module felt hard.</li><li>What’s usually left after skipping: grammar, transitions, notes questions, and vocab questions. All short and fast.</li><li>You get back real, meaningful time (often 10+ minutes per module) to spend making sure every remaining question is airtight.</li><li>Worst case, you guess on the questions you skipped. A 25% shot is still better than the sub-25% you’d get by rushing them anyway.</li><li>At every moment, work on whichever remaining question takes the least time. Never sink 10 minutes into one question.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The order to work a module","html":"<figure><img src=\"curriculum-assets/module_order.png\" alt=\"Work a module starting at the first grammar question (#14–17) through #27, then go back to #1 for the reading questions.\"><figcaption>Work a module starting at the first grammar question (#14–17) through #27, then go back to #1 for the reading questions.</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>First: start at the first grammar question</b>, usually around #14–17, and work forward to #27. Grammar is about a quarter of the module and the quickest, most learnable points on the test. Bank them while your time and focus are fresh.</li><li>The last stretch (transitions and the bullet-point notes questions) is medium difficulty and short, so finish it on the same pass.</li><li><b>Second: go back to #1</b> for the vocab and reading comprehension questions. They start medium and get really hard as you approach the grammar, so the hardest questions in the module are the ones you reach last. Those are exactly the questions the skip chart below tells you to leave.</li><li>To jump ahead, open the question navigator at the bottom of the screen and look for the first question that asks “Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?”</li></ul>"},{"title":"How many questions to leave blank","html":"<p>A starting guide. Adjust up or down based on how much time you actually have left.</p><table><thead><tr><th>R&amp;W Score</th><th>Skip in Module 1</th><th>Skip in Module 2 (hard)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>520 or below</td><td>5</td><td>9 (1 if Module 2 is easy)</td></tr><tr><td>530–550</td><td>4</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>560–580</td><td>3</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>590–620</td><td>2</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>630–640</td><td>1</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>650–660</td><td>1</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>670–690</td><td>0</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>700–730</td><td>0</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>740+</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Never try this strategy for the first time on an official test. Run it on at least one full practice test first, and be ready to skip a question the moment it’s taking too long.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Every word in the right answer is in the text","html":"<ul><li>There’s always exactly one defensible right answer. Use that to double-check yourself.</li><li>Read every answer choice, not just the one you like. Eliminate a choice the instant one word isn’t supported by the passage.</li><li>For your final pick, confirm every single word is backed by the text. Not just the general idea: the specific wording.</li><li>Remember: <b>to get a question wrong, you have to be wrong twice.</b> You wrongly eliminated the right answer AND wrongly accepted a wrong one. That’s hard to do if you’re careful.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Three things to keep in mind","html":"<ul><li><b>Don’t feel relaxed.</b> Extra time back is a reward for effort on the questions you DO answer, not a reason to coast. Stay locked in on every word.</li><li><b>Skip fast, not eventually.</b> The moment a question stalls, flag it and move on.</li><li><b>Never debut a new strategy on test day.</b> Run this system on at least one full practice test first so it feels automatic before it counts.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Why I have Designed this Curriculum this Way","html":"<ul><li>We start with <b>Standard English Conventions</b>: the most approachable content on the test. These are rules. Learn a rule once and it works every single time, which makes these the fastest points to lock in.</li><li>Then <b>Transitions and Rhetorical Synthesis</b>: short questions that reward the same careful reading, but with a clear system for each.</li><li>Then the reading: <b>Information and Ideas</b> first, because that is where we build the reading process every other reading question uses, then <b>Craft and Structure</b>.</li><li>Everything is taught the same way: learn the rule, see exactly where it comes from, then work real College Board questions under the section that uses it, easy to hard.</li><li><b>Talk through the process out loud.</b> Name each step as you apply it: that’s what turns knowledge into speed. Each system ends with a checklist; if you can’t recite it from memory, re-read the section first.</li></ul><p>## STANDARD ENGLISH CONVENTIONS</p>"}],"qids":["8a8236e1"],"title":"Every word in the right answer is in the text"}]}]},{"name":"Standard English Conventions","lessons":[{"id":"e2.1","num":"2.1","title":"The Foundation: Subjects, Main Verbs, and Independent vs. Dependent Clauses","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"Why this comes first","html":"<ul><li>Almost every punctuation question and a big chunk of verb questions are secretly asking the same thing: <b>where does each clause start and end, and does it have its main verb?</b></li><li>If you can pick out the subject, the main verb, and the extra information in any sentence, most of the grammar section becomes mechanical. Everything in 2.2 through 2.8 is built on this lesson.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Look at the answer choices first","html":"<p>The answer choices tell you what is being tested before you read a single word of the passage:</p><ul><li><b>The choices differ in punctuation</b> (a comma in A and B, a semicolon in C, nothing in D): it’s a <b>punctuation</b> question (2.2–2.5).</li><li><b>The choices are all forms of the same verb</b> (refer / referred / referring / to refer): it’s a <b>verb</b> question (2.6–2.8). Adverbs next to the verb don’t change that.</li><li>If the choices differ in punctuation AND in the verb, treat it as punctuation first. Only fall back to verb logic as a tiebreaker.</li><li><b>The choices are pronouns</b> (it / they / its / their) or <b>apostrophes</b> (dog’s / dogs’ / dogs): 2.9.</li><li><b>The choices are the same words rearranged into long clauses</b>: a <b>modifier</b> question (2.10).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Words we’ll use","html":"<p>This is the only grammar vocabulary you need for the whole section. Every rule after this uses these words.</p><ul><li><b>Clause</b>: a group of words with its own subject and verb. “the dog barked” is a clause.</li><li><b>Phrase</b>: a group of words WITHOUT its own subject + main verb pair. “across the river,” “running through the forest,” and “a former astronaut” are phrases.</li><li><b>Subject</b>: who or what does the action. <b>Verb</b>: the action (or a state: is, seems, remains).</li><li><b>Object</b>: what receives the action. In “She wrote a novel,” <i>a novel</i> is the object of <i>wrote</i>.</li><li><b>Preposition</b>: a small linking word that needs an object after it: of, in, to, by, during, for, with, from, about. “during <i>the storm</i>,” “of <i>the elements</i>.”</li><li><b>Additional information</b> (College Board’s term is a <b>non-essential</b> or <b>supplementary</b> element): any phrase or clause you could delete and the sentence would still be complete and mean the same thing.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The Subject","html":"<ul><li>The subject is who or what the sentence is about: the thing doing the main action.</li><li>The subject is always at the start of the sentence, <b>once you strip out additional information</b> (an opening phrase like “In 2019,” or “Although the museum was small,”).</li><li>The subject doesn’t have to be one word. It can be a whole idea:</li><li class=\"sub\">An <b>-ing</b> phrase: <i>Determining whether the ancient manuscript was authentic</i> required extensive analysis.</li><li class=\"sub\">A <b>“that”</b> clause: <i>That the ancient ruins held secrets lost to time</i> fascinated archaeologists for centuries.</li><li class=\"sub\">A <b>“whether”</b> clause: <i>Whether these vocalizations should be considered names</i> remains controversial.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The Main Verb","html":"<ul><li>Every clause has <b>exactly one main verb</b>: the main action the subject performs. The main verb sits right where the subject ends.</li><li>Two forms are <b>always non-main verbs</b>:</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>-ing</b> verbs (running, developing)</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>to + verb</b> (to run, to develop)</li><li>Two places <b>hide non-main verbs</b>:</li><li class=\"sub\">A verb <b>inside the subject</b> itself: “Determining whether X <i>was</i> true <i>required</i> practice.” <i>Was</i> is inside the subject; <i>required</i> is the main verb.</li><li class=\"sub\">A verb <b>inside additional information</b>: “The boy <i>who ran across the street</i> fell.” <i>Ran</i> is extra information about the boy; <i>fell</i> is the main verb.</li></ul><blockquote><i>I jumped across the river, running through the forest.</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>Whether you paid attention in math class or not is irrelevant when you have the Desmos calculator.</i></blockquote><ul><li>In the first sentence, <i>jumped</i> is the main verb. <i>Running</i> is additional information about how you jumped, not the main action.</li><li>In the second, <i>is</i> is the main verb. <i>Paid</i> is inside the subject, and <i>have</i> is inside additional information.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Independent Clauses","html":"<p>An independent clause is a complete sentence on its own. It needs three things, and “does it sound complete?” will fool you on the SAT:</p><ol><li><b>A subject</b>, always at the beginning.</li><li><b>Exactly one main verb</b>, right after the subject. It can’t be an -ing form, a to- form, or a verb buried inside the subject or extra information.</li><li><b>It can’t start with a dependent-clause starter.</b></li></ol><ul><li>About 80% of students misjudge the “whole idea” subjects above on the first try. “That the ancient ruins held secrets lost to time fascinated archaeologists” doesn’t <i>sound</i> like it starts with a subject, but it does: the subject is the whole idea, and the main verb is <i>fascinated</i>.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ The researchers tested the samples.   (subject + main verb: independent)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ Testing the samples in the lab.   (no subject, and an -ing word is never a main verb)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ Because the researchers tested the samples.   (subject + verb, but “because” makes it dependent)</i></blockquote><ul><li>Only the first can stand alone as a sentence. The second is a phrase. The third is a dependent clause: it has a subject and a verb, but it leans on another clause to finish the thought.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Dependent Clauses","html":"<ul><li>A dependent clause has a subject and a verb, but it starts with a word that makes it lean on something else. It can <b>never</b> stand alone as a sentence.</li><li>Dependent-clause starters (remember <b>WASABI</b>: <b>W</b>hile, <b>A</b>lthough, <b>S</b>ince, <b>A</b>s, <b>B</b>ecause, <b>I</b>f), plus:</li><li class=\"sub\">when, whenever, whereas, unless, until, though, even though, after, before, once</li><li class=\"sub\">who, which, whose, where (these start additional information about a noun)</li></ul><blockquote><i>Although the museum was small, it drew thousands of visitors every year.</i></blockquote><ul><li><i>Although the museum was small</i> is dependent: it can’t stand alone.</li><li><i>It drew thousands of visitors every year</i> is independent: subject (it) + main verb (drew).</li><li><b>When the dependent clause comes first, a comma separates it from the independent clause.</b> One comma, right where it meets the rest of the sentence. Not a period, not a semicolon: a dependent clause can’t sit on its own side of either.</li><li><b>When the dependent clause comes after the independent clause, it usually takes no punctuation at all</b>: “The store closes early whenever it snows.” The exception is a contrast word like <i>while</i> or <i>whereas</i>, which takes a comma.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ Although the museum was small, it drew thousands of visitors every year.</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ Although the museum was small. It drew thousands of visitors every year.   (the dependent clause can’t stand alone)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The store closes early whenever it snows.   (dependent clause after: no comma)</i></blockquote>"},{"title":"Rule #1 of punctuation: nothing splits a clause’s core","html":"<ul><li><b>Never put punctuation between a subject and its main verb</b>, no matter how long the subject gets.</li><li>The longer the subject, the more tempting the comma. “The long line of fans waiting outside the theater stretched around the block.” The subject is nine words long, but <i>line</i> is still glued to <i>stretched</i>.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✗ The long line of fans waiting outside the theater, stretched around the block.   (comma between subject and verb)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The long line of fans waiting outside the theater stretched around the block.</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["de55ec71","a2293b3f","83c9c503","403d7bb5","f868d438"],"title":"Rule #1 of punctuation: nothing splits a clause’s core"},{"notes":[{"title":"Nothing splits a verb from its object either","html":"<ul><li><b>Never put punctuation between a verb and its object</b>, or between a preposition (of, to, during, by) and its object.</li><li>Same move as above: find the core, then refuse to break it. A run of prepositions is the usual trap — <i>collected by solar panels during the day</i> is one unbroken stretch, however long it gets.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✗ The museum displays, paintings from the 1700s.   (comma between verb and object)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ The meeting took place during, the storm.   (comma between preposition and object)</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["261f4ca6","6fdddabf","ffea47e1","b1e8b87f","6ea8c23f"],"title":"Nothing splits a verb from its object either"},{"notes":[{"title":"Anything in front of the main clause takes a comma","html":"<ul><li>A <b>dependent clause or an opening phrase in front</b> of the main clause is closed with a comma: <i>Although the museum was small, it drew thousands of visitors.</i></li><li><b>Flip the order and the comma goes away.</b> A dependent clause that comes AFTER the main clause usually takes nothing: <i>It drew thousands of visitors although the museum was small.</i></li><li>The comma goes at the <b>join</b>, never inside the opener. <i>Powered with energy collected by solar panels during the day,</i> is one stretch with one comma at the end — Rule #1 still holds inside it.</li><li>This is where WASABI earns its keep: spot the starter, find where its clause ends, and put the comma exactly there.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Comma jumping","html":"<ul><li>This is how you read a punctuation question without translating the whole sentence: jump from comma to comma and <b>label each stretch</b> “subject,” “main verb,” “dependent clause,” or “additional information.”</li><li>You don’t need to understand the science in the sentence. You need to know which stretch is the core and which stretches are extra.</li><li>Confirm the full structure before locking in: make sure you understand why every comma in the sentence is where it is, not just the one at the blank.</li></ul><p>Worked example:</p><blockquote><i>In 2019, | the museum’s director, | who had worked there for decades, | announced | a new exhibit.</i></blockquote><ul><li>“In 2019,” = additional information (an opener). “the museum’s director” = subject. “who had worked there for decades,” = additional information about the director. “announced” = main verb. “a new exhibit” = object.</li><li>Now every comma has a reason: one closes the opener, and two sandwich the extra information. There is no comma between <i>announced</i> and <i>a new exhibit</i>, because nothing splits a verb from its object.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["b35cefb7","26c8c88c","d75d57a0","333b2b65","a427a52c","cdbbbf94"],"title":"Anything in front of the main clause takes a comma"}]},{"id":"e2.2","num":"2.2","title":"Punctuation I: Joining Two Independent Clauses","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"Every punctuation mark the SAT tests","html":"<p>Framed as: what has to be in the passage for this mark to be correct? This is the reference table for every punctuation question in 2.2 through 2.5.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Mark</th><th>What makes it correct</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Colon ( : )</td><td>Independent clause before it + an expanding thought (its own idea) after it.</td></tr><tr><td>Period ( . )</td><td>Independent clause before AND independent clause after.</td></tr><tr><td>Semicolon ( ; )</td><td>Either separates items in a semicolon list, OR links independent clause + independent clause.</td></tr><tr><td>Comma + FANBOYS</td><td>Independent clause before AND after: functionally identical to a period.</td></tr><tr><td>Isolated comma ( , )</td><td>A comma list, or attaching a non-essential element (or a dependent clause that comes first).</td></tr><tr><td>Dash ( — )</td><td>Attaches a non-essential element (needs an opening AND closing dash), or replaces a colon.</td></tr><tr><td>No punctuation</td><td>Nothing needs to be set off: inside a clause’s core (Rule #1), before “that,” between a title and a name.</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"title":"Every punctuation mark, ranked","html":"<p>The table above says what makes each mark correct. This one says how often College Board actually uses it. Counted across 253 of their Boundaries questions, every answer choice classified.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Appears as a choice</th><th>Right when it appears</th><th>Share of all answers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No punctuation</td><td>31%</td><td>25%</td><td><b>32%</b></td></tr><tr><td>Comma alone</td><td><b>30%</b></td><td><b>17%</b></td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>Semicolon</td><td>14%</td><td>30%</td><td>17%</td></tr><tr><td>Colon</td><td>10%</td><td>22%</td><td>9%</td></tr><tr><td>Period</td><td>7%</td><td><b>43%</b></td><td>13%</td></tr><tr><td>Dash</td><td>4%</td><td>23%</td><td>4%</td></tr><tr><td>Comma + and/but</td><td>4%</td><td><b>45%</b></td><td>7%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Three things fall out of it:</p><ul><li><b>A bare comma is the single biggest trap on this skill.</b> It is offered in 90% of questions</li></ul><p>and is the answer in 22% of them. Never pick it because it feels natural; pick it only because the rule says so.</p><ul><li><b>No punctuation is the most common answer there is</b> — 32% of them. If nothing in the</li></ul><p>sentence demands a mark, the answer is usually nothing.</p><ul><li><b>A period or a comma + and/but is rare but reliable.</b> Together they are 11% of the choices</li></ul><p>offered and right about 44% of the time, nearly double the 25% you would get by guessing. When one appears, read it properly before you move on.</p>"},{"title":"What we’re looking for","html":"<ul><li>Two independent clauses (2.1) sitting next to each other. The question is what goes between them.</li><li>The first job: find <b>where the second clause starts</b>. On hard questions, that is the whole question.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The process","html":"<ol><li><b>Check the choices.</b> They differ only in punctuation, so this is a boundary question and nothing else matters.</li><li><b>Find where the second clause starts.</b> On hard questions this IS the question. Read past the blank, find the second main verb, then walk back to where its clause begins.</li><li><b>Test both sides.</b> Does each one have a subject and its own main verb, and not start with a dependent word (WASABI)?</li><li><b>Both independent?</b> You need a period, a linking semicolon, or comma + and/but. If two of those are offered as separate choices, neither is right — take the one that is left.</li><li><b>Not both independent?</b> Then it is a comma or nothing. Default to <b>nothing</b> unless a rule in 2.3 or 2.4 demands the comma.</li></ol>"},{"title":"The Period ( . )","html":"<ul><li>Independent clause before it. Independent clause after it.</li><li>That’s the entire rule. Nothing more to check.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ The storm knocked out the power. Crews worked through the night to restore it.</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["04bfd364","7b950fc2"],"title":"The Period ( . )"},{"notes":[{"title":"The Semicolon ( ; ) as a linker","html":"<ul><li>Same rule as a period: independent clause before AND after.</li><li>A semicolon has a second job (separating list items, 2.4). Check for a list first; only then treat it as a linker.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ The storm knocked out the power; crews worked through the night to restore it.</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ The storm knocked out the power; working through the night to restore it.   (the second side has no subject + main verb)</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["a421f749","89fbc3eb","2c9c6ca9"],"title":"The Semicolon ( ; ) as a linker"},{"notes":[{"title":"Comma + FANBOYS","html":"<ul><li>FANBOYS = <b>f</b>or, <b>a</b>nd, <b>n</b>or, <b>b</b>ut, <b>o</b>r, <b>y</b>et. On official SAT questions, only <b>and</b> and <b>but</b> actually show up joining clauses.</li><li>Treat “comma + and/but” as ONE punctuation mark that behaves exactly like a period: independent clause before and after.</li><li>Comma placement still matters: the comma sits right before the FANBOYS word.</li><li><b>“And” joining two things that are NOT both independent clauses takes no comma</b>: two verbs (“she wrote the songs and sang them”), two objects (“the team measured how fast the ice melted and where the water went”). Check that BOTH sides could stand alone before adding a comma.</li></ul><blockquote><i>Leibniz wheel calculators were popular in the first half of the twentieth century, but these ingenious devices were eventually replaced by electronic calculators.</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["148be4da","70ced8dc","7ce4ee13"],"title":"Comma + FANBOYS"},{"notes":[{"title":"What can NOT join two independent clauses","html":"<ul><li><b>A comma alone.</b> Two complete sentences glued with a comma is a comma splice, and it is always wrong.</li><li><b>Nothing at all.</b> Two complete sentences with no punctuation is a run-on.</li><li><b>FANBOYS without the comma</b> (“...century but these devices...”) is wrong on the SAT.</li><li>A transition word (however, therefore, even so) does NOT join clauses. “The results were mixed, even so, the team continued” is still a comma splice. It needs a period or semicolon before it: “The results were mixed; even so, the team continued.”</li></ul><blockquote><i>✗ The storm knocked out the power, crews worked through the night.   (comma splice)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ The storm knocked out the power crews worked through the night.   (run-on)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ The storm knocked out the power but crews worked through the night.   (FANBOYS with no comma)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The storm knocked out the power, but crews worked through the night.</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The storm knocked out the power; however, crews worked through the night.</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["a5079e0d","a9e5b788"],"title":"What can NOT join two independent clauses"},{"notes":[{"title":"The interchangeability trick","html":"<ul><li>Period, linking semicolon, and comma + and/but all do the <b>same job</b>. There can only be one right answer.</li><li>So if two of them show up as separate choices in the same spot (a period in B, a semicolon in C), <b>neither is right</b>. College Board’s own questions: when a period and a semicolon are both offered, neither is the answer 31 times out of 36. The exceptions all involve semicolon lists.</li><li>If all three show up, the 4th choice wins.</li></ul><p>Example: four choices at the same blank.</p><blockquote><i>Crews worked through the ______ power by morning.</i></blockquote><ul><li>A) night. Restoring   B) night; restoring   C) night, and restoring   D) night, restoring</li><li>A, B, and C all need a complete clause after the mark, and “restoring power by morning” has no subject. They fail together, which is exactly what the trick predicts. D attaches the -ing phrase as additional information with a comma. <b>D.</b></li></ul>"},{"title":"Hard questions hide where the second clause starts","html":"<ul><li>On the hardest questions, the second sentence doesn’t start with its subject. It starts with an opening phrase, so it doesn’t <i>look</i> like a new sentence: “<b>By dissolving</b> the silk...”, “<b>Built into</b> the rocky outcrops...”, “<b>Translated as</b> ‘listen’...”, “<b>Published</b> from 1866 to 1899...”</li><li>Read PAST the blank. Find the second main verb. Then walk back to where its clause begins: that is where the period goes.</li><li>On hard single-blank questions, if exactly one choice can join two independent clauses (the only period, or the only semicolon), it is almost always the answer. On College Board’s hard questions of this kind, it was the answer 27 times out of 27.</li></ul><blockquote><i>The team published its results in March ______ comparing thousands of samples, the researchers showed that the disease spread through the air.</i></blockquote><ul><li>Read past the blank: the second main verb is <i>showed</i>, and its subject is <i>the researchers</i>. Walk back to where that clause begins: “By comparing thousands of samples,” is its opener. So the period goes before “By”: “...in March. By comparing thousands of samples, the researchers showed...”</li></ul>"}],"qids":["267a13e2","886dc9f9","940ff6f7","7e37affc"],"title":"Hard questions hide where the second clause starts"}]},{"id":"e2.3","num":"2.3","title":"Punctuation II: Additional Information — Commas, Dashes, and When to Use Nothing","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What a non-essential element is","html":"<ul><li>A non-essential element is just <b>additional information</b>: a phrase or clause you could delete and the sentence would still make complete sense.</li><li>Wherever a non-essential element touches the rest of the sentence, <b>put a comma there</b>. This is the actual reason almost every isolated comma exists on the test.</li></ul><blockquote><i>The bridge, which opened in 1937, is painted orange.</i></blockquote><ul><li>Delete “which opened in 1937” and you still have a complete sentence that means the same thing: “The bridge is painted orange.” So it is additional information, and a comma sits wherever it touches the sentence.</li></ul>"},{"title":"How to prove it’s non-essential","html":"<ol><li><b>Try deleting it</b> from the sentence. If the sentence still makes complete sense without it, it’s non-essential.</li><li><b>Confirm it’s genuinely “extra” information</b>, not the core subject or main verb of the sentence.</li><li><b>Check for the word “that.”</b> Never put a comma directly before “that.” It introduces essential information.</li></ol><ul><li>Common non-essential starters: <b>which</b>, <b>who</b>, <b>-ing</b> phrases, <b>-ed</b> phrases (“Founded in 1901, ...”), “according to X,” and transition words (however, therefore).</li></ul>"},{"title":"The comma sandwich: beginning, middle, end, nested","html":"<ul><li><b>At the start</b>: “Although usually overlooked by students, the strategy proved effective.” One comma, right where it meets the sentence.</li><li><b>In the middle</b>: “The strategy, made by James, proved effective.” A comma on both sides: a full comma sandwich.</li><li><b>At the end</b>: “The strategy proved effective, surprising the teachers who doubted it.” One comma before it.</li><li><b>Nested</b>: a non-essential element can sit inside another one. Each layer gets its own comma pair.</li><li>The most common mistake: opening the sandwich and forgetting to close it. If a comma opened the extra information, a comma has to close it before the main verb.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✗ The bridge, which opened in 1937 is painted orange.   (the sandwich was opened but never closed)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The bridge, which opened in 1937, is painted orange.</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["ea8f4658","b0a525be","60713427","ac5536c1","5cc85f01","7f48b098","e67f9967"],"title":"The comma sandwich: beginning, middle, end, nested"},{"notes":[{"title":"Dashes and parentheses: match the opener","html":"<ul><li>A dash pair does exactly what a comma pair does: it sets off non-essential information. It needs an <b>opening dash AND a closing dash</b>.</li><li>Whatever opens the extra information has to close it. Dash opens, dash closes. Parenthesis opens, parenthesis closes. <b>Never mix</b> a dash with a comma.</li><li><b>See one dash already in the passage? The answer choice with a dash is very likely correct.</b> On College Board’s questions, the dash was the answer 11 times out of 14 when there was already an unpaired dash in the passage. Just confirm there’s real extra information between them.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ The bridge—which opened in 1937—is painted orange.</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ The bridge—which opened in 1937, is painted orange.   (opened with a dash, closed with a comma)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The bridge (which opened in 1937) is painted orange.</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["f2eaaf5d","40c3589d","7f226b4b","626a1642"],"title":"Dashes and parentheses: match the opener"},{"notes":[{"title":"Essential information: no commas","html":"<ul><li>Some information looks extra but isn’t: take it out and the sentence stops identifying <i>which</i> thing it means. That information is <b>essential</b>, and it takes <b>no commas</b>.</li><li><b>“That”</b> always introduces essential information: “a company that builds electric buses...” No comma before “that,” ever.</li><li>“Which” (with a comma) is non-essential; “that” (no comma) is essential.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ The paintings that hang in the lobby are copies.   (tells you WHICH paintings: essential)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ The paintings, that hang in the lobby, are copies.</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The paintings, which were donated in 1990, are copies.   (all of the paintings; the date is extra)</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["cabe71d4","d198997b","5ef9fc48"],"title":"Essential information: no commas"},{"notes":[{"title":"Title + Name: no punctuation between them","html":"<ul><li>A “title” (the category something falls under) directly followed by its “name” takes <b>ZERO punctuation</b> between them: “astronaut Buzz Aldrin,” “chemistry professor Yugang Sun,” “the chemical compound aluminum oxide.”</li><li>Read a few words ahead to confirm the title and name are in the same phrase before applying this rule.</li><li>Compare: “Buzz Aldrin, a former astronaut, ...” Here “a former astronaut” is genuinely deletable information, so it gets a comma pair.</li><li>There CAN still be a comma nearby for an unrelated reason: a non-essential element elsewhere in the sentence. Read around it to be sure.</li><li>Rare exception: if the title + name is the subject of an independent clause followed by a colon-style expansion, punctuation can appear after the name. Always sanity-check by reading ahead.</li><li>Heads up: these look easy but College Board rates almost all of them hard (7 of 8). The trap is the comma that looks like it belongs.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ The novel was written by author Toni Morrison.</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ The novel was written by author, Toni Morrison.</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The novel was written by Toni Morrison, an American author.   (now “an American author” is deletable extra information)</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["594b4a94","960dec02","2fd05c15","80aa7690","59a246dc"],"title":"Title + Name: no punctuation between them"}]},{"id":"e2.4","num":"2.4","title":"Punctuation III: Colons, Lists, and Question Marks","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"Comma lists","html":"<ul><li>A list of MORE than two items needs commas between all of them: “apples, pears, and plums.”</li><li>Two items need no comma at all: “yellow and red.”</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ The market sells apples, pears, and plums.</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ The market sells apples, and pears.   (only two items: no comma)</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["a7fdf862","577b09fa"],"title":"Comma lists"},{"notes":[{"title":"Semicolon lists","html":"<ul><li>When at least one list item <b>already contains a comma</b>, the items are separated by semicolons instead: “Paris, France; Lima, Peru; and Accra, Ghana.”</li><li><b>The tell</b>: multiple semicolons already in the sentence, or a semicolon right next to the blank’s neighbors.</li><li>The test is really the <b>placement</b>: where does each list item begin and end? Read the last item in the list first. It reveals the structure every other item must match.</li><li>Always check for a semicolon list BEFORE assuming a semicolon is linking two clauses.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ The tour stops in Paris, France; Lima, Peru; and Accra, Ghana.</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ The tour stops in Paris, France, Lima, Peru, and Accra, Ghana.   (you can’t tell where one item ends and the next begins)</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["e2f77ae7","a8fa749a","c04e9136"],"title":"Semicolon lists"},{"notes":[{"title":"The Colon ( : )","html":"<ul><li><b>An independent clause immediately before the colon.</b> Check this first. “The recipe calls for: flour, eggs, and milk” is wrong because “The recipe calls for” isn’t a complete clause.</li><li><b>After the colon: its own expanding thought.</b> An explanation, a list, an example, or a clarification of what came before.</li><li>The colon is not really about “introducing a list.” That idea gets students the wrong answer. What matters is the complete clause in front of it.</li><li>In the hard second module, a colon answer choice is correct a very high share of the time. On College Board’s hard questions that offered a colon, it was the answer 10 times out of 22. On easy questions, it was the answer 0 times out of 21: there, the colon is bait.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ The recipe needs three things: flour, eggs, and milk.</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ The recipe needs: flour, eggs, and milk.   (“The recipe needs” is not a complete clause)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The results were clear: the new drug worked.   (an explanation after a complete clause)</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["c3397d25","da53d726","fba5d8d1","bb4557cf","8772475b"],"title":"The Colon ( : )"},{"notes":[{"title":"The dash as a colon","html":"<ul><li>A single dash can do everything a colon can: independent clause before, expanding thought after.</li><li>This shows up IN passages far more than as a correct answer choice. Know it so you’re not confused when you see it.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ The results were clear—the new drug worked.</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["92f309b2"],"title":"The dash as a colon"},{"notes":[{"title":"Question marks","html":"<ul><li>Check whether the sentence begins the way you’d normally open a question: who, what, where, when, why, how, do, does, is, can, could...</li><li>If it does, it needs a question mark. If it doesn’t, it doesn’t. “Researchers investigated how people perceive acts of kindness.” is a statement <i>about</i> a question, so it ends with a period.</li><li>Exception: if there’s a colon in the sentence, the question begins right after the colon. Check what comes after it, not the very start of the sentence.</li><li>Question mark questions are always ranked easy by College Board (13 out of 13). Don’t overthink them.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ How do bees find their way home?</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ Scientists wanted to know how bees find their way home.   (a statement about a question: period)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ Scientists wanted to know how do bees find their way home?</i></blockquote>"},{"title":"The priority list: C-P-E-N","html":"<p>When you’re stuck between punctuation choices on a hard question, check them in this order:</p><ol><li><b>C: Colon.</b> Most likely correct when one appears in the hard module.</li><li><b>P: Period / Semicolon / Comma + FANBOYS.</b> All three behave identically. Check whichever appears.</li><li><b>E: Isolated comma</b> (non-<b>E</b>ssential element). Check for a non-essential element touching the comma.</li><li><b>N: No punctuation.</b> Correct when nothing needs to be set off (Rule #1 from 2.1).</li></ol><ul><li>This order is a hard-module tool. On easy questions, “no punctuation” is often the answer and the colon almost never is.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["9091458d","4b0c7b62","1f39ab8b"],"title":"Question marks"}]},{"id":"e2.5","num":"2.5","title":"Punctuation IV: Transition Placement and Multi-Blank Questions","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","html":"<ul><li>The hardest punctuation questions: the choices move a transition word (however, though, rather) and the punctuation around it.</li><li>A transition word describes the relationship between two <b>ADJACENT</b> sentences. Never sentences further apart.</li></ul>"},{"title":"How to place the transition","html":"<ol><li><b>Identify the relationship</b> the transition expresses. “However” and “though” contrast. “Rather” corrects. “For example” gives an instance.</li><li><b>Find which two adjacent clauses actually have that relationship.</b></li><li><b>The transition belongs to the second of those two clauses</b>, and the strong mark (period or semicolon) goes between them.</li></ol><ul><li>About 90% of the time, the transition lands <b>BEFORE</b> the strong punctuation mark, closing the first clause: “Okinaka doesn’t make such decisions single-handedly, <b>however;</b> all historical designations must be approved...” (On College Board’s questions: 11 of 12.)</li><li>The transition itself is additional information, so it gets a comma on its other side.</li><li>Watch <b>“though”</b> and <b>“although.”</b> They can act as a transition word (needs a comma) OR as a dependent-clause starter (no comma). Check whether there’s a comma before deciding.</li></ul><blockquote><i>The bakery is known for its homemade bread. It does not bake everything itself, though; it buys its croissants from a shop next door.</i></blockquote><ul><li>The contrast is between sentence 1 (known for homemade bread) and “it does not bake everything itself.” So <i>though</i> belongs to that clause and closes it, with the semicolon after it.</li><li>Putting it after the semicolon (“...itself; though, it buys...”) would make the croissant clause the contrast, and it isn’t: it just explains how.</li></ul><ul><li><b>If TWO choices both contain a semicolon, do not eliminate either one.</b> The semicolon is</li></ul><p>not what is being tested — where it sits relative to the transition word is. Across College Board's questions, when a semicolon appears in two different choices the answer contains a semicolon in <b>19 of 23</b> of them. Pick between them rather than looking elsewhere.</p>"}],"qids":["ad046778","548f4956","21e58a83","5cc69ee1","b771d175","790fc366","e3c1b4f7","c15069eb","2bb7416a"],"title":"How to place the transition"},{"notes":[{"title":"Two blanks at once","html":"<ul><li>Some choices change punctuation in two spots at once. These are almost always hard (27 of 37 on College Board’s questions).</li><li>Solve <b>one spot at a time</b>. Start with whichever spot you can settle with certainty, and cross out every choice that gets it wrong.</li><li>Then check the second spot only among the survivors.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The full process for every punctuation question","html":"<ol><li><b>Look at the answer choices before reading the passage.</b> They tell you exactly what you’re checking for.</li><li><b>Comma-jump through the sentence</b>, labeling each stretch: subject, main verb, dependent clause, additional information.</li><li><b>Find the clauses.</b> Is the blank inside one clause (Rule #1), between a dependent and an independent clause (comma), between two independent clauses (period / semicolon / comma + and / colon), or at the edge of extra information (match the opener)?</li><li><b>Confirm the full structure before locking in.</b> Know why every comma in the sentence is where it is.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Punctuation: the full checklist","html":"<ul><li>Independent clause = subject + one main verb + no dependent-clause starter.</li><li>Nothing between a subject and its verb, or a verb and its object.</li><li>Colon: independent clause before, expanding thought after.</li><li>Period: independent clause before AND after.</li><li>Semicolon: check for a list first, then independent-clause linking.</li><li>Comma + and/but behaves exactly like a period. Two of the three offered in one spot: neither is right.</li><li>Isolated comma: a comma list, a dependent clause that comes first, or a non-essential element.</li><li>Dash: non-essential sandwich (match the opener), or a colon replacement.</li><li>Never a comma before “that.”</li><li>Priority order on hard questions: Colon → Period / Semicolon / Comma + and → isolated comma → no punctuation.</li><li>Title + name: zero punctuation between them.</li><li>Question marks: check how the sentence normally opens.</li><li>Transitions: relationship between adjacent sentences; the strong mark usually comes after the transition; watch “though / although.”</li><li>Look at the answer choices first, then comma-jump the passage.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["5670a657","78b88c04","3a2d77d7"],"title":"Two blanks at once"}]},{"id":"e2.6","num":"2.6","title":"Verbs I: Does the Sentence Need a Main Verb?","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What counts as a verb question","html":"<ul><li>All four answer choices are different forms of the same verb (refer / referred / referring / to refer). Adverbs next to the verb are fine.</li><li>Verb questions are about a third of all grammar questions, and most of them solve almost instantly once you know the flowchart.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The verb flowchart","html":"<p>Checked top to bottom:</p><ol><li><b>Right after the blank, is there a “by,” “as,” or “to” without a verb directly after it?</b> Pick the -ed answer choice, as long as the sentence already has its main verb (see the last section of this lesson).</li><li><b>Is it one of the 3 answer choice shortcuts?</b> Three -ing/to forms (this lesson), 3-to-1 singular/plural (2.7), or “will” among the choices (2.8).</li><li><b>Read the sentence the blank is in. Is there already a main verb?</b> (Watch out for lists, and treat a semicolon, or a comma + and/but, as a period when finding “the sentence the blank is in.”) If yes: pick the non-main verb. Multiple non-main choices? Is the blank in a non-essential element? If yes, pick -ing. If no, pick “to ___.”</li><li><b>Is there a singular/plural version of the same word?</b> Figure out if the subject is singular or plural, and pick the matching answer (2.7).</li><li><b>Otherwise:</b> look for context clues to see if the answer is past, present, or future tense (2.8).</li></ol><ul><li>Heads up: two verb-question formats from the 2023 pre-digital SAT are still sitting in the College Board question bank. No official digital SAT has used them. If you see one while practicing in the question bank, skip it: it isn’t representative of the real test.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Shortcut: 3 ING/TO","html":"<ul><li>If three answer choices are <b>-ing forms or to-forms</b>, and one choice is neither: <b>pick the one that’s neither.</b></li><li>This does NOT reverse: it must be three ing/to forms and one other.</li><li>Why it works: -ing and to- forms are automatically non-main verbs. When three choices are non-main, College Board is testing whether the sentence needs a main verb, and it does. (On College Board’s questions: 19 out of 19.)</li></ul><blockquote><i>Every spring, the geese ______ north.     A) flying   B) to fly   C) having flown   D) fly</i></blockquote><ul><li>Three non-main forms (flying, to fly, having flown) and one that’s neither: <b>D</b>. The sentence had no main verb, so it needs one.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["7c48a6dd","983d33fa","29c9be28"],"title":"Shortcut: 3 ING/TO"},{"notes":[{"title":"Is there already a main verb?","html":"<ul><li><b>Yes, a main verb exists.</b> A clause can only have ONE main verb, so pick the answer choice that is NOT a main verb: an -ing or to- form.</li><li>Two non-main choices tied? If the blank sits inside a comma sandwich (non-essential element), pick the <b>-ing</b> form. If not, pick the <b>to-</b> form.</li><li>“The Olympic committee once decided <b>to include</b> tug-of-war...” <i>Decided</i> is the main verb, so the blank can’t be another one.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ The storm hit the coast, flooding hundreds of homes.   (-ing: extra information after a comma)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The city built a sea wall to protect the coast.   (to-: no comma sandwich, it gives a purpose)</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["e3b72630","50445680","7c766ceb"],"title":"Is there already a main verb?"},{"notes":[{"title":"Read past the blank","html":"<ul><li><b>No main verb before the blank doesn’t mean there’s no main verb.</b> On the hardest verb questions, the main verb comes AFTER the blank.</li><li>“The report <b>examining</b> twenty years of rainfall data... <b>shows</b>...” The subject is long, the blank is inside it, and the main verb <i>shows</i> is waiting at the end.</li><li>Before you pick a main verb, read the whole sentence and make sure there isn’t already one sitting after the blank.</li><li>The reverse trap: sometimes the blank IS the only place the main verb can go. “Her habit of practicing late at night <b>helped</b> her improve.”</li></ul>"}],"qids":["7f1df833","a30567fd","c9a677e9","61160f0a"],"title":"Read past the blank"},{"notes":[{"title":"By / As / To: the “-ed” pick","html":"<ul><li>Look at the two words right after the blank. If the first is <b>by</b>, <b>as</b>, or <b>to</b>, and the next word is not a verb, pick the <b>-ed</b> form.</li><li>Only look at the FIRST verb of each answer choice. Ignore adverbs (“highly prized”) and anything after it (“I tweet to” counts as one verb).</li><li>Why it works: the SAT is testing whether the -ed word works as a describing word (a participle). You don’t need to know that to use the shortcut.</li><li>“A style of music <b>influenced</b> by jazz...”</li><li><b>Careful: it isn’t 100%.</b> On College Board’s questions it worked 5 times out of 8. It fails when the blank is the sentence’s only main verb: “a bird that <b>goes</b> by two names,” “volunteers who <b>have contributed</b> to the project.” Before you use it, make sure the sentence already has its main verb.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["175df826","6d247c13","f40b447c","4f2ff5f2","c88ba1b7"],"title":"By / As / To: the “-ed” pick"}]},{"id":"e2.7","num":"2.7","title":"Verbs II: Subject–Verb Agreement","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"Verbs are the opposite of nouns","html":"<ul><li><b>Nouns</b>: adding an -s makes them plural (student → students).</li><li><b>Verbs</b>: adding an -s makes them singular (take → takes).</li><li>Test: “The students takes / take the SAT.” <i>Students</i> is plural, so pick the verb with no -s: <i>take</i>.</li><li>Watch out: words that already end in -s (focus) don’t follow the normal rule. <i>Focus</i> is plural, <i>focuses</i> is singular. This is rare, but it exists.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Singular</th><th>Plural</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>is</td><td>are</td></tr><tr><td>has</td><td>have</td></tr><tr><td>was</td><td>were</td></tr><tr><td>takes</td><td>take</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"title":"Shortcut: 3-to-1 Singular/Plural","html":"<ol><li><b>Look only at the first verb of every answer choice.</b> Ignore adverbs and any second verb.</li><li><b>Classify each as singular or plural.</b> This only works on plain present-tense verbs and is/are, has/have, was/were. Not on -ing, to-, or plain past-tense forms.</li><li><b>If it’s 3-to-1</b> (three plural and one singular, or vice versa), <b>the odd one out is always correct.</b></li></ol><ul><li>This is the biggest shortcut in the whole verb section: about a third of all verb questions, and on College Board’s questions it was right 48 times out of 48.</li><li>Fastest tell: two or more helping verbs (is/are, has/have, was/were) among the choices.</li></ul><blockquote><i>The collection of old maps ______ in the library’s basement.     A) are kept   B) is kept   C) were kept   D) have been kept</i></blockquote><ul><li><i>Are, were, have been</i> are plural; <i>is</i> is the only singular: <b>B</b>. Check it: the subject is <i>collection</i> (not <i>maps</i>), which is singular.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["77bf77cd","e62241b7","acf454af","d47bb0a4"],"title":"Shortcut: 3-to-1 Singular/Plural"},{"notes":[{"title":"When it isn’t 3-to-1: find the real subject","html":"<ul><li>Stop and ask <b>“what is actually doing the action?”</b> Don’t just grab the closest noun.</li><li>These traps only come up once you’ve fallen through all the instant shortcuts. Treat them as your last line of defense, not your default approach.</li><li>The hardest agreement questions put a noun of the <b>other</b> number between the subject and the verb: “the color of the <b>leaves</b> on these trees <b>changes</b> in the fall.” That in-between noun is the trap. With it, more than half of College Board’s agreement questions are hard; without it, almost none are.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Rule</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>“X and Y”</td><td>Plural, unless it’s “as well as / along with,” which stays singular.</td><td>The director and her assistant → plural (have)</td></tr><tr><td>“[thing] of [thing]”</td><td>Look at the word before “of,” not after it.</td><td>The color of the leaves → singular (changes), not leaves</td></tr><tr><td>“Each one” / “each ___”</td><td>Always singular.</td><td>Each of the paintings → singular (is)</td></tr><tr><td>Subject after the verb</td><td>Ask what’s really doing the action. It can follow the verb.</td><td>Along the coastline stand several ruins → plural (stand)</td></tr><tr><td>Additional information in between</td><td>Delete the comma sandwich, then match.</td><td>The bridge, which connects the two islands, was built in 1932</td></tr><tr><td>A whole idea as the subject</td><td>An -ing phrase or “that” clause is singular.</td><td>Studying these fossils is difficult</td></tr><tr><td>Collective nouns</td><td>The group itself is singular, no matter what’s inside it.</td><td>A flock of birds → singular (rare on the test)</td></tr></tbody></table>"}],"qids":["43b88827","28166dc6","75f49353","e44db0a0","c7cb5186","0fe5ce68","c91ef0f0","ae439895","8a9d2f4e","dc645172"],"title":"When it isn’t 3-to-1: find the real subject"}]},{"id":"e2.8","num":"2.8","title":"Verbs III: Tense","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","html":"<ul><li>The sentence has no main verb yet, the choices aren’t a singular/plural set, and they differ in time: past, present, or future.</li><li><b>See “will” in the answer choices? This is a tense question.</b> These almost always appear in the easier first module.</li><li><b>“Will” is almost never the answer.</b> On College Board’s questions, a “will” choice was correct 2 times out of 29. It’s furniture.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Read for the time clue","html":"<ul><li>There is always one decisive time clue, in the blank’s sentence or the one right before it.</li><li><b>A date or era in the past</b> (“In 1848,” “during the 1920s”) → simple past: <i>signed, grew</i>.</li><li><b>“Currently,” “today,” “now,” or a general fact</b> → present: <i>includes, is</i>.</li><li><b>“Since [date]”</b> or “for thousands of years” → present perfect (has/have + verb): <i>has grown, have lived</i>. It started in the past and is still going.</li><li><b>“By [a past date]”</b> → past perfect (had + verb): “By 1900, the city <b>had tripled</b> in size.”</li><li><b>Describing what happens in a book, poem, or film</b> → present tense, even if it was written long ago: “in the novel, the narrator <b>returns</b> to her hometown.”</li></ul>"}],"qids":["3580533b","035fd57e","2d6f8304","0ff8477b","265e7f4c","028752cd","67614549","1ee7b429"],"title":"Read for the time clue"},{"notes":[{"title":"“Had” = the past of the past","html":"<ul><li>Use “had” only when two events both happened in the past and you need to mark which one happened FIRST: “the telescope, which she <b>had built</b> two years earlier.”</li></ul>"}],"qids":["52b61716","674e59a3","588887b9","d46ac7e7"],"title":"“Had” = the past of the past"},{"notes":[{"title":"When “will” IS the answer","html":"<ul><li>Only when the time clue points to the future from the sentence’s point of view: a prediction, a plan, or “the results will be published next year.”</li><li>If there’s no future clue, cross out every “will” choice immediately.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Parallelism, rarely","html":"<ul><li>A nearby verb only sets the tense of the blank if it’s logically describing the same action. Don’t just copy the nearest verb’s tense blindly.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Verb questions: the full checklist","html":"<ul><li>Is it actually a verb question (no punctuation differences)?</li><li>By / as / to right after the blank, no verb following, and the sentence already has its main verb? Pick -ed.</li><li>Three -ing/to forms and one that isn’t? Pick the one that isn’t.</li><li>3-to-1 singular/plural among the choices? Pick the odd one out.</li><li>Does “will” appear? Read for the time clue (and “will” is almost never right).</li><li>No shortcut fits: find the sentence’s subject and main verb. Read past the blank.</li><li>Main verb already there? Pick the non-main verb (-ing in a comma sandwich, otherwise to-).</li><li>No main verb yet? Decide singular/plural vs. tense, then pick.</li><li>Watch for the subject traps (and / of / each / reversed order / extra information in between).</li><li>Use “had” only to mark which of two past events happened first.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["57998dd3","3daf126e"],"title":"When “will” IS the answer"}]},{"id":"e2.9","num":"2.9","title":"Pronouns and Possessives","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"Pronouns: find the true subject","html":"<ul><li>The answer is almost always a singular/plural pair. Match it to what the pronoun refers to.</li><li>Read the full passage before answering. These are short, and skimming risks missing the referent.</li><li>Find exactly what the pronoun refers back to, then check whether that thing is singular or plural.</li><li><b>The SAT is not “they-friendly.”</b> A single dog, person, company, or object must use it/its, not they/them, unless the referent is genuinely plural. (College Board: 12 out of 12.)</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Singular</th><th>Plural</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>it</td><td>they</td></tr><tr><td>its</td><td>their</td></tr><tr><td>it (that)</td><td>them</td></tr><tr><td>itself</td><td>themselves</td></tr><tr><td>this</td><td>these</td></tr><tr><td>that</td><td>those</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><i>✗ The company announced that they would open a new store.</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The company announced that it would open a new store.   (one company: it)</i></blockquote>"},{"title":"The process","html":"<ol><li><b>Check the choices.</b> Pronouns (it / they / its / their) means a referent question. Apostrophes (dog’s / dogs’ / dogs) means a possession question. All four at once means both, and it is a hard one.</li><li><b>Point to the referent.</b> Find the exact word the pronoun stands for, in the passage — not the one in your head.</li><li><b>Singular or plural?</b> Decide from that word. The SAT is not they-friendly: one company, one team, one object is <i>it</i>.</li><li><b>Possession, or “is/are”?</b> Substitute “it is” or “they are”. If that reads wrong, you want the possessive, which on this test it almost always is.</li><li><b>For apostrophes, check the next word.</b> It has to be a noun that can logically be possessed. A verb or a comma after the apostrophe eliminates that choice outright.</li><li><b>Down to two?</b> Go back for the explicit clue — “the lake’s surface”, “the players’ uniforms”. Never decide on sound.</li></ol>"},{"title":"It’s / its, they’re / their / there","html":"<ul><li><b>Its</b> = belonging to it. <b>It’s</b> = it is.</li><li><b>Their</b> = belonging to them. <b>They’re</b> = they are. <b>There</b> = a place.</li><li>Mentally substitute “it is” or “they are.” If that substitution makes sense, use the apostrophe form. It almost never does on the SAT.</li><li>When all four (its / it’s / their / they’re) are the choices, the question is hard: first decide singular or plural, then possession or “is/are.”</li></ul>"}],"qids":["c52652c9","97b62fab","c5d39bc7","430d929a","9eb43963","2cb1ee22","3bceeb93","0bcb4417"],"title":"It’s / its, they’re / their / there"},{"notes":[{"title":"The apostrophe placement rule","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>dog’s</td><td>Singular: one dog possesses the next thing.</td></tr><tr><td>dogs’</td><td>Plural: multiple dogs possess the next thing.</td></tr><tr><td>dogs</td><td>Plain plural: no possession at all.</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>The word right after the apostrophe</b> (or right after a following adjective) <b>must be a NOUN that can logically be possessed.</b> If it’s a verb, a comma, or doesn’t logically fit, eliminate that choice immediately.</li><li>This alone answers roughly half of these questions.</li></ul><blockquote><i>✓ The dog’s bowl   (one dog owns it)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The dogs’ bowls   (several dogs own them)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✓ The dogs barked.   (no possession at all)</i></blockquote><blockquote><i>✗ The dog’s barked.   (the next word is a verb, so there can’t be an apostrophe)</i></blockquote>"},{"title":"Down to two? Check the plurality","html":"<ul><li>Left with a singular vs. plural possessive? Read the passage for explicit context clues. Never guess plural just because it “sounds” right.</li><li>There’s always a specific clue confirming singular or plural: “the lake’s surface,” “the players’ uniforms.”</li></ul>"}],"qids":["1d971f75","b260c65a","856b495d","dd428136","819c443d","9f737b2a","2784cbaf"],"title":"Down to two? Check the plurality"}]},{"id":"e2.10","num":"2.10","title":"Modifiers","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What we’re looking for","html":"<ul><li>The sentence opens with a descriptive phrase and a comma, and the blank is everything after it. The four choices say the same thing in different orders.</li><li><b>A modifier must sit right next to what it modifies.</b></li></ul>"},{"title":"The process","html":"<ol><li><b>Read only from the start of the sentence to the blank.</b> That entire stretch is the modifier. Figure out who or what it’s actually describing.</li><li><b>Put that “something” as the very first word(s) of the answer choice.</b> Whatever the modifier describes must sit immediately after the comma.</li><li><b>Watch for the possessive trap.</b> Match the <b>head noun</b>, not the owner: “Compared to that of the older model, <b>the new phone’s battery</b> lasts longer.” The opener compares batteries, so the battery goes first, not “the new phone.”</li></ol><blockquote><i>Built in the 1850s, the lighthouse still guides ships into the harbor.</i></blockquote><ul><li>What was “built in the 1850s”? The lighthouse, not the ships. So <i>the lighthouse</i> goes right after the comma. “Built in the 1850s, ships are still guided by the lighthouse” says the ships were built in the 1850s.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["1684b237","f1c5157d","cd2443c0","dd6a0326","c437dd53","684b8bd2"],"title":"The process"},{"notes":[{"title":"When two answer choices both look right","html":"<ul><li>If two answer choices start with the exact same subject, they can’t both be correct. Read further: there may be a <b>SECOND modifier</b> at the end of the sentence.</li><li>Check what that ending modifier is describing, and match it the same way: the right word sits immediately next to it.</li><li><b>Cross out the wordiest choice.</b> The uniquely longest choice is almost never the answer (4 times out of 72 on College Board’s questions). The exception: when the opener names a property or achievement (“Compared to that of...”), the correct choice is sometimes the longer, less direct one.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Pronouns, possessives, and modifiers: the full checklist","html":"<ul><li>Pronouns: find the referent, match singular/plural.</li><li>The SAT isn’t “they”-friendly: no plural pronoun for one thing.</li><li>It’s / its, they’re / their: try substituting “it is” / “they are.”</li><li>Possessives: the next word must be a possessable noun.</li><li>Tied on plurality? Read for explicit context clues.</li><li>Modifiers: read start-to-blank, put what it describes first.</li><li>Watch the possessive trap: match the head noun, not the owner.</li><li>Two modifier choices tie? Check for a second, closing modifier.</li></ul><p>## EXPRESSION OF IDEAS</p>"}],"qids":["f0864217","2dd1b8bf","37e5c794","b369d54c","9df6da04","188f7e3c","2b6e1c06"],"title":"When two answer choices both look right"}]}]},{"name":"Expression of Ideas","lessons":[{"id":"e3.1","num":"3.1","title":"Transitions I: The Eight Relationships","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"Only two ways to get these wrong","html":"<ul><li><b>1. Misunderstanding the passage.</b> These passages are short and easy: read every word, start to finish, even if you think you already know the answer. It costs a few extra seconds and eliminates almost all comprehension errors.</li><li><b>2. Not knowing the category rules.</b> Every transition answer choice falls into one of 8 categories. Memorize what the passage has to look like for each category to be correct: that’s the whole system.</li><li>The question always asks for the relationship between <b>the sentence before the blank</b> and <b>the sentence with the blank</b>. A semicolon still counts as a period here.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Every transition, ranked","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Appears</th><th>Correct when it appears</th><th>Example words</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Subverting Expectations</td><td>28.6%</td><td>Common</td><td>however, nonetheless, instead, granted</td></tr><tr><td>Zooming In</td><td>16.5%</td><td>~20% (much lower on hard questions)</td><td>for example, for instance, specifically</td></tr><tr><td>Swapping</td><td>15.6%</td><td>Low (a favorite distractor)</td><td>in addition, moreover, similarly, likewise</td></tr><tr><td>Cause and Effect</td><td>15.2%</td><td>~36%</td><td>as a result, thus, therefore, consequently</td></tr><tr><td>Sequence</td><td>12.4%</td><td>~37%</td><td>meanwhile, next, then, ultimately, later</td></tr><tr><td>Restatement</td><td>7.4%</td><td>~2 to 5%: almost always a trap</td><td>in other words, that is, in summary</td></tr><tr><td>Confirmation</td><td>3.3%</td><td>~57%</td><td>indeed, in fact, of course</td></tr><tr><td>Frequency</td><td>0.9%</td><td>~83%: very rare, usually right</td><td>increasingly, sometimes, again and again</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>To classify an unfamiliar word: say “I’m taking the SAT, [word] ...” out loud and see what naturally comes next.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Subverting Expectations","html":"<ul><li>The second sentence goes against what you’d expect from the first.</li><li>Tell: opposing language between the two sentences (barren vs. fertile, thought vs. found).</li><li>however, nonetheless, nevertheless, by contrast, instead, granted, still</li></ul><blockquote><i>Most desert plants store water in thick leaves. ______ the ocotillo has thin leaves and drops them during dry spells.   → However,</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["20733eac","be44fea0"],"title":"Subverting Expectations"},{"notes":[{"title":"Zooming In","html":"<ul><li>The second sentence gives a specific example or more detail about the first.</li><li>Correct less often on HARD questions: College Board loves faking this structure as a trap.</li><li>for example, for instance, to illustrate, specifically</li></ul><blockquote><i>Many birds use tools. ______ crows bend twigs into hooks to pull insects out of logs.   → For example,</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["db8fe023","f07570bb"],"title":"Zooming In"},{"notes":[{"title":"Swapping","html":"<ul><li>The two sentences are interchangeable: you could swap their order with no change in meaning (like “buy milk and eggs” vs. “eggs and milk”). Each sentence adds a separate item of the same kind.</li><li>One of College Board’s favorite distractor categories: plugging in “in addition” often SOUNDS fine without being correct.</li><li>in addition, additionally, moreover, similarly, likewise</li></ul><blockquote><i>The museum offers free tours on weekends. ______ it hosts free concerts on Friday nights.   → In addition,</i></blockquote><ul><li>Swap test: “The museum hosts free concerts on Friday nights. In addition, it offers free tours on weekends.” Still makes sense, so it’s Swapping.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["290a5d77","fd24f48f"],"title":"Swapping"},{"notes":[{"title":"Cause and Effect","html":"<ul><li>Test: can you say “because [sentence 1], [sentence 2]”? If yes, it’s cause and effect.</li><li>A strong category: correct more than a third of the time it appears.</li><li>as a result, thus, accordingly, consequently, therefore, hence</li></ul><blockquote><i>The bridge was closed for repairs. ______ drivers had to take a thirty-minute detour.   → As a result,</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["04ad68ca","17e49403"],"title":"Cause and Effect"},{"notes":[{"title":"Sequence","html":"<ul><li>The passage lays out a timeline: dates, “first... then...,” progression over time.</li><li>Correct more than a third of the time it appears, and the easiest category: almost all sequence answers are on easy questions.</li><li>meanwhile, next, then, ultimately, lastly, later, previously</li><li>A close cousin: <b>listing</b> words (first, second, finally) when the passage is counting off reasons or items. Only right if the passage has actually set up a list.</li></ul><blockquote><i>The team first collected soil samples from twelve farms. ______ they tested each sample for bacteria.   → Then,</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["c78620ba","82ec9628","63c73b50"],"title":"Sequence"},{"notes":[{"title":"Confirmation","html":"<ul><li>New information appears, but it only serves to confirm (or push further) what the first sentence already said.</li><li>Correct more than half the time it appears. Worth watching for.</li><li>indeed, in fact, of course, fittingly</li></ul><blockquote><i>Researchers expected the new drug to lower blood pressure. ______ it lowered blood pressure by 20 percent within a month.   → Indeed,</i></blockquote><ul><li>Careful: the word “expected” tempts you toward <i>however</i>. But the result matched the expectation, so the second sentence confirms the first.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["0fd4df40","b6d9068e"],"title":"Confirmation"},{"notes":[{"title":"Frequency","html":"<ul><li>The rarest category: the second sentence describes how often something happens, or a trend over time.</li><li>When it shows up, it’s correct the vast majority of the time.</li><li>increasingly, sometimes, in many cases, again and again, more often</li></ul><blockquote><i>The river floods most springs. ______ the water rises high enough to reach the town itself.   → Sometimes,</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["28c7a762","b8eec031"],"title":"Frequency"},{"notes":[{"title":"Restatement","html":"<ul><li>The second sentence paraphrases the first: it adds ZERO new information.</li><li>Almost always a trap, not the answer. When it IS right, the second sentence really does just say the first one again in plainer words.</li><li>in other words, that is, in summary, in conclusion</li></ul><blockquote><i>The plan was too expensive to carry out. ______ the city could not afford it.   → In other words,</i></blockquote>"}],"qids":["7c3f0145","2df7b582"],"title":"Restatement"}]},{"id":"e3.2","num":"3.2","title":"Transitions II: Harder Transitions and the Traps","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"Decide the relationship before you look at the choices","html":"<ul><li>On hard transition questions, the surface wording points to one relationship while the actual logic is another. On College Board’s hard transition questions, that happened more than half the time (61%), versus about 15% on easy ones.</li><li>So: read both sentences, say the relationship out loud in your own words (“the second one is a result of the first”), and only then look at the choices.</li><li>Don’t let a single word decide it. A “however” earlier in the passage, or the word “example,” can pull you toward the wrong category.</li></ul><blockquote><i>Many people assume that bigger engines always burn more fuel. ______ some large engines are more efficient than small ones at highway speeds.   → However,</i></blockquote><ul><li>Say it first: “the second sentence goes against the assumption.” That’s Subverting Expectations, and now no choice from another category can pull you away.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["4fde4454","42e6cc83","a266d876","ecb31049","8e9677e6","2b08f514","176edca6","4d2736f0"],"title":"Decide the relationship before you look at the choices"},{"notes":[{"title":"Same-category answer choices are usually both wrong","html":"<ul><li>There can only be one right answer. If two choices do the same job (both <i>however</i> and <i>nevertheless</i>), that’s a red flag.</li><li>About 45% of transition questions include two choices from the same category. When that happens, neither of them is correct about 90% of the time.</li><li>It’s not 100%, though. The exceptions are pairs that point in different directions inside the same category: <i>earlier</i> vs. <i>next</i>, <i>instead</i> vs. <i>granted</i>. “Granted” concedes a point; “however” fully contrasts. “For example” gives an instance; “specifically” adds detail. Do a quick gut check before auto-eliminating.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["9f1a0d91","70c19cf6","e3edc138","a965c6ed"],"title":"Same-category answer choices are usually both wrong"},{"notes":[{"title":"Words that are (almost) never the answer","html":"<ul><li><b>regardless</b> (0 of 16), <b>in conclusion</b> (0 of 11), <b>furthermore</b> (0 of 8), <b>in other words</b> (1 of 36).</li><li>These are College Board’s distractor words. Treat them as wrong unless the passage leaves you no choice.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Context-specific phrase questions","html":"<ul><li>Longer answer choices (“Challenging this assumption,” “Undermining this explanation”). Same 8 categories, more to verify.</li><li><b>1. Read the passage fully and decide the category first</b>, before looking at the choices. This locks in the relationship and stops you from being swayed by a distractor phrase.</li><li><b>2. Eliminate whole answer choices by category.</b> A phrase that’s clearly “confirming” can be cut if you’ve decided the relationship is “contrasting.”</li><li><b>3. Verify the noun inside the surviving phrases.</b> Words like “this” refer back to the prior sentence: confirm which noun it should be pointing to.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Transitions: the full checklist","html":"<ul><li>Read every word of the passage, start to finish.</li><li>Name the relationship out loud before looking: which of the 8 categories fits?</li><li>Subverting Expectations and Cause and Effect are common AND reliable.</li><li>Zooming In and Swapping are common but often a trap on hard questions.</li><li>Restatement is almost never the right answer.</li><li>Two choices in the same category → both are usually wrong.</li><li>Regardless, in conclusion, furthermore, in other words: distractor words.</li><li>Context-specific phrases: decide the category before reading choices, then check the noun.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["2fc22780","7dbcb7f4","e6b1e12c"],"title":"Context-specific phrase questions"}]},{"id":"e3.3","num":"3.3","title":"Rhetorical Synthesis","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What does the question look like?","html":"<ul><li>A student’s notes, as a list of bullet points, followed by: “The student wants to ___. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?”</li><li>These are the last questions of every module, and they are some of the fastest points on the test <b>if you never read the notes first.</b></li></ul>"},{"title":"The process","html":"<ol><li><b>Read the goal first.</b> Skip the notes entirely. The goal is the whole question.</li><li><b>Break the goal into its parts.</b> “Emphasize a difference between the two muslins” has two parts: both muslins must be mentioned, AND the sentence must contrast them.</li><li><b>Eliminate every choice that misses a part.</b> Most wrong answers are perfectly accurate sentences about the notes. They just don’t do what the goal asks.</li><li><b>Only now, check the survivors against the notes.</b> On hard questions, a choice can do the right job but get a detail from the notes wrong.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Worked example (made-up notes)","html":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Harlow Lake is a reservoir in the western United States.</li><li>In 1950, the lake covered 40 square miles.</li><li>Today, the lake covers 12 square miles.</li><li>Drought and increased water use are the main causes.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how much the lake has shrunk. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p><ul><li>A) Harlow Lake is a reservoir in the western United States.</li><li>B) Drought and increased water use have affected Harlow Lake.</li><li>C) Harlow Lake covered 40 square miles in 1950 but covers only 12 today.</li><li>D) Harlow Lake covers 12 square miles.</li></ul><ul><li>The goal has one job: show HOW MUCH the lake shrank. That takes a before AND an after.</li><li>A and B are accurate, but neither says anything about the size. Eliminate.</li><li>D gives only the “after.” You can’t see a shrink without the “before.” Eliminate.</li><li>C gives both numbers and says “only”: it does exactly what the goal asks. <b>C.</b> Notice we never needed to read the first note.</li></ul>"},{"title":"What each kind of goal requires","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>The goal says...</th><th>The right answer must...</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>identify / specify / indicate X</td><td>State that one fact. Nothing else matters.</td></tr><tr><td>emphasize a similarity</td><td>Mention both things AND say what they share (both, like, similarly).</td></tr><tr><td>emphasize / contrast a difference</td><td>Mention both things AND contrast them (whereas, while, unlike, but).</td></tr><tr><td>emphasize X (a size, an order, a significance)</td><td>Put X front and center: the number, the “first... then,” the “most important.”</td></tr><tr><td>present the study and its findings</td><td>Say what was studied AND what was found.</td></tr><tr><td>present the aim / methods of the study</td><td>Only the aim (why they did it) or only the method (how). Not the results.</td></tr><tr><td>introduce X to an audience <b>unfamiliar</b> with it</td><td>Explain who or what X is (“Octavia Butler, an American science fiction writer, ...”).</td></tr><tr><td>... to an audience <b>already familiar</b> with it</td><td>Skip the explanation of what they already know; name it directly.</td></tr><tr><td>explain why / how / an advantage of X</td><td>Give the reason or the mechanism, not just a description of X.</td></tr><tr><td>use a quotation to...</td><td>Include a quotation, AND the quotation has to do the stated job.</td></tr><tr><td>begin a narrative / recount a sequence</td><td>Tell events in order, like the opening of a story.</td></tr><tr><td>make a generalization</td><td>Speak about the whole category, not one named example.</td></tr></tbody></table>"}],"qids":["25a197dd","a3204ab0","264e7415"],"title":"Warm-up: one-part goals"},{"notes":[],"qids":["ca4ff52d","af76771f","7572131d","1bb4aec8","9551ef8b"],"title":"Similarities and differences"},{"notes":[],"qids":["4b376902","eaded344","dd11e5ab","94f48106","1469d23a","58281fc4","39ccb463"],"title":"Emphasize, explain, and audience"},{"notes":[{"title":"Studies, generalizations, and claims","html":"<p>## INFORMATION AND IDEAS</p>"}],"qids":["aa7e10d0","2c7dced2","c3b854fa","f6d454c1","81315093","07456405","8d1ddd1b","3dcc7140"],"title":"Studies, generalizations, and claims"}]}]},{"name":"Information and Ideas","lessons":[{"id":"e4.1","num":"4.1","title":"How to Read an SAT Passage: The Main Claim","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"Why reading “horizontally” fails","html":"<ul><li>Most students read a passage <b>horizontally</b>: every sentence gets equal weight, and they collect a list of facts as they go.</li><li>The problem: your brain is biased toward ideas and words it finds familiar, so you give inflated importance to unimportant pieces of the puzzle. The answer choice that feels familiar is usually the trap.</li><li>SAT reading questions are built to separate the students who understood <b>one specific relationship</b> from those who didn’t. The correct answer is always directly tied to that relationship.</li><li>So we read <b>hierarchically</b>: start with the most important idea, and only dig into the details when we need to.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Chunking: never move on confused","html":"<ul><li>Understand every 3–5 words before moving to the next chunk. If a chunk doesn’t click immediately, stop: reread just that chunk, restate it in your own words, and connect it to what came before.</li><li>If you don’t understand a line and read on anyway, the confusion snowballs: line 2 needs line 1, line 3 needs line 2. By the end, you understand nothing and have to reread the whole passage.</li><li>This is slower per line but faster overall, because you never reread. If you can predict what’s next, you understood it, and you’ll retain it without extra effort.</li><li><b>Predict where the passage is going</b> as you read. Being wrong is fine and expected: the act of predicting is what forces real understanding.</li><li>Test yourself: at the two-thirds mark of any passage, stop and recite the main points from memory. If you can’t, you were skimming, not chunking.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Step 1: Find the Main Claim","html":"<ul><li>The main claim is usually near the end of the passage: “Their finding suggests...,” “The researchers concluded...,” “However, ...”</li><li>Reduce it to a simple model:</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>What changes</b> (the independent variable),</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>what is measured</b> (the dependent variable),</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>how they’re related.</b></li><li>If you can answer the question with just this sentence, you should. It’s faster, and it’s safer: much of the rest of the text is there to distract you or tempt you into offering your own opinion.</li></ul><blockquote><i>what changes  →  relationship  →  what is measured</i></blockquote>"},{"title":"Step 2: Test the model against the answer choices","html":"<ul><li>Your instinct will be to dive back into the passage and “understand it better.” Resist it. Go straight to the choices and evaluate them using nothing but your model.</li><li>On easier questions, the right answer is already determined here.</li><li>On harder questions, this won’t be enough: two choices seem equally good, or the choices use a term you haven’t pinned down. Now you know <b>exactly</b> which terms you need to go back for.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Step 3: Define the missing or vague terms","html":"<ul><li>Go back to the text with one job: define the specific piece your model is missing. Not rereading aimlessly.</li><li><b>Watch for semantic drift</b>: you start with the right main claim, but as you reread, your model quietly expands to include other details from the paragraph. The claim didn’t change. Your model shouldn’t either.</li><li>SAT passages love using the same word two different ways (a “single strategy” vs. a “single crop”). Slow down on the key words.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Step 4: Eliminate what doesn’t fit the model","html":"<ul><li>Don’t ask “does this seem fine?” Ask <b>“do the pieces in this answer choice match the pieces in my model?”</b></li><li>Eliminate choices that bring in something the claim never mentions, flip the relationship, or leave out a critical part of it.</li><li>Don’t overthink. On hard questions, the correct answer often looks terrible at first glance, but it’s the only one that actually fits the model.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Step 5: Match the logic clause by clause","html":"<ul><li>If two choices survive, slow down and read like a lawyer.</li><li>Break each choice into clauses. Most wrong answers fail at <b>one specific clause</b>, not the entire sentence.</li><li><b>Resolve pointing words</b>: this, that, these, those, it, they. Find what each one refers back to. If you don’t know what “that outcome” means, you don’t understand the sentence yet.</li><li><b>Untangle negatives</b>: “avoiding that outcome may be diminished” = “more likely to end up with that outcome.” Flip double negatives into a plain positive statement.</li><li>On easier questions you’ll never get to Steps 4 and 5: the claim is quick to spot and the choices are easy to eliminate. On the hardest ones, Step 5 takes real time and energy, so unless it’s your last question, flag it and come back once you’ve worked through the module.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Worked example (from the March SAT)","html":"<p><i>Some farms are expanding their mix of crop species in an effort to reduce nonagricultural biodiversity loss caused by increasing agricultural specialization, in which farmers focus on a limited range of crops or livestock species to improve yields and, thus, their revenues. To study the joint environmental and economic effects of diversification practices (which can encompass improved soil and water management as well as increased variety in crops and livestock), Laura Vang Rasmussen and team analyzed data from 2,655 farms in eleven countries. They found that using multiple diversification practices concurrently had greater potential for improving both nonagricultural biodiversity and yields than using a single strategy did. Their finding suggests that ______</i></p><ul><li>A) farmers who focus primarily on increasing crop variety without engaging in other diversification practices will likely help reverse nonagricultural biodiversity loss, but their farms will produce smaller yields than if they had continued to focus on developing a single crop.</li><li>B) agricultural diversification doesn’t inherently involve a trade-off between environmental and economic considerations, but farmers’ chances of avoiding that outcome may be diminished if they focus on crop variety to the exclusion of other diversification practices.</li><li>C) farmers who are reluctant to switch from specializing in a single crop to cultivating a variety of crops incorrectly assume that any improvements in yields will be insufficient to offset the costs associated with making that transition.</li><li>D) it is more expensive to increase crop variety while simultaneously implementing other diversification practices than it is to increase crop variety and then subsequently implement other diversification practices.</li></ul><ul><li><b>Main claim</b> (last sentence before the blank): multiple practices together → better biodiversity AND yields than one strategy.</li><li><b>Missing term</b> (Step 3): crop variety is ONE of the diversification practices, so “crop variety only” = a single strategy. A “single crop” means no diversification at all: it isn’t in the claim.</li><li><b>Eliminate</b> (Step 4): A and C compare against a <i>single crop</i>, which the claim never mentions (semantic drift). D is about cost. Only B compares multiple practices to a single strategy.</li><li><b>Clause by clause</b> (Step 5): “doesn’t inherently involve a trade-off” = can have both; “chances of avoiding that outcome may be diminished” = may be forced into a trade-off; “focus on crop variety to the exclusion of other practices” = use a single strategy. B matches the claim exactly.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Predict your answer, then go word by word","html":"<ul><li>Before you look at the choices, form your own version of the right answer, even a rough one. If you can’t predict anything, you missed something in the passage.</li><li>Then read each choice one word at a time and ask: <b>“still correct so far?”</b> The first word that isn’t supported by the text kills that choice.</li><li>Most “getting tricked” isn’t a reading failure. It’s an under-formed idea of the passage letting a wrong answer choice implant itself in your head.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Reading: the full checklist","html":"<ul><li>Chunk in 3–5 word pieces. Never move on confused.</li><li>Predict where the passage is heading, chunk by chunk.</li><li>Find the main claim and reduce it to a model: what changes, what is measured, how they’re related.</li><li>Test the model against the choices before rereading.</li><li>Go back only to define the missing or vague terms. Watch for semantic drift.</li><li>Eliminate choices whose pieces don’t fit the model.</li><li>Down to two: clause by clause, resolve pointing words, untangle negatives.</li><li>Form your own predicted answer, then go word by word. Kill a choice the instant one word isn’t supported.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["6bc0e595","8a3ecac6","01989d77","39e440e4","ce4448b7","ed314256"],"title":"Predict your answer, then go word by word"}]},{"id":"e4.2","num":"4.2","title":"Central Ideas and Details","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What does the question look like?","html":"<ul><li><b>“According to the text, ...”</b>: a detail question. The answer is stated in the passage, usually in close to the same words. These are the easiest reading questions on the test.</li><li><b>“Which choice best states the main idea of the text?”</b>: the main claim from 4.1, restated.</li><li><b>“Based on the text, ...” / “What does the text most strongly suggest...”</b>: the answer is not stated directly. You have to take one small step from what the text says.</li><li><b>“Which question does the text most directly attempt to answer?” / “main topic”</b>: the same main claim, phrased as the question it answers or the topic it covers.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The process","html":"<ol><li><b>Read the stem first and name the type.</b> “According to the text” is stated outright. “Main idea” is the main claim. “Based on the text” or “suggests” is one small step past what is written.</li><li><b>Find the main claim before you read a single choice</b> (4.1): usually the last sentence, or the one right after a “however”.</li><li><b>For a detail question, point at the sentence</b> that answers it. If you cannot put a finger on one, you are guessing.</li><li><b>Say the answer in your own words</b> before looking down.</li><li><b>Eliminate on the three shapes.</b> Too narrow: a real detail that is not the point. Too broad: goes further than the text does — <i>proves, all, always</i>. Half-right: the first half matches and the second half is unsupported.</li></ol>"},{"title":"According to the text","html":"<ul><li>Find the sentence that answers the question, point to it, and pick the choice that says the same thing.</li><li>The wrong answers here are things the text never says: usually a real noun from the passage attached to an unsupported claim.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["78b265b2","2287b040","92c2564d"],"title":"According to the text"},{"notes":[{"title":"The main idea","html":"<ul><li>The main idea is the <b>main claim</b> (4.1): usually the last sentence, or the sentence right after a “however.”</li><li>The wrong answers are built in predictable ways:</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Too narrow</b>: an accurate detail from one sentence, but not the point of the whole text.</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Too broad</b> or <b>overreaching</b>: goes further than the text does (“proves,” “all,” “always”).</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Half-right</b>: the first half matches, the second half is unsupported. Read every word.</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Reuses a passage noun</b> but says something the text never says about it.</li><li>On hard questions, the correct answer describes the text more abstractly than the text itself does. It uses fewer of the passage’s exact words than the traps do.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Worked example (a made-up passage to practice the steps)","html":"<p><i>For decades, scientists assumed that honeybees find flowers mainly by sight. But when biologists trained bees to visit feeders that looked identical but smelled different, the bees chose the correct feeder 90 percent of the time. The researchers concluded that scent plays a far larger role in how bees find food than scientists had believed.</i></p><ul><li><b>Main claim</b>: the last sentence. Scent matters more to how bees find food than scientists thought.</li><li>✓ “Scent is more important to how honeybees find food than scientists previously believed.”</li><li>✗ “Bees chose the correct feeder 90 percent of the time.” True, but <b>too narrow</b>: one detail.</li><li>✗ “Honeybees find food using only scent.” <b>Overreach</b>: the text says scent matters more, not that sight doesn’t matter.</li><li>✗ “Scientists now focus mostly on honeybee eyesight.” <b>Reuses a passage noun</b> (sight) for something the text never says.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["dc5edbf6","0b696a0c","602b47c7","96802cc0","11c68ded","87aa7bab","23ecf625","db2da2bf","a3fb5e77","70aacc03"],"title":"Worked example (a made-up passage to practice the steps)"},{"notes":[{"title":"Literary passages","html":"<ul><li>Passages that open “The following text is from...” a novel, story, or poem.</li><li>Ask: <b>what is happening, to whom, and how do they feel about it?</b> That’s the main idea of almost every literary passage.</li><li>Figurative language is where the traps live: a choice that takes a metaphor literally (“my heart sank” read as a physical event) is wrong.</li><li>Older passages (1800s and earlier) are harder only because of the language. Chunk them slowly and translate each sentence into plain modern English.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["57485f5e","3a1f02b0","adbcbce0","16025337"],"title":"Literary passages"}]},{"id":"e4.3","num":"4.3","title":"Command of Evidence: Textual","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What does the question look like?","html":"<ul><li><b>“Which finding, if true, would most directly support the claim / hypothesis?”</b></li><li><b>“Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken...?”</b></li><li><b>“Which quotation from [the poem / novel] most effectively illustrates the claim?”</b></li><li>In all three, you are NOT asked what the passage says. You are asked what would prove or disprove <b>one specific claim</b>. Find it first.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The process","html":"<ol><li><b>Find the claim.</b> You are not being asked what the passage says. You are being asked what would prove or disprove one specific claim, so locate it before anything else.</li><li><b>Reduce it to the model</b> (4.1): what changes → the relationship → what is measured.</li><li><b>Decide the direction.</b> Support means the result the model predicts. Weaken means the opposite, the wrong direction, or another explanation.</li><li><b>Test each choice against EVERY part of the model</b> — the same group, the same thing measured, the same direction. One part missing makes it wrong, however true it sounds.</li><li><b>For a quotation question, split the claim into its parts</b> and make the quotation show all of them. Translate the old language into plain English before you judge it.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Support: build the model, then find the matching result","html":"<ul><li>Find the claim or hypothesis (it’s usually stated as “she hypothesized that...” or “the researchers claim that...”). Reduce it to the model from 4.1: what changes → relationship → what is measured.</li><li>The right finding <b>matches every part of the model</b>: the same group, the same thing measured, the same direction. If the claim says “X increases Y,” the finding shows Y going up when X goes up.</li><li>The most common wrong answer is a <b>true-sounding detail that does the wrong job</b>: it uses the passage’s own terms, but it doesn’t bear on the claim.</li><li>On hard questions, the right answer is often one you have to connect:</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>The missing premise</b>: the claim needs a link the passage didn’t state, and the answer supplies it.</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>The right comparison</b>: the finding compares the test group to a control group, or the right two groups.</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>A stand-in</b>: the finding measures something that stands in for the claim (more deliveries per day = shorter travel time).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Worked example (a made-up passage to practice the steps)","html":"<p><i>Claim: trees planted along city streets lower summer temperatures on those streets.</i> Model: more street trees → lower summer street temperature.</p><ul><li>✓ “Streets with many trees were, on average, 5°F cooler on summer afternoons than similar streets with no trees.” Same thing measured, same direction, the right comparison.</li><li>✗ “Residents said they prefer living on tree-lined streets.” Uses the topic but doesn’t measure temperature: <b>wrong job</b>.</li><li>✗ “Trees absorb carbon dioxide from the air.” True in the real world, but it isn’t the claim.</li><li>✗ “Streets with many trees were warmer in winter.” Wrong season: the claim is about summer.</li><li>The weaken version would be: “Streets with many trees were no cooler in summer than similar streets without trees.”</li></ul>"}],"qids":["5f6adeee","3091f805","c384987b","a9390104","31ad8024","81430e26","3d91c973","6eb0223c"],"title":"Worked example (a made-up passage to practice the steps)"},{"notes":[{"title":"Weaken: the same model, flipped","html":"<ul><li>Build the same model, then look for the result that shows the <b>opposite</b>: the effect didn’t happen, happened in the wrong direction, or has another explanation.</li><li>Watch the direction words carefully. A choice that supports the claim is the most common trap on a weaken question.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["dc47c2ac","d76ea360","8545ccfe"],"title":"Weaken: the same model, flipped"},{"notes":[{"title":"Quotation questions","html":"<ul><li>The claim is about a character, a speaker, or a poem. Break it into its parts: “the speaker has <b>contradictory</b> feelings about <b>spring</b>” has two parts.</li><li>The right quotation shows <b>every part</b> of the claim. Wrong quotations are accurate lines from the work that show only one part, or none.</li><li>The quotations are old and dense. Translate each one into plain English before you judge it.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["628e1305","72c7dafd","d5d3ea31","99fdf71c","37e15265"],"title":"Quotation questions"}]},{"id":"e4.4","num":"4.4","title":"Command of Evidence: Quantitative","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What does the question look like?","html":"<ul><li>A short passage and a table (on the real test, sometimes a graph). The passage ends with a claim or a blank: “Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement / support the claim?”</li></ul>"},{"title":"The process","html":"<ol><li><b>Read the claim or blank first.</b> It tells you exactly which part of the table matters.</li><li><b>Read the table’s title, column headings, and units.</b> Know what each number means before you compare any of them.</li><li><b>Find only the cells the claim needs.</b> You almost never need the whole table.</li><li><b>Check every choice against the table AND the claim.</b> Both have to be true.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Worked example (a made-up passage to practice the steps)","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Visitors</th><th>Rainy days</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>June</td><td>4,200</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>July</td><td>5,100</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>August</td><td>3,900</td><td>9</td></tr></tbody></table><p><i>Claim: the park drew the most visitors in the month with the fewest rainy days.</i></p><ul><li>The claim needs two cells: the month with the most visitors, and that month’s rainy days.</li><li>✓ “July had the most visitors (5,100) and the fewest rainy days (1).” Both parts of the claim.</li><li>✗ “August had the most rainy days (9).” Accurate, but it doesn’t bear on the claim.</li><li>✗ “June had 4,200 visitors.” Accurate, one cell, no connection to rain.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["0cf13ece","9debe79a","7a1877be","bc1b8a42"],"title":"Worked example (a made-up passage to practice the steps)"},{"notes":[{"title":"The two ways the wrong answers work","html":"<ul><li><b>On easy questions: a misread cell.</b> The wrong answers are the numbers from the wrong row or the wrong column. Slow down and trace the row and column with your eyes.</li><li><b>On hard questions: accurate data that doesn’t bear on the claim.</b> Every choice correctly describes the table, but only one says what the claim needs. “True” isn’t enough; it has to be relevant.</li><li>Hard questions also add a condition:</li><li class=\"sub\">the claim sets a rule, and the data must satisfy it (“relative to population” means the per-person column),</li><li class=\"sub\">a number from the passage has to be applied to the table,</li><li class=\"sub\">the claim has two parts, and the right choice covers both.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["5ff1ba73","a0203977","95388117","dd349efc","2c06139b","80fd9454","df9c5a1d","56f477fb","55df0275"],"title":"The two ways the wrong answers work"}]},{"id":"e4.5","num":"4.5","title":"Inferences","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What does the question look like?","html":"<ul><li>Every inference question ends with a blank and “Which choice most logically completes the text?”</li><li>The answer is the step the text leaves unstated. The passage lays out the premises; you finish the logic.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The process","html":"<ol><li><b>Find the main claim and build the model</b> (4.1).</li><li><b>Predict the blank.</b> What has to be true, given everything before it? Say it in your own words.</li><li><b>Match your prediction</b>, then go word by word through the survivors.</li></ol>"},{"title":"The logical moves College Board uses","html":"<ul><li><b>Restate or generalize a finding</b>: the easiest version.</li><li><b>Apply a rule or definition</b> from earlier in the passage to a new case.</li><li><b>Rule out the alternatives</b>: two explanations are eliminated, so the blank names the one that’s left.</li><li><b>Compare conditions</b>: the result differed between two groups or two times, so something about the difference explains it.</li><li><b>A null or surprising result</b>: nothing changed, or the opposite happened. The blank explains why.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The traps","html":"<ul><li><b>On-topic but unsupported</b>: sounds scientific, uses the right nouns, but the text never gets there. The most common wrong answer.</li><li><b>Overreach</b>: the right direction, but too strong (“proves,” “all,” “the only”). The most effective trap per appearance. Hedged words (“may,” “some,” “likely”) are fine when the text is hedged.</li><li><b>Reversal</b>: the right pieces, the relationship flipped.</li><li><b>Contradicts</b>: directly opposed to a sentence in the text.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Worked example (a made-up passage to practice the steps)","html":"<p><i>Researchers found that tomato plants grown next to basil produced 20 percent more fruit than tomato plants grown alone. In a second experiment, tomato plants grown next to basil plants that were sealed inside plastic bags produced no more fruit than plants grown alone. These results suggest that ______</i></p><ul><li>The second experiment blocks the air between the plants but not the soil, and the effect disappears. So the effect travels through the air.</li><li>✓ “something the basil releases into the air increases the tomato plants’ fruit production.” (Rule out the alternative: it isn’t the soil.)</li><li>✗ “basil increases the fruit production of all vegetable plants.” <b>Overreach</b>: only tomatoes were tested.</li><li>✗ “tomato plants grown alone produce more fruit than those grown near basil.” <b>Reversal.</b></li><li>✗ “basil grows larger when planted near tomatoes.” <b>Unsupported</b>: basil growth was never measured.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["5b4829d2","b4da6aaf","4603d1f7"],"title":"Restate or generalize"},{"notes":[],"qids":["72cbdbc6","4025e00c","3d505895"],"title":"Apply a rule or definition"},{"notes":[],"qids":["b1fab3e1","f1bfbed3","ac285054","58e9e497"],"title":"Rule out the alternatives"},{"notes":[],"qids":["c4d43991","45c03837","4ba0695d"],"title":"Absence, comparisons, and null results"},{"notes":[{"title":"Watch the strength of the claim","html":"<p>## CRAFT AND STRUCTURE</p>"}],"qids":["5105ca38","9c591ff7","a13c1c66"],"title":"Watch the strength of the claim"}]}]},{"name":"Craft and Structure","lessons":[{"id":"e5.1","num":"5.1","title":"Words in Context","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"It’s a process problem, not a vocab problem","html":"<ul><li><b>Come up with a synonym for what the answer should be, using direct evidence from the passage, before you ever read the answer choices.</b></li><li>Your synonym can’t be your first gut reaction. It has to be backed by evidence in the passage.</li><li>That evidence always exists: a synonym stated directly, an example of the word, or the logical flow of the sentence. It sits in the blank’s own sentence about 70% of the time.</li><li>Once you have your synonym, scan the choices for the best match. You don’t need to know every word’s exact definition.</li></ul><blockquote><i>The chef is known for her ______ approach to cooking: she measures every ingredient to the gram and times every step.</i></blockquote><ul><li>Evidence: the colon, then “measures every ingredient to the gram and times every step.” My synonym: <i>precise</i>.</li><li>Choices: A) casual  B) precise  C) expensive  D) popular. Match: <b>B</b>. I didn’t need the choices to know what I was looking for.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["fa014d2d","d529c1ad","69a6d050"],"title":"It’s a process problem, not a vocab problem"},{"notes":[{"title":"“As used in the text”","html":"<ul><li>The word has several meanings, and the question asks which one the text is using. “She gave a sharp reply” means <i>harsh</i>, not <i>pointed</i>.</li><li>Cover the word, read the sentence, and put your own word in its place. Then match.</li><li>The trap is the word’s most common meaning. On harder questions, the text uses a secondary meaning on purpose.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["45a109a3","e3ffb854","359902ae","be94c9fc"],"title":"“As used in the text”"},{"notes":[{"title":"Unusual punctuation points to the answer","html":"<ul><li>Because there can only be one defensible right answer, College Board has to plant the evidence somewhere.</li><li>Watch closely around <b>dashes, colons, semicolons</b>, or clusters of commas: that’s where a synonym or example often sits (mostly colons).</li><li>An example set off between dashes often IS the demonstration of the missing word’s meaning.</li><li>If you misread the passage on a vocab question, there’s no safety net: unlike a main idea question, only one word gives the answer away. So find that evidence before you commit.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["4b8244ca","e8c26398","7b4dde72"],"title":"Unusual punctuation points to the answer"},{"notes":[{"title":"Find the negation first","html":"<ul><li>Before you build your synonym, look for <b>not, although, while, rather than, instead, but, despite</b>. These flip the meaning of the blank.</li><li>“Although the process was once thought to be ___, it is now known to be simple” → the blank means the opposite of simple.</li><li>This is the single biggest difference between easy and hard vocab questions: more than half of the hard ones have a negation or contrast in the blank’s sentence. The trap is the word that fits if you drop the “not.”</li></ul><blockquote><i>Although the lecture was supposed to be brief, the professor’s remarks were so ______ that many students left before the end.</i></blockquote><ul><li>“Although... brief” flips the blank: it means the opposite of brief. Synonym: <i>long</i>.</li><li>Choices: A) concise  B) lengthy  C) humorous  D) confusing. <b>B.</b> <i>Concise</i> is the trap: it fits if you drop the “although.”</li></ul>"}],"qids":["479c7e82","b5898291","1fa751f1","995358b4","22a41819"],"title":"Find the negation first"},{"notes":[{"title":"Unfamiliar answer choices","html":"<ul><li>Even when you don’t know the words, the same process narrows it down: eliminate the choices you DO know don’t match your synonym.</li><li>Pick whichever unfamiliar word sounds closest. Connotation (positive vs. negative) is a legitimate tiebreaker.</li><li>Prefixes that mean “not” (un-, in-, im-, a-, dis-) are genuinely worth memorizing. Most other root and prefix lists rarely help on test day.</li></ul>"},{"title":"How to practice","html":"<ul><li>The skill worth drilling is <b>picking the closest word to a definition you already have in mind</b>, not memorizing word lists.</li><li>Use a common SAT-vocab flashcard set (e.g., on Quizlet), one that doesn’t show the definition next to the choices. Switch the mode to multiple choice, answer with the term: you read the definition and pick the matching word. This mirrors the real question.</li><li>Shuffle and repeat. Exposure to hundreds of common words plus reps at the matching skill compounds fast.</li><li>Skip the 10,000-word lists. You’re unlikely to see most of those words anyway.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Vocabulary: the full checklist","html":"<ul><li>Find the passage’s evidence before reading the answer choices.</li><li>Find the negation or contrast word first.</li><li>Check near dashes, colons, and semicolons for the giveaway.</li><li>Write your own synonym before scanning the choices.</li><li>“As used”: expect the word’s less common meaning.</li><li>Match your synonym to the closest choice; connotation breaks ties.</li><li>Practice word ↔ synonym matching, not raw memorization. Learn the “not” prefixes.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["bce4d106","6c9f1727"],"title":"Unfamiliar answer choices"}]},{"id":"e5.2","num":"5.2","title":"Text Structure and Purpose","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What does the question look like?","html":"<ul><li><b>“Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?”</b>: why did the author write it?</li><li><b>“Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?”</b>: what job does one sentence do?</li><li><b>“Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?”</b>: what does the text do, in order?</li><li>All three are about <b>what the text is DOING</b>, not what it says. The choices describe actions: introduces, explains, challenges, illustrates, concedes.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The process","html":"<ol><li><b>Name the type from the stem.</b> The purpose of the whole text, the function of one sentence, or the overall structure.</li><li><b>Label each sentence with its job, in order</b> — a belief, the evidence, the conclusion. Do this before reading any choice; it answers all three types.</li><li><b>For purpose,</b> finish the sentence “I wrote this to…” in your own words.</li><li><b>For function,</b> read the sentence before and the sentence after. A function only exists in relation to its neighbors.</li><li><b>For structure, check the choices one step at a time.</b> The wrong ones get the first steps right and fail on the last, so never stop reading a choice halfway.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Main purpose","html":"<ul><li>The purpose is tied to the <b>main claim</b> (4.1). Ask: if the author had to finish the sentence “I wrote this to...,” what would they say?</li><li>Wrong answers <b>overreach</b> (the text “argues” when it only “describes”), name only one part of the text, or reverse the author’s attitude.</li><li>For a literary passage: what does this scene show about the character?</li></ul>"}],"qids":["e5ca7687","e19e8478","aa7fc89b","af43b0bd","03c9f327","0b5ecf0e"],"title":"Main purpose"},{"notes":[{"title":"Function of a sentence","html":"<ul><li>Read the sentence <b>before</b> and the sentence <b>after</b> the underlined one. A function only makes sense in relation to its neighbors.</li><li>Name the job in your own words before you look at the choices:</li><li class=\"sub\">gives <b>background</b> for what follows,</li><li class=\"sub\">states the <b>claim</b> the rest of the text supports,</li><li class=\"sub\">gives an <b>example</b> of the sentence before,</li><li class=\"sub\">states a <b>belief</b> that the text then challenges (“Scientists long believed...”),</li><li class=\"sub\">gives the <b>method</b>, the <b>result</b>, or a <b>limitation</b> of a study.</li><li>The most common trap is the <b>wrong part</b>: an accurate description of a <i>different</i> sentence in the passage.</li><li>In a poem or novel, the underlined line is often figurative. Translate the image into what it says about the character or speaker before you look at the choices.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Worked example (a made-up passage to practice the steps)","html":"<p><i>Many people believe that goldfish can remember things for only a few seconds. &lt;u&gt;In one experiment, goldfish learned to push a lever to get food and still used it correctly three months later.&lt;/u&gt; Findings like this suggest that goldfish memory has been badly underestimated.</i></p><ul><li>Sentence 1: a common belief. Sentence 2 (underlined): an experiment. Sentence 3: the conclusion.</li><li>Function of sentence 2: <b>it gives evidence that challenges the belief in sentence 1 and supports the conclusion in sentence 3.</b></li><li>✗ “It states the main claim of the text.” <b>Wrong part</b>: that’s sentence 3.</li><li>✗ “It explains why people believe goldfish have short memories.” The text never says why.</li><li>Overall structure of the same text: “presents a common belief, describes an experiment, then draws a conclusion that challenges the belief.”</li></ul>"}],"qids":["066a3295","b3e145e4","dbb56a02","590f0ad2","c6bd3447","1f2d6173","153aaae2"],"title":"Worked example (a made-up passage to practice the steps)"},{"notes":[{"title":"Overall structure","html":"<ul><li>Label each sentence (or group of sentences) with its job, in order. Then find the choice whose steps match yours.</li><li>Check the choices <b>one step at a time</b>. Wrong answers usually get the first steps right and fail on the <b>last</b> step. Don’t stop reading a choice halfway.</li><li>Hard versions have three steps, and every step has to match.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["2903a041","f6352bd3","34d7bb25","6ae393f7","f61c22f3"],"title":"Overall structure"}]},{"id":"e5.3","num":"5.3","title":"Cross-Text Connections","parts":[{"notes":[{"title":"What does the question look like?","html":"<ul><li>Two short texts on the same topic, usually by different authors. The most common question: <b>“Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to [the claim] in Text 1?”</b></li><li>Other versions: what would both authors agree on, how do the two texts differ, or how would Text 1’s model or rule apply to the case in Text 2?</li></ul>"},{"title":"The process","html":"<ol><li><b>Text 1: find its main claim</b> (4.1). One sentence.</li><li><b>Text 2: find its main claim</b>, and <b>the specific evidence</b> it uses.</li><li><b>Name the relationship</b>: does Text 2 agree, disagree, or <b>qualify</b> (partly agree, but adds a limit or a missing factor)?</li><li><b>Predict the response</b>, then match it.</li></ol>"},{"title":"The right answer uses Text 2’s specifics","html":"<ul><li>On hard questions, the correct answer applies Text 2’s <b>specific evidence or principle</b> to Text 1’s claim. A general “Text 2 would disagree” isn’t enough.</li><li>Text 2 often <b>qualifies</b> rather than flatly disagrees: “X is real, but it isn’t the only cause,” “the conclusion is right, but for a different reason.”</li><li>The traps:</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Right stance, one swapped detail</b>: the choice disagrees for the right reason, but names the wrong thing.</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Right stance, wrong grounds</b>: disagrees, but not for the reason Text 2 gives.</li><li class=\"sub\"><b>Too strong</b>: “Text 1 is completely wrong” when Text 2 only adds a caveat.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Worked example (a made-up passage to practice the steps)","html":"<p><b>Text 1</b>: <i>Some historians argue that literacy spread in Europe mainly because printing made books cheap.</i></p><p><b>Text 2</b>: <i>Cheaper books alone do not explain the rise in reading. In towns that opened public schools, literacy rose quickly, even before cheap books were available.</i></p><ul><li>Text 1’s claim: cheap books caused the rise in literacy. Text 2: schools were also a major cause. Relationship: Text 2 <b>qualifies</b> Text 1.</li><li>✓ “Cheaper books may have helped, but Text 1 overlooks the role of schools.” It uses Text 2’s specific evidence.</li><li>✗ “Books did not actually become cheaper.” Text 2 never says that.</li><li>✗ “Printing had no effect on literacy.” <b>Too strong</b>: Text 2 only says price wasn’t the whole story.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["02fd3da7","ed52a093","c4737d6a","eae66bf9","faee8ec7","f878693b","105ea6de","27d9bb69","d0198544","84dbd633"],"title":"Worked example (a made-up passage to practice the steps)"},{"notes":[{"title":"“Both authors would agree”","html":"<ul><li>Easy version: both texts state the shared point directly.</li><li>Hard version: the texts disagree, and you have to find the one thing they both take for granted. It’s often hiding in a concession (“Although...”).</li></ul>"}],"qids":["8d802289","c68ceeff","7bf79a90","c106b9f7"],"title":"“Both authors would agree”"},{"notes":[{"title":"Differences, and applying one text’s model","html":"<ul><li>“Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors view X?”: name each author’s stance in a word or two, then find the choice that contrasts exactly those two stances.</li><li>“How would Text 1’s model apply to Text 2’s case?”: take the rule from Text 1 and run the new case through it, step by step.</li></ul>"}],"qids":["dc043599","88bb0f6f","1917ba9a"],"title":"Differences, and applying one text’s model"}]}]}]}]};
